.TP
.BI "rollback " version " [\-\-apply] [\-\-yes] [\-\-hosts]"
Prepare tacctl's state for the release before this one; run on the newer
release, before the older one is installed. A dry run by default: it lists
every step and every warning and changes nothing. The versions it takes are
.B 0.2.22
(the release before this one),
.BR 0.2.21 ,
.BR 0.2.20 ,
.BR 0.2.19 ,
.BR 0.2.18 ,
.BR 0.2.17 ,
.BR 0.2.16 ,
.B 0.2.9
and
.BR 0.2.3 ;
.BR 0.2.4 " to " 0.2.8
and
.B 0.2.2
and older are refused, with the way there (one release at a time: this
rollback,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.9" ,
then the rollback of that release), and so is any other word (0.2.10 to
0.2.15 included: the refusal names the targets). Every target but 0.2.22, 0.2.21, 0.2.20
and 0.2.19 is refused while an emergency lockdown locks a group (the refusal lists each
one and
.B "emergency lockdown <scope> disable"
lifts it; a release before 0.2.19 would render the locked users back into the
scope; 0.2.19 has the record and keeps it), and every target is refused while a device's record says a confirmed commit of
.B "device config apply"
is
.B pending
or
.B unknown
(the refusal names each device and the time, and
.B "device config apply <name> \-\-check"
or
.B "device config forget <name>"
settles it), and every target but 0.2.22, 0.2.21 and 0.2.20 while the store has a user whose
name has a period (no other target reads such a name: the refusal lists each, with
.B "user rename <name> <new\-name>"
as the way out, and changes nothing; an override under such a name in the
ignored
.B users
mapping of console.yaml does not stop it, the rollback takes that entry out).
The
.B tiers
and
.B users
mappings of console.yaml that this release ignores stay in the file, and the
release a rollback goes to reads them as it always did: a user or a tier
switched off there gets a plain shell again, and the password cache is on again
for every user of a tier that is allowed it, so the plan lists each user who
would gain a system shell or the cache, with the reason, as a warning that
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.BR \-\-yes .
Every target also takes out of
.I console.yaml
the settings of 0.2.20 that no older release reads:
.BR settings.color ,
.B settings.system_shell_users
(the system shell of single users; the tiers' list
.B settings.system_shell_tiers
stays) and
.B settings.password_cache_users
(the users who turned the password cache on; the cache's tiers and lifetimes in
.B settings.password_cache
stay, for the releases that have them) are removed and a
.B settings.list_max
of \-1 (never ask) is written as 1000, with every other setting kept and a
snapshot first.
.IP
Every target but 0.2.22 and 0.2.21, which have it themselves, keeps every user's
login to this server: an older release answers
it only for the users that have the server's scope
.RB ( tacctl )
in their own scopes, so the plan adds it to the scopes of each user who logs in
to the server and lacks it (it counts and names them; a warning, so
.B \-\-apply
needs
.BR \-\-yes ),
after a snapshot. Every target but 0.2.22 removes
.B server.name
(the name of
.BR "server name" ),
and every target but 0.2.22 and 0.2.21
.B server.addresses
as well, from
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
which no older release knows; the host registry keeps the address in this
server's entry. Every target but 0.2.22 also removes the timing scale:
.B device.timeout_scale
and
.B timeout_scale_scope.\fIscope\fR
from
.I tacctl.yaml
and the
.B timeout_scale
of each device from
.IR devices.yaml ,
because no older release knows the keys (an older parser refuses a device with a
key it does not know). It is a note in the plan, not a warning: the older
release connects to every device with its fixed timings again, which a slow or
distant device may not meet, and the values stay in the snapshot.
.IP
Every target removes the users' email addresses
.RB ( "user email" ,
.BR "user add \-\-email" )
from
.IR store.yaml :
no older release knows the key, and the ones that return early, 0.2.22 among
them, convert this file too. The plan names the users who have one (never the
address) in a warning, so
.B \-\-apply
needs
.BR \-\-yes ,
and takes the snapshot first. The older release prunes
.I backups/
by its own retention and knows no marker, so the apply also keeps the snapshot
.RB ( "backup keep" ):
a copy in
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups\-kept/ ,
where no retention reaches it, with the note
.B "rollback to <target>: dropped the email addresses of N users"
(a count, no name, no address). The apply names the snapshot, the kept copy and
.BR "tacctl backup recover <snapshot> \-\-only email" ,
which puts the addresses back once this release is installed again
.RB ( "backup unkeep"
removes the copy after that); the audit line of the rollback names the kept
snapshot and the users. A store that cannot be read
refuses every target. Until the older release is installed, the two verbs that
set an address write the key again, and the command says so.
.IP
Every target writes the SNMP credential files
.RI ( snmp.yaml ,
.IR snmp/ scope .yaml ,
.IR snmp/devices/ name .yaml )
in version 1 again: version 2, which holds a pool of v3 users and a default
(\fBv3\-user add\fR), is refused by every older release ("unsupported
version"). Version 1 holds one v3 user for each level, so a level keeps its
default user and drops the rest, and a device whose default names a user of its
scope's or the config's pool loses the choice, and a device with no
.B snmp:
entry in
.I devices.yaml
loses its credentials file whole (the older releases ignore the credentials of
such a device, so a file in version 1 would hand it to another level's
credentials unseen). A level with several users and
none that is its own default has no answer, and the rollback is refused naming the
level. The plan names the levels and counts the users (never a name or a
passphrase) in a warning, so
.B \-\-apply
needs
.B \-\-yes
when anything is dropped; one that drops nothing only rewrites the files. The snapshot
is kept with the note
.BR "rollback to <target>: dropped N SNMPv3 users in M levels" ,
the audit line adds
.BR "snmp\-users=N snmp\-levels=M snmp\-choices=K snmp\-devices=P" ,
and the apply names
.BR "tacctl backup recover <snapshot> \-\-only snmp\-users" .
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.22"
converts nothing but the email addresses and the SNMP credential files: 0.2.22
reads every other file this release writes, so the server name, the timing
scale, the server address, the users' login to this server, a user name with a
period and an emergency lockdown stay. With no address in the store and no SNMP
credential file in version 2 (a second or non\-default user, a default naming
another level's user, a device's file without an
.B snmp:
entry) it takes no snapshot (there is no file to convert) and needs no
.BR \-\-yes ;
with an address, or a version 2 file, it takes the snapshot and needs
.B \-\-yes
when anything is dropped.
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.22" .
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.21"
removes
.B server.name
and the timing scale and converts nothing else: no scope is added to the users, and a user name with
a period and an emergency lockdown stay, as 0.2.21 reads both. It needs no
.B \-\-yes
(it has no warning of its own: the email addresses, above, are the one), and
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.21" ,
and names the verbs that write a key 0.2.21 refuses until then
.RB ( "server name" ,
.BR "device timeout\-scale" ,
.BR "scope timeout\-scale" ,
.B "config devices timeout\-scale"
and the two that set an email address).
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.20"
removes
.B server.addresses
and
.B server.name
and the timing scale, and adds the scope to the users (above); 0.2.20 reads every other file this
release writes, so nothing else is converted, and
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.20" .
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.19"
.B \-\-apply
rewrites the per\-tier sudoers drop\-in, when it is exactly the text this
release writes, with the one 0.2.19 writes (after
.BR "visudo \-cf" ):
the engineer's rule of
.B "scope mgmt\-acl * mode"
and the operator's rules of
.B "console system\-shell"
and
.B "console password\-cache"
are taken out. An edited drop\-in is left (0.2.19's upgrade rewrites it). The
emergency lockdown record, a lockdown that stands included, is 0.2.19's own and
stays. Besides what every target removes (below), nothing is converted:
.IR devices.yaml ,
the group drop\-in, the blocks, the apply state, the host registry and the
hosts are in formats 0.2.19 reads.
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.19" ,
and names the verbs that write a key 0.2.19 refuses until then
.RB ( "scope mgmt\-acl" " ... " "mode strict" ,
.B "user add"
and
.BR "user passwd" ,
which require a password change by default,
.BR "user require\-password\-change" ,
.BR "console color" ,
.BR "console list\-max" ,
.B "console system\-shell user"
and
.BR "console password\-cache on" ).
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.18"
.B \-\-apply
rewrites the per\-tier sudoers drop\-in, when it is exactly the text this
release writes (or the one 0.2.19 wrote), with the one 0.2.18 writes (after
.BR "visudo \-cf" ):
the engineer's rules of
.BR "device config apply" ,
.BR "device enroll" ,
.BR "device collect" ,
.BR "device onboard" ,
.B "device sync"
and
.B "device support\-bundle"
are taken out. An edited drop\-in is left (0.2.18's upgrade rewrites it). A
partial lockdown record (keys under
.BR emergency.lockdown. ,
which locks nothing) is removed from
.I tacctl.yaml
after a snapshot, because 0.2.18's schema refuses it. Nothing else is
converted:
.IR devices.yaml ,
.IR console.yaml ,
.IR store.yaml ,
the group drop\-in and the hosts are in formats 0.2.18 reads.
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.18" .
.IP
Every target but 0.2.19 and 0.2.3 lists in a warning (and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.BR \-\-yes )
the system blocks that hold statements 0.2.19 added to their grammar: the
syslog, login\-policy, ssh and summer\-time statements, the login announcement,
the
.B absent
lines and the IOS
.B "banner motd"
and
.BR "banner exec" .
An older release lints such a block as an error and plans nothing for the
devices it reaches. The block files are not rewritten; take the statements
out before the upgrade.
.IP
Every target removes the management filter mode of the scopes
.RB ( scope_mgmt_acl.mode.\fIscope\fR ,
only
.B strict
is stored; 0.2.20) from
.I tacctl.yaml
after a snapshot, because the older schema refuses the key, and lists the
scopes that were strict in a warning (and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.BR \-\-yes ):
the older release renders and plans the
.B ssh\-only
filter for them, which ends in a
.B default\-accept
term, so its
.B "device config plan"
or
.B "device config apply"
would put that filter back on a device that holds the strict one, without
saying so. The rollback is not refused, and no device is contacted.
.IP
Every target removes the required password change
.RB ( password_change_required
of a user in
.IR store.yaml ,
written only for a flagged user; 0.2.20) after a snapshot, because the older
schema refuses the key. The dry run lists the flagged users, and
warns, with
.B \-\-apply
refusing without
.BR \-\-yes ,
of those who are not superusers (the flag never binds a superuser): the older
release renders them into every scope of theirs again, so they can log in to
the devices with the password an administrator set or knew, the one they were
to change. The rollback renders nothing, so the running TACACS+ and RADIUS
files keep them off the devices until the older release renders.
.IP
Every target keeps a break\-glass entry of the Junos root account
.RB ( root:admin
in
.BR breakglass_scope.\fIscope\fB.users ;
0.2.20), which the older schema accepts, and names the scopes that record it
in a warning (and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.BR \-\-yes ):
the older release takes
.B root
for a local user like any other, so its Junos walkthrough renders a commented
.B "set system login user root"
line, which Junos refuses, in place of
.B "system root\-authentication"
and
.BR "system services ssh root\-login allow" ,
its Cisco and WTI walkthroughs render a root account, and (but for 0.2.3) its
.B "device config pull"
and
.B diff
expect the login line on each Junos device of the scope, whose
.B breakglass
section reads
.BR differs .
.B "tacctl scope breakglass <scope> remove root"
takes the entry out.
.IP
Every target but 0.2.3 takes the statements of root's access
.RB ( "system root\-authentication" ,
the leaf's name only, and
.BR "system services ssh root\-login" )
out of the
.B breakglass
section of the stored pulls
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/ name .yaml
and the plain view beside it; every other line stays). This release's pull
keeps them for every Junos device, and an older release takes them for extra
statements of the section, which would read
.B differs
until its next pull. The files are derived and not in the snapshot; this
release, if it stays, reads the two as missing until its next pull.
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.17"
.B \-\-apply
rewrites the per\-tier sudoers drop\-in, when it is exactly the text this
release writes (or the one 0.2.19 or 0.2.18 wrote), with the one 0.2.17 writes (after
.BR "visudo \-cf" ):
the rules of
.BR "device ios\-syntax" ,
.B "device notice list"
and
.B "device client\-config"
become 0.2.17's
.BR "device config\-syntax" ,
.B "device notices"
and
.BR "device ssh\-config" . An edited drop\-in is left (0.2.17's upgrade rewrites
it). When a device of
.I devices.yaml
has a configuration syntax setting
.RB ( ios_syntax ),
the command takes a snapshot first and writes the setting under
.BR config_syntax ,
the key 0.2.5 to 0.2.17 read (they refuse a key they do not know); the value
and every other key stay. A partial lockdown record is removed from
.IR tacctl.yaml .
Nothing else is converted:
.IR console.yaml ,
.IR store.yaml ,
the group drop\-in and the hosts are in formats 0.2.17 reads.
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.17" .
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.16"
.B \-\-apply
takes a snapshot, then removes from
.I tacctl.yaml
the keys a 0.2.16 binary refuses,
.B audit.strict
and a partial lockdown record
(its schema reports it as an unknown key, and its
.B "backup restore"
refuses a snapshot that has it; the key is checked against that release). When
the key is
.B true
the dry run warns that 0.2.16 runs a command whose audit line cannot be
written, and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.BR \-\-yes ;
a
.B false
value is removed without a warning. The configuration syntax setting of
.I devices.yaml
is written under
.BR config_syntax ,
as for 0.2.17. Nothing else is converted:
.IR console.yaml ,
.IR store.yaml ,
the apply state directory, the sudoers drop-ins and the hosts are in formats
0.2.16 reads, and the snapshot manifests gained keys that nothing reads.
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.16" ,
and the audit line
.BR "rollback target=0.2.16" .
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.9"
