.I ~/.zfunc/_tacctl
(with that directory on
.B fpath
before
.BR compinit )
or to
.IR ~/.config/fish/completions/tacctl.fish .
The bash script ends with a key binding: on a line that starts with
.BR tacctl ,
the question mark prints the help of the words before the cursor
.RB ( "tacctl user add ?"
prints what
.B "tacctl user add \-\-help"
prints, in colour on a terminal) and leaves the command line as it is; the word
being typed is not part of the question. The key runs one thing, the hidden
.BR "tacctl __help \-\- " words ,
which prints the help and runs nothing else: the words are plain words to it,
so a flag that waits for its value, a
.B \-\-
earlier on the line or a hidden command is never run as a command or given
.BR \-\-help .
The key types a literal question mark instead when the line does not start with
.BR tacctl ,
has a second line before the cursor, or has before the cursor anything but
ASCII letters, digits, spaces, tabs and
.BR "_ @ % + = : , . / \-"
(so a quote, a backslash,
.BR "$ * ~ ! # [ {" ,
a shell operator, a carriage return or a non-ASCII character anywhere before the
cursor;
.B "ls *.?"
works). On a line that does start with
.B tacctl
and has only such words, the key always asks for help, even inside a word
.RB ( "tacctl device description sw1 why?"
prints help); quote the character first (Ctrl-V, then
.BR ? )
to type it there.
.B TACCTL_HELP_KEY
read when the script is sourced changes the key: unset or
.B ?
is the question mark, a readline key spelling such as
.B \\eh
is that key instead, and
.B off
