A new user must change the password at the first login to this server
(see
.BR "user require\-password\-change" ):
until then no device accepts the user.
.B \-\-no\-change\-required
skips the requirement. It is not set, and the command says why, for a
superuser (never held to it) and for a user with no login on this server (the
server is not enrolled, its name is no Linux account name, its group has no
privilege level, or, for a server still registered in a scope it shares with
devices, the user is not in that scope), who could never change the password.
.PP
When this server is enrolled
.RB ( "host enroll \-\-local" ),
the verbs that change an account on it end by syncing this server's own
accounts at once:
.BR "user add" ,
.BR "user remove" ,
.BR "user rename" ,
.BR "user move" ,
.BR "user enable" ,
.BR "user disable" ,
.B "user scope"
.RB ( add ,
.BR remove ,
.B replace
and
.BR "remove \-\-all" ),
.B "user require\-password\-change"
(once, whatever the number of users) and
.B "user passwd"
when it enables a disabled user. It is the accounts\-only sync of
.B "host sync"
of this server, run after the change is stored and the service restarted,
without a snapshot of its own, so that a new user has an account and a UID at
once and can log in to change the password. One line says what was done
.RB ( "This server's accounts were synced: 1 created (UID 80003)." ):
the counts of accounts created, updated, renamed and removed, and no other
user. The sync's own lines are not shown, but its warnings are. A sync that
fails is a warning with the retry command
.RB ( "tacctl host sync <name>" ),
followed by what the script said as plain lines: the change is stored and the
command exits 0. A server that is not enrolled gets no sync and no word about
one. The sync writes its own
.B "host sync"
