ssh runs as the user who invoked
.BR sudo (8),
using their keys, and forwards neither their agent nor any port to the host;
the remote login must be root or able to sudo. A host whose address is one of
this machine's own (loopback and
.B 0.0.0.0
included) is refused unless it is enrolled with
.BR \-\-local .
That login (the provisioning account:
.I user
of the target, else the invoking user's name) must be a local account on the
host that does not authenticate through tacctl;
.B "host enroll"
refuses a user of tacctl, and
.B "host sync"
warns about an existing enrolment that uses one.
.B "tacctl ssh"
never uses it.
.IP
The registry name is
.B \-\-name
or the host's short name, lowercased (host names are not case\-sensitive; a host
already enrolled under exactly that spelling keeps it). A name no enrolled host
has with exactly that spelling is a new one: it is lower\-case letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
or
.B \-
starting with a letter (253 at most, each dotted part 63 at most), is not a tacctl word (the words a device
cannot be called either, such as
.BR all ,
.B scope
or
.BR local ),
and is not the name of an enrolled host or a registered device in any case:
names are not case\-sensitive, so
.B web1
and
.B Web1
are one name. A
.B \-\-name
with upper case is refused with the lower\-case spelling to use. A host enrolled
before with upper case keeps working, is listed by
.BR "tacctl config validate" ,
and is renamed with
.BR "host rename Web1 web1" .
Enrolling a registered host again under its registered
spelling is allowed (that is how it is enrolled again or moved). A refused
name changes nothing and nothing is attempted over ssh. The device registry is
read again once the client script has run, when the registry line is written:
a device registered meanwhile makes the command take the line back, print
.B "A device named 'web1' (192.0.2.60) was registered while the client script ran"
with the command to enroll again under another
.BR \-\-name ,
and exit 1. The script has already run on the host, which authenticates
through this server as long as its scope covers its address; no record, pin
or audit line is written. What the command did before the script stays and is
warned of: a scope opened to both protocols for the host
.RB ( "Scope 'linux\-web1' is still open to tacacs, radius" )
and a
.B \-\-staging
bench address, which
.B "scope staging remove"
takes out. The same holds when another enrolment of the name finishes first. A
break\-glass hold recorded under a new name is refused before anything is done,
as for
.BR "host rename" :
only
.B "breakglass release"
removes a hold. An option that takes
a value
.RB ( \-\-method ,
.BR \-\-scope ,
.BR \-\-server ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-identity )
given without one is a usage error that names the option.
.IP
After a successful run the host's public key files are read over the same
ssh connection
.RB ( "cat /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub" ),
and the target is scanned
.RB ( ssh\-keyscan
of the host and port, as root; asked one key type at a time when the scan of
all the types gives no key from a host that accepted the connection). Only the keys both reads hold are pinned in
the device registry for
.BR "tacctl ssh" ;
the output lists their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints. A key type the two reads give different keys for pins nothing:
both sets are printed and
.B "host hostkey\-mismatch"
is logged to syslog (auth.warning). A type only one read has is reported, not
pinned. Unreadable key files, or a host that does not answer the scan (the warning
names the reads that were made), pin
nothing, with a warning and the
.B "device hostkey"
command; the enrolment succeeds either way.
.B "host sync"
of a pinned host compares the key files with the pin and reports a
difference; it never re-pins. A host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
is not pinned.
.IP
For
.BR tacplus ,
pam_tacplus is built on this server, in a rootless
.BR podman (1)
container matching the host's OS release, cached, and shipped as a binary.
Debian, Ubuntu and RHEL-family hosts (RHEL, AlmaLinux, Rocky, CentOS
Stream, Oracle Linux; built against AlmaLinux of the same major release) are
supported. The host compiles the module from source instead when no image
matches its OS, its architecture differs, the container build fails, or
.B \-\-build\-on\-host
is given. A host that already has the module from the same source is not
touched. Requires
.B "config linux build"
to have been run. For
.B radius
none of this applies
.RB ( \-\-build\-on\-host
warns that it is not applicable).
.IP
A reject from the server is final. When the server does not answer (or the
host's shared secret is wrong), login falls through to the local password,
which tacctl's accounts do not have; local administrators and every other
local account outside
.B tac\-users
never reach the server. With the server silent a user of tacctl is refused
after about 5\(en6 s (tacplus) or 8\(en9 s (radius).
