.SS Backup management \fB(tacctl backup)\fR
.TP
.B backup list
List snapshots, newest first, then old-style backups. A snapshot that
.B "backup keep"
copied is marked
.B "snapshot, kept"
and a note column shows its note; one whose original has been pruned from
.I backups/
is
.BR "kept only" .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backup list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backup list
.TP
.BI "backup diff " [ timestamp ]
Diff
.I store.yaml
and
.I tacctl.yaml
against a snapshot (default: most recent), then, each only where either side has
one,
.IR devices.yaml ,
.IR console.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
each record of
.IR hosts/ ,
.I staging
and
.IR linux\-uids .
A snapshot taken by 0.2.13 or older does not know the hosts and a restore of it
leaves the live host files alone, so they are not listed as only in the current
state: one line,
.BR "host files: not in this snapshot (it predates 0.2.14)" ,
stands in their place when the server has host files.
The output shows shared secrets and password hashes.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backup diff
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backup diff
.TP
.BI "backup restore " timestamp " " [ \-\-legacy " | " \-\-keep\-uids ]
Restore both files from a snapshot after confirmation, re-render every
enabled backend's config (all or none), and restart the backends. The current
state is snapshotted first, so a restore can itself be undone. Nothing changes
if the snapshot is invalid or the render fails. A snapshot's
.B backends.enabled
comes back with the rest of
.IR tacctl.yaml :
a backend it enables must already be installed (otherwise nothing changes and
the message says to run
.BR "backend enable" ),
is enabled at boot and started; one it leaves out is stopped and disabled. With
.B \-\-legacy
the argument names an old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
file, which goes through
.B store import \-\-check
before it is imported. When this server is enrolled a red line says that
users whose tier fell keep their old groups until
.BR "host sync" .
.PP
A snapshot that knows the hosts (it holds
.IR linux\-hosts ,
which every snapshot of this release does) also puts the host files back:
.I linux\-hosts
whole,
.I hosts/
made to match (a record the snapshot lacks is removed),
.I staging
back or removed, and
.I linux\-uids
as it was. A snapshot without
.I linux\-hosts
(tacctl 0.2.13 and older) leaves all four as they are. No host is touched and
nothing is reconciled. Before the confirmation, and again after the restore, a
note names the hosts that come back, those that go and those registered
differently, and what to do about a host that no longer matches
.RB ( "host sync" ,
.BR "host show \-\-check" ,
.B "host unenroll"
again for one that comes back after
.BR "host unenroll" ;
.B "host enroll"
again, or
.B "host unenroll \-\-force"
after that, for one that goes). The restored ledger can forget a number a host
already holds, so the note also names every user whose UID it drops or
changes; a host file that cannot be read is named in a red line,
.BR "could not compare the host files" ,
and the restore still puts the files back. The snapshot of the current state taken first holds the live host files.
A break\-glass hold on a name that the restore enrolls or forgets is worded as a
host's or a device's in the backend files, so when the enrolled names change
the files are rendered again once the registry is back (no more than the one
restart the restore makes); if that render fails the restore stands, a warning
says so, and
.B "config render"
does it.
.PP
With
.B \-\-keep\-uids
the ledger
.RI ( linux\-uids )
is left as it is, and the registry, the records and
.I staging
still come back. Use it when the snapshot is wanted for its users, groups,
scopes or devices and the hosts have synced since: the live ledger is the one
the hosts agree with, and the restore does not make them disagree. In place of
the comparison the note, before the confirmation and again after the restore,
is one line saying that the ledger is kept and the restored users keep the
numbers they have now. A user the snapshot brings back who has no line in the
live ledger is numbered at the next
.B "host sync"
as any new user is, and the line of a user the snapshot lacks stays, as after
.BR "user remove" .
The snapshot of the current state taken first is made as always. A snapshot
that does not know the hosts never touches the ledger: the flag is accepted
and a line says the ledger stays either way.
.B \-\-keep\-uids
with
.B \-\-legacy
or without a store is a usage error, since an old-style restore never touches
.IR linux\-uids .
.PP
The restore holds the host registry's lock from before it changes anything: when
another tacctl command keeps it for 30 seconds the restore stops with the
registry busy and nothing changed. It puts the UID ledger back under the
ledger's lock
.RI ( /etc/tacctl/.linux\-uids.lock ),
which
.B \-\-keep\-uids
does not take, and every snapshot copies the ledger under that lock, so a ledger
another command keeps for 30 seconds stops the snapshot of the current state
and the restore with it, before anything changes. The note after the restore
compares the ledger as that snapshot holds it with the one put back. It also tries the lock of each host, the one
.BR "host provisioner rotate" ,
.B host rename
and
.B host forget
hold while they run, for every name in the live registry and in the snapshot's,
without waiting: when another command holds one the restore stops before it
takes the snapshot of the current state, with nothing changed, and names the host
.RB ( "'<name>' is locked by another tacctl command" ).
The locks are released when the host files are back. A snapshot that does not
know the hosts takes neither lock. The host files are put back last. If that
step fails the rest of the restore stands and the backends are restarted, and
two warnings name the error, the snapshot of the state from before the restore
and the commands that undo the restore or try the host files again.
.PP
A snapshot is the directory
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/<timestamp>/
holding
.IR store.yaml ,
.I tacctl.yaml
and a manifest (the
.I rendered.json
of that moment, the tacctl version, and the session id, user and command
that took it), root-only, and the files of the
registries and hosts when they exist
.RI ( devices.yaml ,
.IR console.yaml ,
.IR snmp* ,
.IR blocks/ ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR hosts/ ,
.I staging
and
.IR linux\-uids ;
.I linux\-hosts
is always there, empty when there is no registry). One is taken
automatically before every change, unless the files already equal the
newest snapshot. For the host records the comparison ignores what
.B host enroll
and
.B host sync
write on every run (the last run and the facts it read) and looks at the
provisioner entry only, so a sync alone does not make the next command take
a snapshot; the snapshot that
.B backup restore
takes first compares everything. The newest 30 are retained. Without a store,
list, diff and restore work on old-style backups only.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backup restore
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-legacy\fR
The timestamp names an old\-style backup (backups/legacy).
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-uids\fR
Leave the UID ledger (linux\-uids) as it is; the registry, records and staging come back.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backup restore
.TP
.BI "backup keep " timestamp " " [ \-\-note " " text ]
Copy a snapshot to
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups\-kept/<timestamp>/ ,
where no retention reaches it: this release prunes
.I backups/
beyond the newest 30, and the older releases a rollback goes to do the same
without knowing any marker. The copy is a snapshot like any other (the same
files, the manifest included, mode 0600 in a directory of 0700) with a file
.I kept
that records the note, who last set it (the user and the session id of the audit
lines) and when: the keeper until a new
.B \-\-note
replaces the note, and then whoever replaced it. The note is free text of at most 200
printable characters (no control or format character, no line break); it is stored in
the kept copy and shown by
.BR "backup list" ,
and it is not a place for secrets. The audit line
.B "backup keep id= note=yes|no already=yes|no"
says only whether a note was given, and the note is written as
.B <redacted>
in the command line it logs. Keeping a snapshot that is kept already says so and
changes nothing, but for
.BR \-\-note ,
which replaces the note. A kept snapshot is looked up after
.IR backups/ :
.BR "backup diff" ,
.B "backup restore"
and
.B "backup recover"
find an id in
.I backups/
first and then among the kept copies, so they work on a copy whose original is
gone. A restore does not touch
.IR backups\-kept/ .
When the backups are preserved,
.B uninstall
archives
.I backups\-kept/
with
.IR backups/ .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backup keep
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fI<text>\fR
Record why the snapshot is kept: free text, stored in the kept copy, shown by backup list and never written to the audit log. It is not a place for secrets. A new note replaces the old one.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backup keep
.TP
.BI "backup unkeep " timestamp " " [ \-\-yes ]
Remove the kept copy of a snapshot after confirmation
.RB ( \-\-yes
does not ask). When no snapshot of that id remains in
.IR backups/ ,
the command says that the kept copy is the only copy before it asks. The
snapshot in
.I backups/
is not touched. The audit line is
.BR "backup unkeep id= only-copy=yes|no" .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backup unkeep
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Do not ask for confirmation.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backup unkeep
.TP
.BI "backup recover " snapshot " " [ \-\-only " " kind[,kind...] ] " " [ \-\-dry\-run ] " " [ \-\-yes ]
Put back from a snapshot only the kinds of data named, and leave everything else
as it is
.RB ( "backup restore"
puts back the whole state). It is the way back for what
.B rollback
removed: the rollback keeps the snapshot it took
.RB ( "backup keep" )
and names it. It reads the snapshot's
.I store.yaml
and, for each kind, the live state, and lists per user what it would restore and
what it skips, with the reason (the names of users, never an address). The kinds
are
.B email
(the email addresses of users,
.BR "user email" )
and
.BR snmp\-users ,
which is reserved: it is accepted by name and refused with
.BR "not available yet" ,
and nothing is changed. Without
.B \-\-only
every kind that can be recovered is recovered. For
.BR email ,
a user the snapshot gives an address and who exists now with none gets it
(through the same validation as
.BR "user email" ,
so an address belongs to one user). A user who no longer exists, one who has
another address now (the current one is kept) and an address another user holds
now are skipped and named; a user who has the address already is nothing to
do. Nothing is rendered or restarted. A snapshot of an older release that holds
no address, or an old\-style backup, recovers nothing. The snapshot is looked up
as every command that takes an id does: in
.IR backups/ ,
then among the copies
.B "backup keep"
made in
.IR backups\-kept/ ,
which no retention reaches (a rollback keeps the snapshot it names there, so the
data it dropped can be recovered after an older release pruned
.IR backups/ ).
.B \-\-dry\-run
lists and changes nothing. Otherwise the command asks for confirmation
.RB ( \-\-yes
does not), takes a snapshot of the current state first (the snapshot being read
is kept by the retention), writes the change, and ends with the number
recovered and skipped. A kept copy that was read is named, with the command
that removes it once the data is recovered
.RB ( "backup unkeep" ).
The audit line is
.B "backup recover id= pre= kinds= restored= skipped="
and holds no address.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backup recover
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR \fI<kind>[,<kind>...]\fR
Recover only the listed kinds of data: email, snmp\-users. Without it every kind that can be recovered is.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
List what would be recovered and change nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Do not ask for confirmation.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backup recover
