.SS Interactive shell \fB(tacctl shell)\fR
.TP
.BI "shell " "[ \-\-no\-history ] [ \-\-idle " min " ] [ \-\-space\-completion { on | off } ] [ \-\-password\-cache ] [ \-c " line " ]"
A prompt,
.BR "tacctl> " ,
where each line is a tacctl command without the
.B tacctl
in front:
.BR "user list" ,
.BR "scope show lab" .
The shell runs as the invoking user and holds no privilege: each line runs as
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.I tacctl words
with the terminal attached, so sudo's policy and log line and tacctl's tier
gate apply exactly as from a login shell.
.B \-n
is used for members of
.B tac-users
in the readonly or operator tier, or in no tier group (they have no local
password and their rules are NOPASSWD); a line that sudo refuses them and
that their tier's sudoers rules do not cover is reported as not permitted for
the tier. A member of
.B tac-superuser
runs plain
.BR sudo ,
which asks for the network password on the terminal when a line needs it
(once; sudo's cache then applies).
.B hash
and
.B completion
lines run without sudo.
For
.B host
lines the agent socket
.RB ( SSH_AUTH_SOCK )
is passed as it is when tacctl re-executes itself under sudo.
Messages and exit statuses are those of the command; after a command that
ends with a non-zero status the shell prints
.BR "[exit " \fIN\fB ] .
.IP
A line is split into words at blanks (space, tab); single quotes, double
quotes and backslash quote as in
.BR sh (1),
and nothing else is special: there are no variables, globbing, pipes,
redirections, command separators, command substitution or history expansion
(\c
.BR ; ,
.BR | ,
.BR $( ,
backticks,
.BR > ,
.B !
are ordinary characters of a word). A line is at most 4096 bytes.
A line pasted from the help or the documentation may start with
.BR tacctl ,
.B sudo tacctl
or
.BR /usr/local/bin/tacctl :
that first word (and the
.B sudo
before it) is dropped, once, and the rest runs, completes and is explained as
it would without it; only the first word counts, never one inside the line or
an argument.
A line that is only
.B tacctl
prints what
.B tacctl
with no arguments prints, the top-level usage, and ends with status 1. The
history keeps the line as it was typed.
The shell's own words are
.B help
(the top-level usage),
.BI "help " command
(the usage of that command's family),
.B history
(the lines entered, as stored),
.B console forget
(empties the password cache, see
.BR \-\-password\-cache ),
.B exit
and
.BR quit .
.IP
Editing keys: Left/Right, Home/End (Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E), Esc-b and Esc-f (or
Alt-Left, Alt-Right) by word, Backspace, Delete, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-W,
Ctrl-T, Ctrl-L; Up/Down (Ctrl-P, Ctrl-N) step through the history; Ctrl-R
searches it backwards as you type (Ctrl-R again: further back; Enter runs the
match; Ctrl-G gives the line back). Tab completes commands, sub-commands, flags
and names (users, groups, scopes, hosts, devices, ..., asked of
.B sudo \-n tacctl _completion-names
and kept for 5 seconds); a second Tab lists the matching words alone. ?
inserts nothing and shows help for the cursor's position (\fB\-h\fR or \fB\-\-help\fR after the words of a command prints the same help): the words that can
come (\(lqPossible completions:\(rq in colour on a terminal: command names, sub-commands, fixed words and options teal, live names bold, with each one's argument column, at most 40
characters and cut with \(lq...\(rq when longer, as a name with no column is, and its description, which goes
on under its column when it does not fit, the blanks that align a name's vendor, address and scope kept; a fixed word such as priv\-lvl after
.B group edit
<name> with the description of the usage line that has it; a command
or sub-command has the same description as in the generated completion
scripts), or after a
command that takes arguments the usage lines of the forms the words typed so
far fit, its options and what comes next (\(lqNext: <username> <group>\(rq;
where the forms part, the choice:
\(lqNext: <name> priv\-lvl|juniper\-class|wti\-level|tier ...\(rq;
\(lqNext: <Enter> to run\(rq). Inside
quotes, or after a backslash, ? is typed as a character. A list of more than
40 entries asks first (\(lqShow all 45 devices? [y/N]\(rq): y shows it, any
other key (or Ctrl-C) prints how to narrow it and gives the line back. Help taller than the terminal is
paged (\(lq\-\- more (Space: page, Enter: line, q: quit) \-\-\(rq; q or Ctrl-C
stops). A paste is inserted as one line (its newlines become blanks, at most
4096 bytes) and runs only on Enter. Ctrl-C at the prompt discards the line;
Ctrl-C while a command runs ends the command, and the shell carries on.
Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-\e do nothing (there is no job control). Ctrl-D on an empty
line, like
.BR exit ,
leaves the shell.
.IP
Tab, ?, a typed space and the shell's top-level
.B help
list only the commands and sub-commands that the caller's tier can run
(the rows of the tier table): a readonly user does not see
.B store
or
.BR install ,
an operator does not see
.BR host ;
a superuser and a caller outside
.B tac-users
see everything. The verbs of
.B device config
are listed by the tier of each (an operator sees
.BR list ,
an engineer all but
.B forget
and
.BR shapes ).
The list ends with \(lqShown: the commands the
.I tier
tier can run\(rq.
.BI "help " command
stays complete: it describes every verb of the family and ends with the tier
each one needs (with a note for the verbs whose sub-verbs the code splits:
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope staging" ,
.BR "host default\-method" ,
.BR "device import" ).
? after a verb typed by hand names the tier it needs
(\(lqNeeds the \fItier\fR tier.\(rq; after a command family the tier cannot run at
all, \(lqNeeds the \fItier\fR tier. help \fIfamily\fR describes it.\(rq). This is
display only: the tier gate and sudo remain the check. The shell learns a
.B tac-users
member's tier from the root side
.RB ( "sudo \-n tacctl _console-policy" ,
as the console does) when it starts, in the background and within 3 seconds, so
typing never waits for it; until the answer is in, or when it cannot be
learned, only the read-only commands are listed, and the next Tab, ? or space
asks again once 30 seconds have passed. A disabled tacctl user (the account has
no tier) is listed no command;
.BR help ,
.B exit
and
.B quit
remain. The usage printed by
.B tacctl
itself is not filtered.
.IP
The history is kept in
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
(mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines).
A line is stored redacted: everything after
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-hash
or
.BR \-\-password ,
after
.B set
in a
.B secret ... set
line, after
.B import
when a value follows it, and after the word of a
.B passwd
line is replaced by
.BR \[u2026](redacted) ;
.B "scope secret lab set x"
is stored as
.BR "scope secret lab set \[u2026](redacted)" .
A recalled line that carries the mark is refused; type the value again.
Lines ended with Ctrl-C are not stored.
.B \-\-no\-history
keeps the history of the session in memory only.
.IP
.BR "\-\-space\-completion on" | off
(default on) makes a typed space work as on a Junos device. A space is
refused (nothing is inserted) at the start of the line, right after a space and
right before one. At the end of a word that is a command, a sub-command, a
fixed choice or a flag name, it completes the word when exactly one such word
starts with what is typed and adds the blank
.RB ( de
and a space give
.BR "device " );
when several do, they are listed once as ? lists them, without the question or
paging, and nothing is inserted (more than 40 words, or the console's
.BR list_max ,
print nothing). A word that is complete already
.RB ( user
while
.B user\-x
exists), a name (user, group, scope, host, device, file), free text, a typo and
a space in the middle of a word get the space as typed. Inside quotes, after a
backslash, in a Ctrl-R search, and with Tab and ?, nothing changes. A paste is
inserted as it is.
.B \-c
and batch input are not affected. The login console reads the setting from
.BR "console space\-completion" .
.B off
turns off the refusal of repeated spaces and the completion: every typed space
is a space.
.IP
.B \-\-password\-cache
keeps your network password in the memory of this shell for the session (see
.B "console password\-cache"
for the policy and the lifetimes): the first device pull asks for it once (a
pull stores the password a device accepted;
.B ssh
and
.B "device ssh"
ask for it themselves the first time, before they connect, and keep it) and
later pulls, diffs and
.B ssh
lines use it. It needs an interactive shell, and for a member of
.B tac-users
a tier that
.B "console password\-cache tiers"
names; a caller outside
.B tac-users
asks for it themselves. When the cache cannot start (memory cannot be locked,
a debugger is attached, no private directory is available for its socket) one
line says why.
.B console forget
empties it. The password is never written to a file, a log, an argument or the
environment.
.IP
.BI "\-\-idle " min
ends the shell after
.I min
minutes at the prompt without input (0, the default: never; at most 1440);
the time a command runs does not count.
.IP
.BI "\-c " line
runs one line and exits with its status; the line is stored in the history.
With standard input not a terminal (\c
.BR "tacctl shell < file" ),
the shell runs the lines of the input in order, with no prompt and no
history, and stops at the first line that ends with a non-zero status,
exiting with that status. The commands of such a batch get no standard input
(it is the script), so a command that asks a question cannot be answered
from it: use its
.B \-\-yes
flag where it has one.
Over ssh, ask for a terminal to get the interactive shell:
.BR "ssh \-t " "host tacctl shell" ;
without
.B \-t
ssh allocates none, and the shell reads commands from standard input and
says so on standard error.
Bare
.B tacctl
on a terminal prints the usage and exits 1.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command shell
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-history\fR
Keep no history file for this session.
.TP
\fB\-\-idle\fR \fI<min>\fR
End the session after this many idle minutes at the prompt.
.TP
\fB\-\-space\-completion\fR \fIon|off\fR
A typed space completes a fixed word and never doubles (default on).
.TP
\fB\-\-password\-cache\fR
Keep your network password in this shell's memory (when your tier's policy allows).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fI<line>\fR
Run one line and exit.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command shell
.SH SCOPE MANAGEMENT
A
.I scope
is a named bundle of (client CIDR prefixes, shared secret), kept in the
store and shared by every backend: the same prefixes and secret serve
TACACS+ and RADIUS, unless the scope's
.B protocols
filter limits it. Each user's
.B scopes
list references one or more of these by name. A client is matched to the
scope with the most specific prefix that contains its address (tacquito
walks its rendered
.B secrets:
list, sorted by specificity, first match wins; FreeRADIUS takes the longest
prefix); authentication succeeds only when that scope is in the user's
list.
.PP
A fresh install has a single scope,
.BR lab ,
the least-privilege default. Add further scopes (for example
.BR prod ,
.BR edge ,
.BR dmz )
as needed and place users in them explicitly.
.PP
A change that writes what the scope's devices are rendered from
.RB ( "scope prefixes" ", " secret ", " mgmt\-acl ", " snmp ", " block ,
and the protocol, AAA order, timeout and group settings) says, in one line,
when at least one device of the scope has a stored pull that
.B "device config plan"
would now change:
.B "The configuration of devices in scope <name> would change: tacctl device config apply \-\-scope <name> \-\-check"
(a caller who is not a superuser is pointed to
.BR "device config plan \-\-scope" ).
It reads the stored pulls only: no login, no network, and nothing for a scope
with no pulled device.
.SS Top-level \fB(tacctl scope)\fR
.TP
.B scope list
One row per scope, with its prefixes joined as a comma list: names, prefix
counts, user counts and default marker. For the first-match routing view use
.BR "scope routing" .
For full per-scope detail (secret posture, per-scope knobs, user list) use
.BR "scope show " \fIname\fR .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope list
.TP
.B scope routing
Print every (scope, prefix) pair in first-match order, as rendered into
tacquito's
.B secrets[]
list (one row per scope-prefix pair, numbered by slice position). A
multi-prefix scope repeats its name on each prefix row; reading top to bottom
shows the order tacquito tries providers for a connecting client.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope routing
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope routing
.TP
.BI "scope show " name
Full detail for one scope: prefixes, users that reference it, the
shared secret's length and posture (never its value:
.BI "scope secret " name " show"
prints that), whether the scope is
the default, its protocols filter and auth-method, its vendor attributes
and tagged addresses (RADIUS).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope show
.TP
.B "scope add " \fIname\fR " --prefixes " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " [ --secret \fIvalue\fR | --secret generate ] [ --protocols \fIcsv\fR ] [ --vendor-attrs \fIcsv\fR ] [ --default ]
Create a new scope. The name is lower case: a letter, then up to 31 lower\-case
letters, digits,
.BR _ " or " \- ;
a name with upper case is refused with the lower\-case spelling to use.
Prefixes are required; each must not already
belong to another scope (overlap with a broader scope's supernet is
fine: the new scope's narrower prefix wins first-match).
.B --secret generate
produces a strong key.
.B --default
makes the new scope the default for
.BR "user add" .
.B --protocols
and
.B --vendor-attrs
set what
.B "scope protocols set"
and
.B "scope vendor-attrs enable"
would.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope add
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-prefixes\fR \fI<cidrs>\fR
(add) The scope's CIDRs, comma\-separated.
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\fR \fI<value>\fR
(add) The shared secret, or a generated one.
.TP
\fB\-\-protocols\fR \fI<csv>\fR
(add) Limit it to tacacs, radius (default: all).
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\-attrs\fR \fI<csv>\fR
(add) RADIUS vendor attributes to send (cisco, juniper, wti).
.TP
\fB\-\-default\fR
(add) Make it the default scope.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope add
.TP
.BI "scope remove " "name " [ --force ]
Remove a scope from the store (and so from every backend's rendered
config). Refuses if any user references the
scope; with
.BR --force ,
strips the name from all affected users and removes the scope,
with a warning. Its vendor attributes and tagged addresses go with it. Every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the scope's name
.RB ( aaa.order ,
.BR exec_timeout ,
.BR tacacs_group ,
.BR radius_group ,
.BR scope_auth_method ,
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.cisco|juniper
and
.BR scope_mgmt_acl.permits ),
its SNMP settings
.RB ( snmp_scope.\fIscope\fR.* )
and its SNMP credentials file are removed with it, so a new scope of that
name starts from the defaults.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope remove
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
(remove) Also take it out of the users that have it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope remove
.TP
.BI "scope rename " "old new"
Rename a scope. The new name is lower case; changing only the spelling
.RB ( "scope rename Lab lab" )
is how a name from before is fixed. Rewrites the scope in the store (protocols, vendor
attributes and tagged addresses included), every user's
.B scopes
reference, and
.B scope.default
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(if it pointed at
.IR old ),
and moves every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the old name to the new one. The enrolled hosts that are
registered in the scope and the staging addresses of it name the new scope
afterwards.
.B "host rename"
offers this rename for a host's own scope
.BI linux\- name \fR.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope rename
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope rename
.TP
.BI "scope default " [ name ]
Show (no arg) or set (with arg) the default scope. Stored under
.B scope.default
in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Used by
.BR "user add"
when
.B --scopes
is omitted, and by
.BR "config cisco" " / " "config juniper" " / " "config wti"
when
.B --scope
is omitted.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope default
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope default
.TP
.BI "scope lookup " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Resolve an IPv4 / IPv6 address or CIDR to the scope that owns it, by the same
first-match walk tacquito's provider selector uses. Prints the matching prefix
and lists the scopes that also cover the query below the winner. An address
the scope has tagged with a vendor
.RB ( "scope devices" )
is shown with its tag.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope lookup
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope lookup
.SS Per-scope prefixes \fB(tacctl scope prefixes)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "list"
Show the scope's current prefixes.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope prefixes
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
(remove) Remove every prefix of the scope, which removes the scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
(remove \-\-all) Also take the scope out of the users that have it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope prefixes
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " add " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Add one or more CIDRs. Each is canonicalized
.RB ( 10.1.5.5/24 " -> " 10.1.5.0/24 ),
checked against the one-CIDR-per-scope invariant (rejected with the
owning scope's name if already claimed elsewhere), and added as its own
.B secrets[]
entry in the rendered
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
sorted globally by prefix specificity so first-match routes each client to the
narrowest scope that contains it (and its own RADIUS client).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Remove one or more CIDRs. The last prefix of a scope cannot be removed
(use
.B "remove \-\-all"
or
.BR "scope remove" ),
and a prefix whose removal would leave a tagged address
.RB ( "scope devices" )
outside the scope is refused.
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "remove \-\-all " [ \-\-force ]
Empty the entire prefix list for the scope (with confirmation): the scope
and its per-scope settings are removed, so it vanishes from every rendered
config. The command refuses to proceed when users reference the scope
unless
.B \-\-force
is given, which strips the scope from those users.
.B \-\-all
takes no CIDRs, and
.B \-\-force
is only valid with it. A scope enrolled Linux hosts use is not removed
(move them first:
.BR "host move" ).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " move " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " " \fIother\fR
Move the CIDRs from
.I scope
to
.I other
in one change, so no address they hold is left unanswered. The same checks
as remove and add apply. Enrolled hosts whose addresses another scope then
answers are named, with the
.B "host move"
that follows them; none is moved.
.SS Staging addresses \fB(tacctl scope staging)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope staging " "[ list | remove " address " ]"
A host or network device provisioned off-site for a scope, at a bench
address the scope does not cover
.RB ( "host enroll ... \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging" ,
.BR "config cisco" | juniper | wti " \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging " "ip " [ "\-\-name " device ]),
has that address added to the scope as a /32: the scope's own secret and
users answer it there, so nothing changes on the device when it is
installed in the scope's prefixes. The /32 is removed once the host (its
enroll or sync) or the registered device
.RB ( "device set" )
is seen at another address the scope covers.
.B list
(the default) shows them with where each is seen now;
.B remove
takes one out by hand.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope staging
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may run list for a scope of their own; the other verbs need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope staging
.SS Per-scope secret \fB(tacctl scope secret)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "show"
Print the scope's raw shared-secret value plus its length and posture.
Superuser only; an engineer may read the secret of a scope of their own (each
read is logged), and may not set or generate one.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope secret
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may run show for a scope of their own; set and generate need the superuser tier.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
(set) Read the value from one line of standard input, so it is not on the command line.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope secret
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "set [" value " | \-\-stdin]"
Set the scope's key after validation (at least
.B config secret-min-length
chars, mixed character classes). Without a value the key is asked for twice at
a terminal, without echo;
.B \-\-stdin
reads it from one line of standard input (for a script; typed without echo at
a terminal). The line is taken as it is, less its line end, and a secret with a
control character is refused from every source. The checks apply to the value
read. A value on the command line works as well, but sudo's log
records the whole command line and
.BR ps (1)
shows it while the command runs; the other forms keep it off both. With
RADIUS enabled, a secret FreeRADIUS
cannot carry (a backslash together with a
.BR $ )
is refused;
.B "config render"
warns about one beyond the interoperability advice (63 printable
characters, no space). TACACS+ and RADIUS share the secret.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "generate"
Generate a strong key with
.B openssl rand -base64 24
and apply it.
.SS Per-scope protocols \fB(tacctl scope protocols)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "[list]"
Show which protocols serve the scope. Without a filter, every enabled
backend serves it.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope protocols
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope protocols
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "set " protocol\fR[,protocol...]
Limit the scope to the named protocols
.RB ( tacacs ", " radius ).
A backend whose protocol is not listed leaves the scope out of what it
renders: for TACACS+, its prefixes, its secret and its users' grant of it are
absent from tacquito.yaml; for RADIUS, its prefixes are not clients. Refused
when the list leaves out the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" .
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "clear"
Remove the filter.
.SS Per-scope vendor attributes (RADIUS) \fB(tacctl scope vendor-attrs, tacctl scope devices)\fR
Over RADIUS an Access-Accept always carries
.B Service-Type
.RB ( Administrative-User
at priv-lvl 15, else
.BR NAS-Prompt-User ).
A vendor's privilege attribute is added only where the operator opted in:
.B Cisco-AVPair \(dqshell:priv-lvl=N\(dq
(cisco),
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
(juniper),
.B WTI-Super
0\-3 (wti). An Access-Reject carries none. TACACS+ is not affected.
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope
Show the vendors enabled for the scope, or
.B "not sent"
(the state of a new scope).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope vendor-attrs
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope vendor-attrs
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope " enable|disable " vendor\fR[,vendor...]
Enable or disable a vendor's attribute for every device of the scope that
is not tagged. There is no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none :
nothing is sent until it is enabled. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( vendor_attrs );
the change re-renders the RADIUS backend and restarts it.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " [list]"
List the scope's tagged addresses.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope devices
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope devices
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " set " "ip\fR|\fIcidr vendor"
Tag an address (a bare IP is a /32 or /128) or a range of the scope
with its vendor: it gets that vendor's attribute and no other, whatever
the scope enables. The address must be one
.B "scope lookup"
answers with this scope: inside a prefix of it, and inside no more
specific prefix of another scope. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( devices ).
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " unset " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Remove a tag.
.B "scope prefixes remove"
refuses to leave a tag outside the scope's prefixes (unset it first, or
add the new prefix before removing the old one); tags go with the scope
on rename and remove.
.SS Per-scope idle-session timeout \fB(tacctl scope exec-timeout)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope exec-timeout " scope " [" minutes ]
Show or set the idle-session timeout (in minutes) that
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR
render into device configs. Cisco emits
.BR "exec-timeout \fIn\fR 0"
on
.B line con 0
and
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos emits
.BR "set system login idle-timeout \fIn\fR"
(for a registered device whose recorded release is before 16.1, which has no
such statement, the timeout is set on each login class instead).
Range 0..60. A value of
.B 0
disables idle expiry on both vendors. Default 60.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope exec-timeout
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope exec-timeout
.SS Per-scope mgmt-acl \fB(tacctl scope mgmt-acl)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " list|add|remove|clear [ " entry\fR[,\fIentry\fR...] " ]"
.TQ
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " cisco-name|juniper-name [ " label " ]"
.TQ
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " mode [ ssh-only | strict ]"
Per-scope management ACL, with the subcommands of the global
.BR "tacctl config mgmt-acl" .
The effective permit list of a
scope drives the Cisco VTY-ACL, the Junos filter and the permit lines of
the WTI IP Tables list (Step 5 of
.BR "config wti" ).
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR list ,
.BR "add " <entry>[,...] ,
.BR "remove " <entry>[,...] ,
.B clear
— manage the per-scope permit list; an entry is a CIDR, or a CIDR with its
services
.RB ( 10.0.0.0/8:tcp/22,udp/161,icmp ;
see
.BR "config mgmt-acl" ),
and
.B remove
of a CIDR alone removes every entry of that CIDR. Render fallback is per-scope
-> global
.RB ( mgmt_acl.permits )
-> empty. An empty per-scope list prunes the override so the scope
falls back to the global; \fBclear\fR is the explicit way to revert.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "cisco-name " [ label ] ,
.BR "juniper-name " [ label ]
— per-scope ACL / filter name. Fallback is per-scope -> global
.RB ( "config mgmt-acl cisco-name / juniper-name" )
-> shipped default (\fBVTY-ACL\fR for Cisco, \fBMGMT-ACL\fR for
Junos). A name starts with a letter, then
[A-Za-z0-9_-]; 1..63 chars.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "mode " [ ssh-only | strict ]
— the mode of the scope's Junos filter; it affects Junos devices only, and the
Cisco VTY-ACL and the WTI IP Tables list are the same in both. With no
argument it prints the mode and where it comes from. The default,
.BR ssh-only ,
is the filter that restricts ssh, NETCONF and SNMP and ends in a
.B default-accept
term, so everything else is accepted.
.B strict
is deny by default: the filter accepts every protocol from the permit list (an
entry with services keeps only those services, a plain entry accepts
everything from its source; the tacctl server's own address stays first), keeps
the SNMP client terms and has no
.B default-accept
term, so the implicit discard at the end of the filter drops the rest. The
walkthrough has a second server address only when the device authenticates
against another address than the one tacctl reaches it from:
.B \-\-server
(unless it is the detected address or
.BR \-\-source ),
the recorded server address
.RB ( server.addresses ,
the default of
.BR \-\-server ,
unless it is the detected address or
.BR \-\-source ),
.B \-\-source
alone (the device is still told the address detected for the server), or a
RADIUS listener bound to another address. With neither flag and nothing
recorded, with
.B \-\-server
equal to the detected address or to
.BR \-\-source ,
or with the address inside a plain entry of the permit list, there is one
address and one source /32 is rendered. The second address is not a source of
.BR permit\-mgmt :
a term of its own,
.BR permit\-auth\-replies ,
after the SNMP terms, accepts from it only the answers of the server (for
TACACS+ tcp from source port
.B tacacs
that are not the first packet of a connection, so a new connection from that
port is refused; for RADIUS udp from
.B radius
and
.BR radacct ).
The filter keeps no state and matches them by source port, so for RADIUS it
cannot tell an answer from another udp packet sent from those ports (udp port
161 is decided by the SNMP terms first, when the scope has SNMP clients).
Once a
strict filter is applied to lo0, these stop reaching the routing engine:
routing protocols carried over IP from neighbours that are not in the list
(BGP, OSPF, LDP, VRRP, BFD), TACACS+ and RADIUS replies from a server that is
not in the list (those of the server the walkthrough names are accepted), NTP,
syslog and DHCP
from hosts that are not in the list, SNMP from sources outside it (the SNMP
clients still poll), and every other protocol from them. IS-IS is not carried
over IP, so a family inet filter on lo0 does not see it: the filter neither
stops nor permits it. Add every neighbour and server the device
needs to the permit list the scope renders from first
.RB ( "scope mgmt\-acl" " scope " list
says which: the first entry of the scope's own list replaces the global list
for that scope, so add the global entries to it too), keep a console session
open when the filter is applied, and commit with
.BR "commit confirmed" .
The lo0 application stays a comment in the walkthrough. A list with no IPv4
entry that permits ssh (a plain entry, or one with tcp/22) renders no filter in
either mode, and the walkthrough says why. Setting
.B ssh-only
removes the key (only
.B strict
is stored). An engineer may read the mode of a scope of their own
.RB ( "mode" ,
with no value); the setter and every other verb of
.B "scope mgmt-acl"
are the superuser's, and another scope does not exist for an engineer. The value is
.B scope_mgmt_acl.mode.\fIscope\fR
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
a rename moves it and a removal deletes it. A word that is not a mode (the
words are lower case) is refused with
.B "Unknown mode '<word>'. Use: ssh\-only | strict."
(exit 1), a word after the value as an unknown argument with the usage (exit 1),
and in both nothing is written; setting the mode the scope already has says so
and writes nothing.
.RE
.IP
Cisco puts the effective name in
.B "ip access-list standard <NAME>"
and the matching
.B "access-class <NAME> in"
on
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos puts it in
.B "set firewall family inet filter <NAME>"
and the commented lo0 apply.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope mgmt-acl
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may read the management filter mode of a scope of their own (mode, with no value); the permit list, the ACL and filter names and setting the mode need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope mgmt-acl
.SS Per-scope NETCONF service \fB(tacctl scope netconf)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope netconf " scope " [ show | enable | disable | leave ] [ \-\-connection\-limit " n " | none ] [ \-\-rate\-limit " n " | none ]"
The NETCONF service of the Junos devices of a scope.
.B enable
renders and plans
.BR "set system services netconf ssh" ;
.B \-\-connection\-limit
and
.B \-\-rate\-limit
(1 to 250; an enable's, and
.B none
removes one) add the limits, in which case the statements are the limits' own:
a limit's statement creates
.B ssh
by itself, and the device prints no bare
.B ssh
beside it.
.B disable
plans
.BR "delete system services netconf"
and drops the scope's limits.
.B leave
(the default, and what removes the scope's setting) plans and compares nothing
of the service, as before. A device's own setting
.RB ( "device netconf" )
wins over the scope's, and each limit layers on its own. The limits bound the
service's own port (830); tacctl reaches NETCONF through the SSH subsystem,
which they do not bound on Junos 25.4.
.B show
(or no word) prints the setting. The values are
.B netconf_scope.\fIscope\fR.{state,connection_limit,rate_limit}
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; a rename moves them, a removal deletes them. The verb is
the superuser's.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope netconf
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-connection\-limit\fR \fI<n>\fR
(enable) The connection limit of the service, 1 to 250; none removes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-rate\-limit\fR \fI<n>\fR
(enable) The rate limit of the service, 1 to 250; none removes it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope netconf
.SS Timing scale \fB(tacctl scope timeout\-scale)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope timeout\-scale " scope " [ " n " | clear ]"
The timing scale of the devices of a scope: a whole number from 1 to 10 that
multiplies every network timing used to reach a device, for devices behind a long
round trip (a satellite circuit) or with a slow CPU. 1, the default, is every
timing as it has always been. The scale in force for a device is its own
.RB ( "device timeout\-scale" ),
else its scope's, else the server's
.RB ( "config devices timeout\-scale" ),
else 1; the same order as the NETCONF service setting. With no value the verb
prints the scale in force for the scope and where it comes from, and, for a scale
above 1, what it makes of each timing.
.I n
sets the scope's own scale and
.B clear
removes it, so the server's applies again. The scale multiplies:
.IP \(bu 2
the connect and handshake budget of every ssh and NETCONF session to a device
(10 s), as
.BR "device config pull" ,
.BR "device config apply" ,
.BR "device onboard" ,
.BR "device collect"
and
.B "device support\-bundle"
use it, and of
.B "tacctl ssh"
(its
.B ConnectTimeout
of 10 s);
.IP \(bu 2
the host\-key scans of
.BR "device add" ,
.BR "device hostkey" ,
.BR "device scan" ,
.BR "device check" ,
.B "device enroll"
and the others: ssh\-keyscan's
.B \-T
(5 s and 3 s) and the built\-in client's connect (3 s), handshake (10 s) and
per\-type attempt (5 s);
.IP \(bu 2
the SNMP timeout
.RB ( snmp.timeout ,
.BR "device snmp timeout" ,
.BR "scope snmp timeout" ,
default 2 s), but never above its maximum of 10 s;
.IP \(bu 2
the per\-device time of a pull and of an apply (the default
.BR device.config.timeout ,
90 s), kept between 10 and 600 s.
.PP
An option typed on the command line wins over the scale:
.B \-\-timeout
of a pull or an apply is the time of every device of that run, as typed; the
scale replaces only the default,
.BR device.config.timeout .
The confirmed\-commit windows
.RB ( device.config.confirm_timeout ,
.BR device.apply.confirm_timeout )
are timers on the device and are never scaled. The scale is
.B timeout_scale_scope.\fIscope\fR
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; a rename moves it, a removal deletes it. A scale above 1 is
shown by
.B "device show"
(a
.B "Timeout scale"
row, and
.B timeout_scale
in
.BR \-\-json ,
with where it comes from) and by
.BR "scope show" ;
.B "config validate"
checks the stored values. The verb is the superuser's; an engineer reads the
scale in
.B "device show"
and
.BR "scope show" .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope timeout-scale
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope timeout-scale
.SS Per-scope SNMP \fB(tacctl scope snmp)\fR
.PP
The SNMP settings of the devices of a scope. A setting the scope does not set
comes from the default
.RB ( "config snmp" ),
then the built-in; every
.B show
says which one a value is. The non-secret settings are
.B snmp_scope.\fIscope\fR.{version,port,timeout,v3.auth,v3.priv,contact,clients}
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; the community and the pool of v3 users with their
passphrases and the default user are the file
.IR /etc/tacctl/snmp/\fIscope\fR.yaml
(mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, the format of
.IR snmp.yaml ),
printed only by
.BR "show \-\-reveal" .
A scope's rename moves them, its removal deletes them. The SNMP lookup of
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
uses the credentials of the device's scope (the one that answers its address;
a device in no scope uses the default). The verbs that set or clear anything,
and
.BR test ,
are the superuser's.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " show [ \-\-reveal [ user ]]"
The effective version, port, timeout, protocols, community or v3 user, contact
and allowed clients of the scope, each with where it comes from
.RB ( scope ,
.BR default ,
.BR built\-in ).
.B \-\-reveal
prints the credentials (the community and the effective user's passphrases;
with a
.I user
only that user's passphrases, of the scope's pool or the default's); an
engineer may reveal the scopes of their own, and the reveal is logged as
.BR "secret\-read kind=snmp" ,
with the user's name when a user's passphrases are printed.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope snmp
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may read the settings of a scope of their own (show [\-\-reveal [<user>]], version, port, timeout, contact, v3\-user list|default, clients list); every setter, clear and test needs the superuser tier.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-reveal\fR
Print the community and the effective user's passphrases, or one user's (an engineer: their own scopes; logged).
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\fR
Remove the contact.
.TP
\fB\-\-none\fR
Choose no default user.
.TP
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
(community, v3\-user add) Read the secrets from stdin, one per line.
.TP
\fB\-\-default\fR
Make the user the default.
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\fR \fIsha|sha256\fR
The authentication protocol: sha (HMAC\-SHA\-96) or sha256 (HMAC\-SHA\-256\-192) (default: the default's, else sha).
.TP
\fB\-\-priv\fR \fI<value>\fR
The privacy protocol: AES\-128 (the only one).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope snmp
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " version [ v2c | v3 ]"
Show or set the scope's SNMP version.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " community [ \-\-stdin ]"
Ask twice, without echo (or read one line from stdin), for the scope's v2c
community; the scope's version becomes
.BR v2c .
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " v3\-user " "add | remove | default | list" " [ user ] [ \-\-auth sha|sha256 ] [ \-\-priv aes128 ] [ \-\-default ] [ \-\-none ] [ \-\-stdin ]"
The scope's pool of v3 users and its default user, as
.B "config snmp v3\-user"
does for the default.
.B add
changes only the pool (and the scope's protocols, when given): the scope's
version is
.B version\ v3
and its default user is
.BR default .
A name that the default's pool or the pool of one of the scope's devices holds
is refused, naming the level. The scope's default names a user of the scope's
pool; with none, the default's applies. A user that a device's default names
cannot be removed. An engineer may run
.B list
and the plain
.BR default .
.BI "scope snmp " scope " clients { list | add | remove } " [ cidr [ , cidr ...]]
The ranges allowed to query the scope's devices: IPv4 networks in canonical
form, kept in the order given, at most 32, never
.B 0.0.0.0/0
(the final refusal of everything else is always rendered and never stored)
and no IPv6. The tacctl server's own address is always the first client and is
not listed. An overlap of two ranges is a note, not an error. A scope without
ranges allows this server only.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " contact [ " text " | \-\-clear ]"
Show, set or remove the scope's SNMP contact (up to 120 characters, no control
characters, no
.BR ? ).
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " port [ n ]"
Show or set the agents' UDP port for the scope.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " timeout [ seconds ]"
Show or set the wait for an answer for the scope, 1 to 10 seconds.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " clear"
Remove the scope's own settings, credentials, clients and contact: the default
applies again.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " test " address|device
Read the sysName of one address (or registered device) with the scope's
settings.
.SS Per-scope configuration blocks \fB(tacctl scope block)\fR
.PP
A configuration block is vendor syntax that a scope, or one device, owns beside
its structured settings, kept as a text file of its own and held to a grammar.
The vendors with blocks are
.B juniper
(the blocks
.B snmp
and
.BR system )
and
.B cisco
(IOS and IOS\-XE: the blocks
.B snmp
and
.BR system ) ;
a WTI unit has none. Junos and IOS are never translated into each
other, so a scope with both has a file for each.
.PP
The Junos
.B snmp
block is the SNMP that the scope's settings do not hold: communities, their
client lists and trap groups. The Junos
.B system
block is the login message and announcement, domain name and search list, time
zone, name servers, NTP, syslog hosts, the login retry options and the password
policy. A Junos block is
.B set
statements and nothing else, one per line, with
.B #
comment lines and blank lines; the login message is the one statement that is
a heredoc (the
announcement, shown before the login, is the other),
.BR "set system login message <<'EOF'" ,
its lines, and
.BR EOF .
A
.B delete
line says that what it names is not managed on this device: it is kept, never
rendered, and in a device's block takes the scope's object of that name out for
that device. A Junos block may also state
.B "set snmp trap\-options source\-address <address>|lo0"
(one; an IPv4 or IPv6 address or the keyword
.BR lo0 ):
set over the device's own value and never deleted. An address is one device's
own, so only a device's block states one; a scope's block states
.B lo0
only (with a note that a device without it cannot take it), and the check
refuses an address there. The same rule holds for
.B "set system ntp source\-address"
and
.BR "set system syslog source\-address" ,
and for the IOS
.B "snmp\-server trap\-source"
(a scope's block states a loopback by its name only) and the
.B source
of an
.BR "ntp server" .
.PP
The Junos system block also takes the statements of a closed table, each
checked: syslog
.B "set system syslog host <address|name> <facility> <level>"
(the facility and level Junos names),
.BR "... host <h> facility\-override <facility>" ,
.BR "... host <h> log\-prefix <word>" ,
.BR "... host <h> explicit\-priority" ,
.BR "set system syslog time\-format year|millisecond"
and, in a device's block only,
.BR "set system syslog source\-address <address>" ;
the login retry options
.B "set system login retry\-options"
.BR tries\-before\-disconnect " (1 to 10), " backoff\-threshold " (1 to 3), " backoff\-factor " (5 to 10), "
.BR minimum\-time " (20 to 60), " maximum\-time " (20 to 300) and " lockout\-period " (1 to 43200 minutes);"
and the password policy
.B "set system login password"
.BR minimum\-length " (6 to 20), " minimum\-changes " (1 to 128), " change\-type " (character\-sets or set\-transitions) and " format " (md5, sha1, sha256 or sha512)."
A statement is identified by its words through the host and facility (or the
leaf), a more specific layer's replaces the less specific one's, and a
.B delete
line names the same words. Anything else under
.B syslog
or
.B login
(a file, the console, users, classes) is refused: the block only states what is
the same for every device of the scope.
.PP
The IOS
.B snmp
block is configuration lines, one per line, with
.B #
and
.B !
comment lines and blank lines:
.B "snmp\-server community <string> RO|RW [<acl>]"
(the access list is a name, never a number),
.B "snmp\-server host <address> [vrf <name>] [informs|traps] [version 1|2c] <community> [udp\-port <n>] [<type> ...]"
(a trap host, one object per address, vrf, kind, udp\-port and community;
.B informs
need
.BR "version 2c" ;
SNMPv3 is refused),
.B "snmp\-server enable traps [<type> [<subtype> ...]]"
.B "snmp\-server trap\-source <interface>"
(one, the interface written in full, as the device stores it:
.BR Loopback0 )
and
.B "ip access\-list standard <NAME>"
with
.B "permit <address> [<wildcard>]"
(also
.B "permit host <address>"
and
.BR "permit any" )
under it. A
.B no
line (of a community, a host, an
.B enable traps
line, the trap source or a list) says what it names is not managed on this
device, as
.B delete
does. The IOS
.B system
block is configuration lines too, with the login banner as a heredoc:
.BR "banner login <<'EOF'" ,
its lines, and
.BR EOF
(and the same for
.B "banner motd"
and
.BR "banner exec" ;
a device's own motd or exec banner is the section's only where the block states one of that kind);
.B "ip domain name [vrf <name>] <name>"
and
.B "ip domain list [vrf <name>] <name>"
(the older spellings
.B ip domain\-name
and
.B ip domain\-list
are read as these);
.BR "clock timezone <zone> <hours> [<minutes>]" ;
.BR "ip name\-server [vrf <name>] <address> [<address> ...]" ;
.BR "ntp server [vrf <name>] <address\-or\-name> [prefer] [version <1\-4>] [key <n>] [source <interface>]" ;
.BR "ntp authentication\-key <n> md5 <string>" ;
.B "ntp trusted\-key <n>"
and
.BR "ntp authenticate" .
The banner is typed as IOS's multi\-line banner
.RB ( "banner login <d>" ,
the text,
.B <d>
alone on a line), where
.B <d>
is the first of
.B "^ ~ % # @ $ &"
that its text does not contain; a banner is at most 20 lines of 80 characters,
ASCII, with no tab or control character. A statement that names a vrf is
another object than the same statement without it (the domain name is one per
vrf), a vrf's name is letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
and an IPv6 NTP server is not in a block.
.PP
The IOS system block also takes the statements of a closed table, each checked:
.BR "logging host <address|name> [vrf <vrf>]" ,
.BR "logging trap <severity>" ,
.BR "logging buffered <4096\-2147483647> [<severity>]" ,
.BR "logging origin\-id hostname" ,
.BR "service timestamps log|debug datetime msec [localtime] [show\-timezone]" ,
.BR "login on\-failure log [every <1\-65535>]" ,
.BR "login on\-success log [every <1\-65535>]" ,
.BR "login delay <1\-10>" ,
.BR "security passwords min\-length <1\-16>" ,
.BR "security authentication failure rate <2\-1024> log" ,
.BR "ip ssh version 2" ,
.BR "ip ssh time\-out <1\-120>" ,
.B "ip ssh authentication\-retries <0\-5>"
and
.BR "clock summer\-time <zone> [recurring [<week> <day> <month> <hh:mm> <week> <day> <month> <hh:mm> [<offset\-minutes>]]]" .
A statement is identified by its words up to the value, a more specific layer's
replaces the less specific one's, and a
.B no
line names the same words. Any other
.BR logging ,
.BR service ,
.BR login ,
.B security
or
.B "ip ssh"
statement is refused; the three
.B "ip ssh"
settings are the only ones the plan types.
.PP
Both system blocks also take the services a scope requires to be absent, a
hardening that no \fBset\fR line can state: on Junos
.B "absent system services telnet|ftp|finger|xnm\-clear\-text|web\-management"
and on IOS
.B "absent ip http server|ip http secure\-server|service tcp\-small\-servers|service udp\-small\-servers|ip finger|service finger"
(only a statement the running configuration prints while the feature is on, so
that its absence can be compared). The device that holds the service, or
anything under it, differs; the plan deletes the whole statement once
.RB ( "delete system services telnet" ,
.BR "no ip http server" ),
under a comment that names the block, and the back\-out types it again. The
\fBdelete\fR (Junos) and \fBno\fR (IOS) line of a device's own block takes a
scope's marker out for that device, as it does for any statement. These deletes
are the only ones under \fBsystem services\fR, besides the NETCONF setting's,
that an apply admits. A block of \fBabsent\fR lines alone is a block: nothing
else of the device is compared with it.
.PP
The blocks of a scope are the files under
.IR /etc/tacctl/blocks/\fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR ,
and a block is in force only while
.I tacctl.yaml
names it
.RB ( blocks.\fIscope\fR.\fIvendor\fR ).
A block with an error is never rendered in part: every device of its vendor it
reaches is refused.
.PP
The communities and trap groups of a Junos
.B snmp
block become objects tacctl owns: the plan of
.B "device config plan"
sets what a device lacks and deletes a client or target of those objects that
the block does not state, under the object's real name, and the device's other
communities are kept and listed as unmanaged. The Junos
.B system
block is additive: a statement is set when it is missing, a single value is
replaced in place, and nothing the device holds is deleted.
.PP
The IOS
.B snmp
block owns a community (its line is retyped as the block states it, access and
access list; another community of the device is kept and listed as unmanaged)
and a standard access list it defines (whole: an entry the block does not state
is removed with its own
.B no
line); a list it only names is the device's own and is kept. Trap hosts (an
entry of an address, vrf, kind, udp\-port and community, as IOS keeps it, so
one address as traps and as informs is two hosts; the version and the types are
replaced in place when they differ, and IOS leaves out the default version,
kind and port) and
.B enable traps
lines (one counts as present when the device enables it, in the expanded form
it prints; a line with no type is present when the device has any) are only
ever added, and what the device holds besides is its own. The trap source is
one value, retyped in place when the device's differs (the interface is compared
without case) and kept while the block states none. The IOS
.B system
block is additive like the Junos one: a missing statement is set, a single value
(the banner, the domain name, the time zone, an NTP server's options, a trap
source) is replaced in place, a list entry is only added, and nothing the device
holds is removed. An
.B ntp authentication\-key
string is typed only where the device has no key of that number (keys compare
by number alone). A device in the IOS 12.x syntax stores
.B "ntp server <host> version 3"
without the option, so its walkthrough, managed section and plan type and
compare the line without
.BR "version 3" ;
every other option and version stays. The plan types the NTP lines in the order IOS needs: the
keys, the trusted keys, the servers and
.B ntp authenticate
last. The banner is compared by its size and a digest, never by its text, and a
banner replaced by the plan cannot be typed back by the back\-out: the back\-out
names
.B configure replace
and the saved configuration. IOS has no confirmed commit, so the plan of an IOS
device is pasted line by line and undone with a back\-out of
.B no
lines (a trap host's is
.B no
and its line up to its
.BR udp\-port ,
without the types). The report marks the section of an IOS block in the plan as
not checked on a device. A statement that names a vrf is another object than
the same statement without it, and a host of another vrf, kind or port is
another entry; the device's own stay
.RB ( "ok (+N unmanaged)" ).
tacctl does not create a vrf: the device refuses a block that names one it
lacks when the plan is pasted.
.PP
A block holds community strings, so every output but
.B "show \-\-reveal"
shows marks instead:
.B <secret:N>
for a community string,
.B <group:N>
for a trap group's name and
.B <secret>
for an NTP key, and a comment line is never shown. The login message, the
announcement and an IOS banner are no secrets (a device shows them before the
login): they are printed as they are, by
.B show
and
.BR "show \-\-effective" ;
the diff of
.B check
and
.B set
prints their size,
.BR "<message: N lines, M characters>" ,
instead.
.PP
A device's own block is laid over its scope's per object: a community (by its
string), a trap group (by its name) or a trap\-options leaf of the device
replaces the scope's of the same key whole, one it adds is added and the
scope's others stay; in the system block a single value is the device's and a
list entry is added or, when it has the same value, replaced. The
management filter's
.B permit\-snmp
term permits the resolved block's unrestricted IPv4 clients.
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " cisco|juniper list"
The blocks of the scope: for each, whether it is in force, has no file, or is a
file that
.I tacctl.yaml
does not name (and is ignored), its size, and what it states (communities and
trap groups, or statements and the login message's lines, or the number of
errors), then the findings: a file no list names and a name with no file.
Nothing is secret. An engineer may list the scopes of their own.
