Superuser only, and only at a terminal (a refusal without one changes nothing).
Open the block in the editor of the invoking user, as
.BR visudo (8)
and
.BR sudoedit (8)
do:
.BR VISUAL ,
else
.BR EDITOR ,
else
.BR editor ,
else
.BR vi .
A value may carry arguments
.RB ( "emacs \-nw" )
and a name is looked up in
.BR PATH ;
a path must be absolute. The editor runs as the invoking user, never as root,
on a private copy (mode 0600, named for the block) in a directory of its own
(mode 0700, made under
.I /var/tmp
and handed to that user) with the user's
.BR HOME ,
.BR TERM ,
.BR LANG ,
.B LC_*
and
.BR PATH ,
.B VISUAL
and
.B EDITOR
as given, and nothing of sudo's or tacctl's; the directory, swap and backup
files included, is removed afterwards on every path, a stop signal included.
The seed is the file in force; for a
block that does not exist, the grammar of the block and an example, all as
.B #
lines, and for a device's new block the scope's objects as
.B "# set ..."
