.SS Per-scope break-glass local users \fB(tacctl scope breakglass)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope breakglass " scope " [" list ]
.TQ
.BI "scope breakglass " scope " add " name " [" "--role admin" | operator | readonly ]
.TQ
.BI "scope breakglass " scope " remove " name
List, add or remove the local accounts a scope's devices keep for the day the
server cannot be reached. Only the names and roles are recorded; the role
defaults to
.BR admin .
A name must be a device-safe user name (a letter, then up to 31 letters,
digits,
.B _
and
.BR - ),
must not be a user of the scope, and must not collide with a
template-user or class name of the Juniper walkthrough or be the reserved
name
.B tacquito
or the Junos account
.B remote
(the template account of every remote user without a local-user-name). Names
compare without regard to case. The exception is
.BR root :
the Junos system account, accepted as a break-glass entry of the scope's Junos
devices, always
.B admin
(\fB--role operator\fR and \fBreadonly\fR are refused) and spelled in lower case;
Cisco and WTI render, compare and list nothing for it.
.BR "user add" ,
.BR "user scope" " (" add " and " replace )
and
.B "user rename"
refuse a user name equal to a break-glass account of a scope the user is in or
would be in, and name the
.B remove
verb as the way out. At most 16 users per scope. Stored as
.B breakglass_scope.<scope>.users
(a list of
.IR name : role )
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; it follows
.B "scope rename"
and is removed with the scope. Superuser only.
.IP
.B tacctl never stores a password or hash.
.B "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
render the account lines for the scope with a placeholder where the credential
goes (commented out, so a paste does not create an account with a placeholder
for a password) and list each recorded account on an
.B "Unfilled break-glass credentials"
line (the line stays as long as the account is recorded). Cisco:
.B "username <name> privilege <15|7|1> secret 9 <TYPE9-HASH>"
(the hash itself, as
.B "show running-config"
prints it:
.B "algorithm-type scrypt secret"
would take a plaintext password and hash it again; IOS 12.x with
.B --legacy
uses
.BR "secret 5" ),
next to the
.B "aaa authentication login default"
line that follows the scope's
.BR aaa-order .
Junos:
.B "set system login user <name> class <class> authentication encrypted-password '<HASH>'"
with the local class of the built-in
.BR superuser ,
.B operator
or
.B readonly
group for
.BR admin ,
.B operator
and
.BR readonly .
WTI: a local account of the matching access level (admin is Administrator, or
the level the group's
.B wti-level
sets, as in the mapping table of the same output) in a menu step, with
.B <PASSWORD>
where the password goes. For
.B root
the Junos walkthrough renders, commented out and with the same placeholder rule,
.B "set system root-authentication encrypted-password '<HASH>'"
(Junos keeps root there, not under
.BR "system login user" )
and
.BR "set system services ssh root-login allow" :
Junos refuses root over ssh by default, so the second line is part of the
access, and a security trade-off the person decides (root can then log in over
ssh with the password: keep the device behind the management filter, or leave
the line out and keep root on the console). The Cisco and WTI walkthroughs say
in one line that nothing is rendered for
.BR root ,
and a scope whose only entry is
.B root
still gets the no-break-glass notice there; its Cisco and WTI devices are
named, by vendor, in a
.B "config validate"
warning. A scope without break-glass users gets the existing
advice and a notice, and
.B "config validate"
warns (not an error), on one line naming the scopes, that they are a lockout
risk;
.B "scope show"
says which scope has none. The credential on the device is never created,
rotated or removed by tacctl. These are the accounts a
.B "breakglass hold"
makes usable while the server is up.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope breakglass
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fI<role>\fR
(add) The role: admin (default), operator or readonly.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope breakglass
.SH DEVICE REGISTRY
The
.I device registry
gives names to the network devices that authenticate against this server, so
that they can be listed, looked up and acknowledged by name. It is kept in
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
(mode 0600, root only) and never touches
.IR store.yaml :
a device's scope and vendor tag are looked up from the store each time they are
shown, and registering a device renders nothing and restarts
nothing. Every write takes a snapshot first (see
.BR "backup list" ).
.PP
A device is found by name or by its registered address; names are compared
without regard to case. Enrolled Linux hosts
.RB ( "tacctl host" )
share the namespace: they are listed and shown as
.B linux
devices, read-only, and a device cannot take a host's name (nor
.B "host enroll \-\-name"
a device's). An address is registered at most once. An address that is not
registered is not found, even when a scope covers it.
.PP
A name that is a factory or image default (\c
.BR switch ,
.BR router ,
.BR cisco ,
.BR ubuntu ,
.B ip\-10\-0\-0\-1
and the like; matched without regard to case) is refused with the command that
gives the device a name of its own, unless
.B \-\-allow\-generic
is given; the device then carries a
.B generic-name
notice. A host already enrolled under such a name is not refused, and carries
the notice. The built-in list can be extended with
.B generic_names:
(regular expressions matched against the whole name) in
.IR devices.yaml .
.PP
Host keys are pinned when a device is registered: tacctl reads the keys the
device offers with
.BR ssh\-keyscan (1)
(as root, outbound, without logging in; a device that accepts the
connection but gives no key to the scan of all the key types, because it
limits the simultaneous connections
.B ssh\-keyscan
opens or drops its long offer, is asked again one key type at a time,
ed25519, ecdsa, rsa, each with
.BR "\-T 3" ,
and the keys of every type that answers are pinned; a device whose name
.B ssh\-keyscan
reports as not resolving is not asked again; for a
.B legacy\-ssh
device that
.B ssh\-keyscan
cannot read, because it cannot do the SHA-1 key exchange and CBC ciphers
such a device offers, for any device that accepted the connection but gave
no key even one type at a time, and for a device it reads nothing from and
prints nothing about (a server slow to greet, a refused or filtered address),
with tacctl's own ssh client, which stops at the
host key, before authentication), keeps them in
.IR devices.yaml ,
and prints their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints to be compared with the device console. Enrolled hosts are pinned
by
.B "host enroll"
and
.BR "host sync" .
Every registry write regenerates
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts ,
which lists exactly the pinned keys under each device's name, for
.BI "ssh \-o UserKnownHostsFile=/var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts \-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o HostKeyAlias=" name " \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes" .
A pin changes only through
.BR "device hostkey accept" " or " "device hostkey set" ,
never by itself.
.PP
What the daemons saw of each device is kept in the
.I seen cache
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json ),
which
.B "device scan"
fills from the logs: the tacquito journal (the
.BI "accepting user [" user "] from [" address "]"
and
.B "failed to validate the user"
lines, the
.B "bad secret detected for ip"
and
.BI "remote [" address "] has no secret providers"
errors, and at log level 30 the
.B "prefix secret provider matches remote"
lines) and FreeRADIUS's
.I tacctl\-auth.log
with its rotations. For each address it keeps the first and last sighting,
the count, the last user, outcome and backend, and the last NAS-Identifier.
.B list
and
.B show
read the cache only, and say how old it is. An interactive session of the
console or of
.B "tacctl shell"
for the operator tier and up starts
.B "device scan \-\-if\-stale \-\-quiet"
in the background as it opens, so the logins are in the cache by the time the
list is looked at and nothing a person types waits for it. That scan does
nothing when the cache is younger than five minutes or another scan holds it
(sessions that start together run one), reads a device's host keys again only
when that device's last scan is a day old, goes on after the session ends and
prints nothing. Seen data an hour old or more is said so, with the command to
bring it up to date, in the footer of
.B list
and in the
.B Last seen
line of
.BR show .
.PP
Notices are computed each time they are shown; only acknowledgements are
stored. Besides the registry's own
.RB ( generic-name ,
.BR hostkey-unpinned ,
and for an enrolled host
.BR address\-changed :
its address changed between two enrolments or syncs),
the seen cache raises
.B ambiguous-nas-id
(one NAS-Identifier sent from several addresses; a fully qualified host name
on each device tells them apart),
.B generic-nas-id
(a device identifying itself by a generic name),
.B name-mismatch
(a NAS-Identifier other than the registry name or the hostname, either in full
or by its first label, as
.B sw1
for
.BR sw1.site-a.example ;
informational),
.B duplicate-address
(two entries at one address, or an address identifying as another entry),
.B identity-changed
(the NAS-Identifier an address sends changed: a replaced or reset unit), and
the host-key re-scan of
.B "device scan"
and
.B "device check"
raises
.B hostkey-changed
(no pinned key offered, or another key of a pinned type; it cannot be
acknowledged, only
.B "device hostkey accept"
or
.B set
clears it),
.B hostkey-added
(a new key type, the pins unchanged) and
.B hostkey-unreachable
(no key could be read; with the last good scan). A scan never changes a pin.
.B vendor\-differs
is raised when the vendor a device last named by SNMP (read by
.BR "device add" ,
.BR "device check" ,
.B "device vendor \-\-from\-device"
or
.BR "device discover \-\-snmp" ,
and kept in the seen cache) is not its registered vendor; it cannot be
acknowledged: setting the vendor, or a later read that names none, clears
it.
Notices appear in the scan output,
.BR "tacctl status" ,
.BR "device list" ,
.BR "host list" ,
.B "device notice list"
and
.B "device show"
(which counts the acknowledged ones;
.B \-\-all
on either lists them, marked).
.PP
Reading verbs
.RB ( list ,
.BR show ,
.BR "notice list" ,
.BR client\-config )
and
.B "tacctl ssh"
are open to the read-only and operator tiers, which see only the devices and
hosts whose scope is one of their own; an entry no scope covers is seen by
administrators only.
.BR export ,
.BR scan ,
.BR discover ,
.B check
and
.B "list \-\-scan" | "\-\-probe" | "\-\-differs"
are operator-level (the scan output's notices are filtered the same way;
.B discover
lists every address). Everything else needs an administrator.
.SS Device registry \fB(tacctl device)\fR
.TP
.B device
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary and exits 0.
.TP
