.B sources
array of
.IR address ,
.IR interface ,
.I result
.RB ( ok ,
.BR unreachable ,
.B mismatch
or
.BR skipped ),
.IR fingerprint " and " pinned
(a mismatch),
.I source
(a source left out) and
.IR reason .
.B \-\-json
prints the rows for scripts, the sysName as
.B sysname
(null when none was read) with
.BR sysname_match ,
the location as
.B syslocation
with
.B syslocation_match
(null when the registry has none) and
.BR syslocation_error ,
and the vendor as
.B vendor_detected
(null when none is named) with
.B vendor_reason
and
.BR vendor_error .
A juniper device also has a NETCONF row: what the probe of its last pull found
.RB ( "hello ok" ,
.BR "port closed" ,
.BR "no hello" ),
with the time, or
.B "not probed"
and the command that probes it; the check itself logs in to nothing. It is
.B netconf
in the JSON.
