.\" END GENERATED: command device config show
.TP
.BI "device config pull " "name[,name...] | \-\-all | \-\-scope " scope " | \-\-vendor " vendor " | \-\-stale | \-\-differs | \-\-name " text " | \-\-ip " addr|cidr|text " [ \-\-transport auto|netconf|ssh ] [ \-\-concurrency " n " ] [ \-\-timeout " seconds " ] [ \-\-max\-failures " n " ] [ \-\-diff ] [ \-\-json ] [ \-\-server " "address|name" " ] [ \-\-source " address " ] [ \-\-login " local-user " ]"
Log in to the devices as the user who invoked tacctl and read what each runs
(nothing is written to a device). The login is that user's: with
.B sudo
the invoking user, never root (it is refused), with the user's tacctl password,
asked for once on the terminal without echo and never stored, put in an
argument or the environment, written to a file or logged. The user must be an
active user of tacctl whose scopes include the device's. A device needs a pinned host key
.RB ( "device hostkey" ):
an unpinned device is refused with that command, a key that is not the pinned
one is
.B host\-key\-mismatch
and raises the hostkey notice. The devices are
named (comma lists too), or selected with
.B \-\-all
(every device of yours),
.BR \-\-scope ,
.BR \-\-vendor ,
.B \-\-stale
(never read, last read in failure, or differing from what tacctl renders now),
.B \-\-differs
(the last pull differs from what tacctl renders now; decided from the records,
with no login),
.B \-\-name
(the name contains the text) and
.B \-\-ip
(an address, a prefix it lies in, or a text it contains); those filters
combine, a filter given with no value is refused (exit 2), and
.B \-\-name
and
.B \-\-ip
take a comma list (any item matches). The same selectors serve
.BR diff ,
.BR plan ,
.B apply
and, for
.B \-\-name
and
.BR \-\-ip ,
.BR "device config list" .
Because
.B \-\-name
and
.B \-\-ip
match text, select the devices of an
.B apply
by exact names, a full address or a CIDR:
.B "\-\-ip 10.1.1"
also matches 172.10.1.x and 10.1.10\-19.x, and
.B "\-\-name sw1"
also matches sw10.
A vendor
.BR other ,
and a device in no scope are left out and said so.
.PP
A WTI console server has no command that prints its configuration. It is read
as its menus show it, over ssh as you
.RB ( "\-\-transport auto" " and " ssh ;
.B "\-\-transport netconf"
is refused for a unit): the pages the tables of command
menu version 8.10 name (the login's help, the network status, the Site ID, and
under
.B /N
the SNMP access and trap pages, the TACACS+ and RADIUS pages and the IP tables
list) are opened in turn with only the keys a page lists. No prompt that takes
a value is opened, nothing is typed at a prompt, the prompts of the communities
and secret words are never visited, and the final ESC, which makes the unit save
its configuration, is never sent. The system name, contact and SNMP location
that the SNMP access page cuts at about 16 characters
.RB ( ... )
are read whole by opening the item's text prompt
.RB ( /N ,
25, then 4, 5 or 6) with a CR and leaving it with ESC, which keeps the value.
The user directory
.RB ( /F )
is read in a second connection with the same password. Both connections end on
a page, which writes nothing. Only the extraction is kept, not the pages; a
community the unit prints in clear is compared against what tacctl renders and
kept as a label. A record that holds a cut value is compared by its beginning,
with a note, until the unit is pulled again. The line of a unit says how many other sessions it had
.RB ( "1 other session on the unit" ;
a count, no name) and warns when its user directory could not be read (the
second connection was refused, or the list is longer than a pull reads): the
pull is
.B ok
all the same, and
.B breakglass
reads
.B n/a
with a note. Three results belong to a unit.
.B denied
is a login whose WTI level cannot configure it
.RB ( "the login's WTI level is User; SuperUser or Administrator is needed" ,
with
.B "tacctl group edit <group> wti\-level superuser"
for the login's group): no page is opened, the pull is a
failed one (exit 1) and the batch goes on.
.B unsupported
is a command menu that is not version 8.10
.RB ( "command menu version 7.4; tables are verified for 8.10" ),
checked before any page.
.B parse\-failed
is a page the tables do not know or that is laid out otherwise: the reason names
the page's path and its title line, never its text.
.PP
Junos is read over NETCONF (the ssh subsystem, a
.B <command>
RPC) where the device answers and over an ssh exec channel otherwise;
.B \-\-transport netconf
and
.B ssh
never fall back. IOS and IOS\-XE are read over the ssh command line, with
paging off, after
.BR "show version | include Version" ,
whose line is kept on the record with the configuration syntax it says (see
.BR "device ios\-syntax" );
a device that answers no image line (the boot loader's
.B ROM:
and
.B BOOTLDR:
lines are none) or one that maps to no syntax, and has no setting, is refused
with the command to set one and nothing is recorded. A Cisco device is never
read over NETCONF:
.B "\-\-transport netconf"
refuses it before any login, as a failed line that leaves its
record alone, or as wrong arguments when every selected device is Cisco. From the
text six sections (and a seventh,
.BR system ,
for a scope that has a Junos system block; the four of a WTI unit, which are
.BR aaa ,
.BR mgmt\-acl ,
.B snmp
and
.BR breakglass )
are extracted, as in the table of
.BR "device config diff" ,
compared with what tacctl renders for that device and recorded
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json ,
with the sections themselves in
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/<name>.yaml ):
a secret is compared by presence and its value is neither kept nor printed. A
failed pull changes the record's result and leaves the sections of the last
good one.
.PP
A good pull also records three read-only facts of the device, replaced by every
good pull and never written to
.IR devices.yaml :
the release, the model and the serial number
.RB ( "device show"
and
.BR "device config list" ).
The release is the
.B Junos:
line of
.B "show version"
on Junos (an older Junos prints none: the
.B "JUNOS Base OS boot [...]"
line; else the
.B "set version"
statement of the configuration the pull read),
the version of the
.B "Cisco IOS Software ... Version"
line of the Cisco version output (else of its first image line), and a WTI
unit's command menu version. The model and the serial number are read with
more commands in the same session, after the configuration:
.B "show chassis hardware"
on Junos (the Chassis row's description and serial number; the Model line of
.B "show version"
when the row gave none) and
.B "show inventory"
on IOS and IOS\-XE (the first entry's PID and SN). A device that refuses a
command, or answers text that holds none, leaves the field empty: the pull does
not fail and its result is unchanged, and a command that stalls takes it at
most 15 seconds more. A WTI unit has a release only.
.PP
A Cisco device stores some typed lines in another form, which the comparison
takes as the typed ones: a standard ACL entry "permit host A" or
"permit A 0.0.0.0" is stored as "permit A"; trailing blanks and the order of
the aaa lines do not count; the "privilege exec level N <words>" lines IOS adds
for the words that lead to a command the rendering carries are not extra (when
nothing rendered is beneath one, it is extra, and so is the command it was
added for); the line an IOS 15.x or IOS-XE image keeps where a privilege line
is negated ("no privilege exec level 7 clear counters" leaves "privilege exec
level 15 clear counters") is the image's own and is neither compared nor
reported when the command is one tacctl lowers or has lowered (ping,
traceroute, monitor capture, clear counters, clear line, clear ip arp, clear
arp\-cache, clear mac address\-table dynamic, undebug all, show running\-config,
and the words leading to them), the level is that command's own, it has no
"all", and the rendering has no line for the command (a line at another level is
extra, and a rendered line the device lacks is still missing); an image that
does not know the last words of a privilege command (IOS 12.4 has no "monitor
capture") stores the ones it knows, which is not extra either (the command
itself stays missing, with a note that names the line the device holds
instead); and "snmp\-server queue\-length 100", which IOS 12.4 prints beside the
first community, is not extra when tacctl renders a community.
.PP
On Cisco the
.B aaa
section also holds the login method of the console and VTY lines
.RB ( "line con 0 > login authentication CONSOLE" ,
.BR "line vty 0 4 > login local" ),
read by range: with
.B "aaa new-model"
a line that has no
.B login
statement is on the default method list, which IOS does not print, and counts
as
.BR "login authentication default" ;
the rendered
.B "line vty 0 15"
is satisfied when the device's ranges with that method cover it, and a range
that says
.BR "login local" ,
.B login
or
.B "no login"
is a difference. A console with no explicit
.B "login authentication CONSOLE"
shows
.B "\- line con 0 > login authentication CONSOLE"
and
.BR "+ line con 0 > login authentication default" .
Without
.B "aaa new-model"
a line with no
.B login
statement has no method. The standard has no
.B "aaa authentication enable"
list, for TACACS+ and RADIUS alike: a
.B default
list of that kind other than
.B "aaa authentication enable default enable"
(what IOS does with no list) is an extra
.RB ( + ", severity ACCESS); a list of another"
name is an extra of that name. A source interface the device has
where the scope sets none is not listed. Legacy forms
.RB ( "tacacs-server host" " on a modern image, " "radius-server host"
beside a
.BR "radius server" " block, a server group of another name)"
are listed as extras
.RB ( + " lines), never as satisfied lines, and the walkthrough removes none of"
them:
.B "device config plan"
replaces an entry at the standard server's address in its region and
.B \-\-converge
removes the others; what neither does stays on the device until you remove
it there.
.PP
An old
.B "tacacs-server host"
entry at the standard server's address on a modern image, on a device that
.B plan
refuses (command authorization on and no server in use), is replaced from the
console, as a user that reaches privilege 15 there, in four steps:
.BR "no tacacs-server host " <address> ;
the standard's
.B "tacacs server TACACS"
block;
in each
.BR "aaa group server tacacs+ " <group>
that held
.BR "server " <address> ,
.B "server name TACACS"
and then
.BR "no server " <address> ;
then a new session and one command to check. When no group holds the old
address, because the method lists use
.BR "group tacacs+" ,
the standard group is created and pointed at the named block and the lists keep
using
.BR "group tacacs+" .
On a device that
also has
.B "aaa authorization console"
command authorization falls to local once the group has no server, and a
TACACS+ user is not in the local database: first, while the TACACS+ user's
commands still pass, type
.BR "configure terminal" ", " "no aaa authorization console" ", " end
(the standard never sets it and it is not put back); if the console session is
refused after it, type steps 1 to 4 from a new session as a user that reaches
privilege 15.
.PP
The presence of an
.B enable
secret or password is recorded, never its value: a device with none gets a
note under
.B aaa
when the rendering turns AAA on (with the server unreachable, a local user of
privilege 15 is then the only way to level 15). A record that predates these
checks is not compared on the lines (a note says so) and gets no note about the
secret until the next pull.
.PP
One line per device (rewritten in place on a terminal, appended otherwise):
.BR ok ,
.BR "ok, differs in aaa,snmp" ,
.B "failed: <reason>"
(authentication failed, timed out, unreachable, a host key that is not the
pinned one, output that is not a configuration, for a WTI unit a login level
that cannot configure it or a command menu version that is not 8.10), then a
summary
.RB ( "12 ok, 2 differ, 1 failed" ).
At most
.B device.config.max_concurrency
devices are read at once (8 by default, at most 64;
.B \-\-concurrency
lowers it for a run and is refused above it, naming
.BR "config devices" ),
each for at most
.B \-\-timeout
seconds (10 to 600, default
.BR device.config.timeout ,
connect included). The first rejected password stops the batch (and the
password cache forgets it).
.B \-\-max\-failures
stops starting devices after that many failures. The first Ctrl\-C stops
starting devices and lets the running ones finish (the rest are reported
.BR "not started" ),
a second cuts the running ones off; the status is 130 either way.
.B \-\-diff
prints the differences after the summary
.RB ( "ok (+N unmanaged)"
for a section that holds only statements of the device's own, as for
.BR "device config diff" );
.B \-\-json
prints one JSON object per device as it ends and a last object
.BR "{\(dqsummary\(dq: ...}" ,
the sections of a device with their
.B unmanaged
count when it is not 0.
.BR \-\-server " and " \-\-source
are those of
.BR "config cisco|juniper|wti" :
the addresses the expected configuration is rendered with, for devices behind a
translating firewall. A Cisco device registered with
.B legacy\-ssh
is compared with the IOS 12.x syntax. Each device is logged to syslog
.RB ( "auth.info device config pull user= device= transport= result= duration=" ),
and an engineer's pull logs the
.B secret\-read
lines the walkthrough logs. Exit status: 0 when every device was read
(differences are not failures), 1 when one failed or was refused, 2 for wrong
arguments, 130 when interrupted.
.IP
.BI "\-\-login " local-user
(superusers;
.BR pull ,
.B diff \-\-pull
and
.BR "plan \-\-pull" )
logs in to one device as one of its local accounts instead of you: the
device is named (no list,
.BR \-\-all ,
or any selector
.RB ( \-\-scope ,
.BR \-\-vendor ,
.BR \-\-stale ,
.BR \-\-differs ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-ip ):
exit 2), registered as a Junos device with a pinned host key, and a scope
answers its address; anything else is refused before a password is asked, and so is a device on
which break-glass is held (release it first). Any local account of the
device may be named, a break-glass account included; only
.B root
is handled apart. The
account's password is asked once on the terminal, sent to that one connection
and never kept: the password cache is neither asked, nor stored into, nor told
to forget. The record says
.BI login=local: account
and the pull is logged as
.BI "auth.info device config pull user=" "you " "device=" "name " "login=local:" account
(never the password).
.B root
is accepted only for a device that was never read as your own login (a pull
record file that cannot be read refuses it); its csh
is left at once by the remote command
.BR "sh \-c 'PATH=$PATH:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin; exec cli'" ,
and it reads over the ssh command line, never NETCONF. An engineer with
.B \-\-login
is refused by the tier gate (logged as every denial).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device config pull
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer gets the devices of their own scopes only.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
(pull, diff, plan, forget) Every device of yours that a scope covers.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fI<scope>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices of that scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\fR \fIcisco|juniper|wti\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only that vendor's devices.
.TP
\fB\-\-stale\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices never read, last read in failure, or differing.
.TP
\fB\-\-differs\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose last pull differs from what tacctl renders now (from the records; no login).
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<text>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose name contains the text (comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-ip\fR \fI<addr|cidr|text>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose address is that address, lies in that prefix or contains the text (comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fIauto|netconf|ssh\fR
(pull, diff, plan) NETCONF where the device answers (auto, default), NETCONF only, or the ssh command line only; (list) netconf, ssh or none: only the devices last read over that transport (none: never read).
.TP
\fB\-\-concurrency\fR \fI<n>\fR
Read at most n devices at once (never above device.config.max_concurrency).
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<seconds>\fR
Per device, connect included (10\-600; default device.config.timeout).
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-failures\fR \fI<n>\fR
Stop starting devices after n failures.
.TP
\fB\-\-timing\fR
After the summary, print how long each phase took for each device (connect, login, session, read, compare, record: a phase the device did not reach is left out; one that failed shows the time it was in) and its total, then the slowest device. With \-\-json each device's object has a "timings" object (the steps in order with their milliseconds, and the total) and the summary object names the slowest device. Nothing is stored.
.TP
\fB\-\-server\fR \fI<address|name>\fR
The address the devices are told to authenticate against, for devices behind a translating firewall (not stored).
.TP
\fB\-\-source\fR \fI<address>\fR
The address this server reaches the devices from (not stored).
.TP
\fB\-\-login\fR \fI<local\-user>\fR
(pull, diff \-\-pull, plan \-\-pull) Engineers, one registered Junos device with a pinned key: log in as that local account of the device instead of you (any local account, a break\-glass one included; only root is handled apart: a device never read as you, through its csh wrapper); its password is asked once on the terminal and never kept or cached.
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\fR
Print the differences after the summary.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(pull, diff, plan, list) Print JSON (a pull: one line per device, then the summary).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device config pull
.TP
.BI "device config diff " "name[,name...] | \-\-all | \-\-scope " scope " | \-\-vendor " vendor " | \-\-stale | \-\-differs | \-\-name " text " | \-\-ip " addr|cidr|text " [ \-\-pull [ \-\-login " local-user " ] ] [ \-\-section " list " ] [ \-\-exit\-code ] [ \-\-json ]"
Compare the managed sections of the last pull of each device with the
statements tacctl renders for it now. The sections are
.BR aaa ,
.BR roles ,
.BR mgmt\-acl ,
.BR snmp ,
.B netconf
(compared only where the scope's or the device's NETCONF setting enables or
disables the service) and
.B breakglass
(the accounts by name, never a hash; an account that differs from the scope's
only in case, such as
.B netengineer
and
.BR NetEngineer ,
is the same account and the section reads
.B ok
with a note that says so; the names are case\-sensitive on the device, and a
login by the scope's spelling is refused there; for a scope that records
.B root
a Junos device is also checked for its root password, by presence, under
.B "system root-authentication"
(a device with ssh keys only has no password, and the section says so) and for
.BR "system services ssh root-login allow" :
.B deny
and
.B deny\-password
read as a difference with the reason, and no hash or key is ever kept). A WTI unit has four sections:
.BR aaa ,
.BR mgmt\-acl ,
.B snmp
and
.BR breakglass ;
.BR roles ,
.B netconf
and
.B system
are not listed for it. The statements of the unit's other protocol page are
listed as
.B +
and kept. A section is what tacctl renders plus whatever the device holds in
the same hierarchy, so a stray server is a difference. Each section is
.BR ok ,
.B "ok (+N unmanaged)"
(a section whose extras are only statements of the device's own; see below),
.BR differs ,
.B missing
(nothing of tacctl's on the device; its lines are listed all the same),
.B n/a
(tacctl renders nothing for it; the device's statements are listed with
.BR ~ )
or
.B "not visible"
(the login could not see the secrets it consists of). The lines are
.B \-
(rendered, absent on the device),
.B +
(on the device, not rendered),
.B !
(the same statements in another order: a Junos authentication order, the terms
of a filter, a Cisco ACL),
.B ?
(a secret the device omits for that login) and
.B =
for a secret that is present
.RB ( "(present, not compared)" ).
Statements compare as sets, secrets by presence, so no value of a key,
community or hash is printed. A Junos class without the secret permission sees
its secrets masked: a masked statement is present, an omitted one is
.BR ? ,
never
.BR \- .
.B \-\-json
prints an array with the lines of each section.
.B \-\-exit\-code
makes a difference exit 2, as
.BR git (1)
does; a device that could not be compared exits 1. A device never pulled is
said so, with the command that reads it.
.PP
The name of an SNMP community is its secret and is never shown. In the compared
form it is a label:
.B <secret:N>
for the N\-th community tacctl renders for the scope (in rendering order) and
.B <other:N>
for the N\-th other string on the device (by first appearance). One string has
one label throughout a pull, a Junos
.B trap\-group
name and the community word of an IOS
.B "snmp\-server host"
included. The labels are relative to the rendering the pull was compared
with: after a scope's community is changed, pull the device again. A record
that holds the bare
.B <secret>
for every name is compared by presence, a note says so, and
.B "device config plan"
leaves every statement under a community of it alone
.RB ( "the community's name is not visible" )
and says to pull the device again.
.PP
The
.B snmp
section reads
.B "ok (+N unmanaged)"
when nothing tacctl renders is missing or different and every extra statement
is one the plan keeps by rule: another community and its client lists and
leaves, a client list, view or SNMPv3 object tacctl does not render, a
location, contact or description when tacctl renders none, and the statements
no ownership row covers
.RB ( "snmp name" ,
.BR engine\-id ,
trap groups and options, and on IOS
.BR "snmp\-server host" ,
.B "enable traps"
and the rest of
.BR snmp\-server ).
The
.B aaa
section reads so when the extras are the leaves of tacctl's own server that
the standard does not state (Junos
.B "system tacplus\-server"
and
.BR "system radius\-server" :
.BR source\-address ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR retry ,
.BR routing\-instance ;
IOS
.B "tacacs server"
and
.BR "radius server" :
.BR send\-nat\-address ,
.BR automate\-tester ),
the
.BR "ip vrf forwarding" ,
the group's own
.BR "ip tacacs source\-interface" " or " "ip radius source\-interface"
and the
.B deadtime
of the IOS server group tacctl renders, and the options of the client
.RB ( "system tacplus\-options" ,
.BR "system radius\-options" )
that do not decide who can log in or what they may do.
A leaf the standard states with another value is a difference, which the plan
replaces in place. A TACACS+
.B port
is a difference: the standard states one only for a server off the default
port, so a port on the device against a server on the default is the wrong
one. So are the options that change the service asked for or how a password is
checked
.RB ( "tacplus\-options service\-name" ,
other than
.BR junos\-exec ,
.BR strict\-authorization ,
.BR "radius\-options password\-protocol" ).
Another server or server block (a
.BR server ,
.B "server name"
or
.B server\-private
line of tacctl's group included), another group, a method list or login list of
another name, the login method of a line, the authentication order, a
.B "tacacs\-server host"
line and a deactivated statement stay differences: they decide who can log in.
The plan's Kept line names the statements of the server
.BR "TACACS+ server settings" ,
.BR "RADIUS server settings" ,
.BR "TACACS+ options" ,
.BR "RADIUS options" ,
.B "TACACS+ group settings"
or
.BR "RADIUS group settings" .
The CONFIG column of
.B "device list"
and the state of
.B "device config list"
are the pull's record and follow the next pull of the device;
.B \-\-differs
recomputes at once.
The statements are still listed, as
.B +
lines, with the note
.BR "N statements of the device's own, kept on purpose" .
The state is
.B ok
for
.BR "device config list" ,
.B \-\-differs
and
.BR \-\-stale ,
and a device that is
.B ok
only so does not make
.B \-\-exit\-code
of
.B diff
or
.B plan
exit 2 (the plan has no item for them, only a Kept line with their number). Only
statements that are no business of who can log in read this way (the
.BR snmp ,
.B system
and
.B netconf
sections, and the leaves of tacctl's own server in
.BR aaa ):
an extra server, login class, filter or user in
.BR aaa ,
.B roles
or
.B mgmt\-acl
is a finding about who can log in and the section
.BR differs ,
as does a deactivated statement, a statement under a community of a record
with bare labels, and a statement of the rendered community itself (a stray
.B clients
entry, which the plan deletes under the community's real name).
.B \-\-json
keeps the
.B state
strings
.RB ( ok
stays
.BR ok )
and gives each section the integer
.B unmanaged
(left out when 0).
.PP
The source of the AAA client must be on the device. A Junos
.B source\-address
under tacctl's
.B "system tacplus\-server"
or
.BR "system radius\-server" ,
and an IOS
.B "ip tacacs source\-interface"
or
.B "ip radius source\-interface"
(the device's own, tacctl's own, and the one under tacctl's server group), are
the device's own only while they name something the device has: an address
that one of its interfaces holds (a prefix length is ignored; the virtual
address of a VRRP group counts; a deactivated interface, unit, family or
address does not; a shut interface, Junos
.B disable
or IOS
.BR shutdown ,
still holds its address, for operational state is not checked), an interface
that exists and carries an address (its name is the first word of the
.B interface
line). Otherwise the source may not be used and the AAA packets may not reach
the server, without a word, so the statement is a difference: the
.B aaa
section
.BR differs ,
the diff lists it as a
.B +
line with the reason, and the plan reports an item of severity
.B ACCESS
(\c
.B "source\-address 192.0.2.99 is not an address of any interface this login can see"
or
.BR "source\-interface Loopback9: no such interface with an address that this login can see" ),
which the paste never fixes and
.B \-\-converge
never removes:
.BR "Left alone: add the address to an interface, or change the source (not managed by tacctl)" .
Addresses compare as addresses, not as text. An interface that takes or
derives its address by itself makes every source possible (Junos
.BR "family inet dhcp" ,
.BR dhcp\-client ,
.BR negotiate\-address ,
.B "family inet6 dhcpv6\-client"
and an
.BR "address ... eui\-64" ;
IOS
.BR "ip address dhcp" ,
.B negotiated
or
.BR pool ,
.BR "ipv6 address dhcp" ,
.B autoconfig
or
.BR "... eui\-64" ,
and
.BR "ip unnumbered" ).
The global IOS source interface is not checked while tacctl's server group
holds a source interface of its own for the protocol, which overrides it.
Only the source of the AAA client is checked, not the sources of syslog, NTP or
SNMP. The check assumes that the login of the pull sees every interface: a
Junos class that hides only some of them cannot be told from one that shows
all, and a source on a hidden interface reads as one the device does not hold.
The addresses are read at the pull from the configuration it reads anyway and
kept in the record
.RB ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json ,
not snapshotted, rebuilt by a pull). A record made before they were recorded,
or a pull whose login saw no interface, cannot be checked: the source stays
the device's own, and the diff and the plan say so once
.RB ( "their addresses are not recorded (pull the device again)" ,
.BR "this login cannot see the interfaces" ).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device config diff
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer gets the devices of their own scopes only.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
(pull, diff, plan, forget) Every device of yours that a scope covers.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fI<scope>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices of that scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\fR \fIcisco|juniper|wti\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only that vendor's devices.
.TP
\fB\-\-stale\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices never read, last read in failure, or differing.
.TP
\fB\-\-differs\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose last pull differs from what tacctl renders now (from the records; no login).
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<text>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose name contains the text (comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-ip\fR \fI<addr|cidr|text>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose address is that address, lies in that prefix or contains the text (comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fIauto|netconf|ssh\fR
(pull, diff, plan) NETCONF where the device answers (auto, default), NETCONF only, or the ssh command line only; (list) netconf, ssh or none: only the devices last read over that transport (none: never read).
.TP
\fB\-\-concurrency\fR \fI<n>\fR
Read at most n devices at once (never above device.config.max_concurrency).
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<seconds>\fR
Per device, connect included (10\-600; default device.config.timeout).
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-failures\fR \fI<n>\fR
Stop starting devices after n failures.
.TP
\fB\-\-timing\fR
With \-\-pull: after the summary, print how long each phase of the pull took for each device, as 'device config pull \-\-timing' does; with \-\-json each device's object has the "timings" object, and the slowest device's says so ("slowest": true).
.TP
\fB\-\-server\fR \fI<address|name>\fR
The address the devices are told to authenticate against (with \-\-pull; not stored).
.TP
\fB\-\-source\fR \fI<address>\fR
The address this server reaches the devices from (with \-\-pull; not stored).
.TP
\fB\-\-login\fR \fI<local\-user>\fR
(pull, diff \-\-pull, plan \-\-pull) Engineers, one registered Junos device with a pinned key: log in as that local account of the device instead of you (any local account, a break\-glass one included; only root is handled apart: a device never read as you, through its csh wrapper); its password is asked once on the terminal and never kept or cached.
.TP
\fB\-\-pull\fR
Read the devices first (takes the pull options).
.TP
\fB\-\-section\fR \fI<list>\fR
Only these sections: aaa, roles, mgmt\-acl, snmp, system, netconf, breakglass (a WTI unit has aaa, mgmt\-acl, snmp and breakglass).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(pull, diff, plan, list) Print JSON (a pull: one line per device, then the summary).
.TP
\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR
Exit 2 when a device differs (and none failed).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device config diff
.TP
.BI "device config plan " "name[,name...] | \-\-all | \-\-scope " scope " | \-\-vendor " vendor " | \-\-stale | \-\-differs | \-\-name " text " | \-\-ip " addr|cidr|text " [ \-\-pull [ \-\-login " local-user " ] ] [ \-\-section " list " ] [ \-\-converge ] [ \-\-secrets | \-\-no\-secrets ] [ \-\-plain | \-\-json | \-\-backout ] [ \-\-out " file " | \-\-out\-dir " dir " ] [ \-\-force ] [ \-\-from " address " ] [ \-\-restore\-point[=" fs "] ] [ \-\-exit\-code ]"
Print, for each device, what to paste to make it match what tacctl renders,
from its last pull. It reads no device unless
.B \-\-pull
reads them first and writes to none: the paste is the technician's. The output
of one device is, in this order: a box naming the device, its address, scope
and vendor; the
.BR report ,
one numbered item per finding
.RB ( missing ,
.BR different ,
.BR "out of order" ,
.BR extra ,
.B "secret not visible"
and
.BR "not plannable for this login" ),
each with its severity
.RB ( ACCESS :
anything on the login path, AAA, the authentication order, template users, the
management filter, a second AAA server;
.BR MONITORING :
SNMP communities and clients;
.BR COSMETIC :
order, contact, location, accounting events, privilege lines) and what the
paste does about it
.RB ( "Fixed by region line 3" ,
.BR "Left alone" ,
.BR "Not fixable by this login: EN\-CLASS cannot change 'snmp'" );
the line to type first, on the device, to check its name; and
.BR "one region" ,
between
.B "PASTE FROM HERE"
and
.BR "PASTE TO HERE" ,
which holds only statements the device takes as they stand, the wrapper lines
and one label comment per section. The region is safe pasted whole. On Junos
it is
.BR "configure exclusive" ,
the statements,
.BR "show | compare | no\-more" ,
.B "commit check"
and
.BR "commit confirmed N" :
unless the one word
.B commit
is typed by hand after the check, the switch undoes the paste by itself in N
minutes (N is
.BR "config devices confirm\-timeout" ,
default 5). The session holds the configuration lock until
.B exit
is typed (after
.BR commit ,
to confirm). If
.B "configure exclusive"
prints
.BR "error: configuration database modified" ,
nothing happened: another session has uncommitted changes, which that person
commits or discards before the paste is made again;
.B "error: configuration database locked by"
means another session holds the exclusive lock. On IOS it is
.BR "configure terminal" ,
the lines and
.BR end :
the change is live at
.B end
and nothing rolls it back by itself (there is no revert timer);
.B "write memory"
is typed by hand after the check, never in the region. After the region, as
comments: the check, the one line to type, how to undo, and what not to do
(type the follow-up before the check, paste outside the banners, paste into
another device, keep the output in a file when it holds a secret).
.PP
A statement in a standard hierarchy that the standard does not have is an
.BR extra :
the report gives the exact line that removes it, and it is left alone.
A statement of the device's own that the comparison counts as
.B unmanaged
(see
.BR "device config diff" )
is no extra and no irregularity: it has no item, is not in the number of the
header, does not keep its section out of the
.B Consistent:
line and does not make the plan non\-empty. One line per section keeps it
visible,
.BR "Kept: snmp 17 statements of the device's own (name, communities, trap groups, ...): left alone, listed by device config diff" ;
the number is the one
.B "device config list"
shows as
.BR "snmp +17 unmanaged" ,
and the kinds never name a community or a value.
.B \-\-json
has the
.B kept
list (section,
.BR count ,
.BR kinds ;
omitted when empty) and marks these statements in
.B extras
with
.BR own ;
the cover page does not count them as extras.
.PP
A secret is compared by presence only, so the line
.B Secrets:
is information and no irregularity:
.BR "Secrets: 3 present (the TACACS+ server key, the SNMP community); only their presence is checked, \-\-secrets writes them again" .
It names the kinds, never a value, a community or a label; secrets the pull's
login could not read are said apart
.RB ( "N not visible to the pull's login: the plan could not check them" ).
.B \-\-json
has the
.B secrets
object
.RB ( present ,
.BR kinds ,
.BR not_visible ;
omitted when there is none).
.PP
.B \-\-converge
makes a region that removes the extras of the kinds the standard names, for a
device that has nothing else to paste: make the plan after the paste and the
pull that verified it
.RB ( "device config pull <name> \-\-diff"
reads ok for
.BR aaa ,
and a new session as yourself works). That region is the whole output of the
device, between
.B "CLEANUP FROM HERE"
and
.BR "CLEANUP TO HERE" ,
never beside a first region, because whoever copies a whole output runs every
region in it. A device with a first region gets that region and one line,
.BR "cleanup comes after this region is pasted and verified: pull the device, then plan \-\-converge again" .
The cleanup removes other servers of the scope's own protocol and their groups
(IOS: a legacy
.B tacacs\-server host
line, a
.B tacacs server
block, an old
.BR "aaa group server" ;
Junos: another
.BR "system tacplus\-server" ,
or on a RADIUS scope another
.BR "system radius\-server" ),
a login class no user uses, and the entries of another client list. It never
removes an SNMP community (it and what is under it are left alone), a login, a
line, the enable secret, a method list (a named
.B "aaa authorization commands"
list included: the pull does not say which line uses it, and the report gives
the line to type), another ACL, a server or group of the other protocol (RADIUS
on a TACACS+ scope may serve 802.1X), a server that a staying
.B "system accounting destination <protocol> server <address>"
names, a server or group that a staying statement names, any server of the
protocol while a staying list uses
.B "group tacacs+"
or
.BR "group radius" ,
or a line that holds the address of
.BR \-\-from ;
without
.B \-\-from
it removes no client\-list entry. It is refused as a whole while the
standard's server is missing and the paste does not put it there, and a
section the pull's login could not see completely is not cleaned. On Junos the
report and the follow-up of the cleanup also ask that
.B "show system commit"
shows no pending
.B "commit confirmed"
(type the plan's
.B commit
first).
.PP
The Junos management filter is owned whole, so a move of a scope's filter mode
.RB ( "scope mgmt\-acl" " scope " mode )
between
.B ssh\-only
and
.B strict
is planned in both directions (the
.B deny\-mgmt
and
.B default\-accept
terms and the ssh and NETCONF leaves of
.B permit\-mgmt
are deleted and the service terms set, and back), and a pulled filter is
compared with the rendering of the scope's mode. A plan that changes the filter
warns of what it does to the traffic. For
.BR strict ,
where the device's filter has a
.B default\-accept
term or there is none: the traffic that stops once the filter is applied to
lo0 (routing protocols carried over IP from neighbours that are not in the list,
BFD included, TACACS+ and RADIUS replies from a server that is not in the list,
NTP, syslog and DHCP, SNMP from sources outside it other than its clients, every other
protocol; IS-IS is not carried over IP, so the filter neither stops nor permits
it),
the sources
the filter accepts (the address it accepts the authentication server's replies
from apart), and to keep a console session open. For
.BR ssh\-only ,
where the device's filter has terms and no
.B default\-accept
term (a strict filter, or the site's own under the same name): that the plan
adds the term, that the device then accepts everything but ssh, NETCONF and
SNMP from outside the list again, and the command that sets
.BR strict .
When the pulled configuration applies the filter to lo0, a plan that changes it
warns without
.BR \-\-from ,
and with it refuses the filter's section (the rest of the plan stands) if the
filter would not permit that address: in
.B ssh\-only
the address must be inside a source of
.BR permit\-mgmt ;
in
.B strict
inside a source that the filter accepts for ssh, in
.B permit\-mgmt
or in a service term that holds tcp/22 (the
.B permit\-auth\-replies
address is not one). An IPv6 address is not checked against
the family inet filter, and a filter the device does not apply to lo0 has no
check. An apply makes the same check with the address it connects from.
.PP
.B \-\-backout
prints the back\-out region alone
.RB ( "BACK\-OUT FROM HERE" ):
the statements that put back what the pull showed, for the sections the plan
changed (on IOS the
.B no
lines in reverse order with the console and VTY login leaves typed again,
never a
.BR "no line" ;
a secret tacctl does not hold is a comment saying to type it again). Every
plan that has changes keeps its back\-out beside the device's record
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/ <name> .backout ;
0600, stamped with the pull it was computed from, the device's address and the
sections it undoes, a secret's value never in it, carried by
.B "device config forget"
and
.BR "device rename" ).
.B \-\-backout
prints the kept one whenever one exists, with a first comment line that names
the sections and the pull, and prints it even when the plan itself cannot be
made (a stale record), with one line on standard error. A narrower plan from
the same pull does not replace it; a later plan with changes does, and the
report then says
.BR "this plan replaces the kept back\-out of the plan from the pull of <time> (UTC); print it first with 'tacctl device config plan <name> \-\-backout'" ;
a plan with changes and no line tacctl can type to undo them removes it and
says so; a plan with nothing to paste leaves it. For a device whose address
changed since the plan it says
.B "nothing to back out: the kept back\-out is for another device address"
(exit 0); with none kept it is computed from the pull, and with no plan to
undo it says
.B "nothing to back out: no plan was made from an earlier pull of <name>"
(exit 0).
.B "\-\-backout \-\-pull"
is refused (exit 2).
.B \-\-out\-dir
also writes it beside the plan.
.PP
.BR \-\-restore\-point [ =\fIfs\fR ]
(IOS and IOS\-XE only) adds a restore point: a block of its own before the
region, outside the
.B "PASTE FROM HERE"
banners, between
.B "RESTORE POINT FROM HERE: <device>"
and
.BR "RESTORE POINT TO HERE: <device>" .
It is one
.B "copy running\-config <fs>tacctl\-<device>\-<yyyymmdd\-hhmm>.cfg"
and one empty line (it answers the
.B "Destination filename"
prompt; a paste tool that drops blank lines turns the closing banner into the
file name, and
.B "file prompt quiet"
turns the prompt off), where
.I <device>
is the device's name up to its first dot and the time is the plan's, in UTC.
Paste the block first and on its own at the privileged prompt, then check that
.B "dir <fs>"
lists the file (for
.BR "archive config" ,
that
.B "show archive"
lists a new entry) before you paste the plan; the copy needs free space on
<fs>. The file holds the device's secrets in their own forms: protect it and
delete it when done.
<fs> is the file system named
.RB ( \-\-restore\-point=disk0: :
lower case letters and digits, then a colon; a network file system such as
.B tftp:
is refused) or, for the bare flag, the device's own archive path from the
pull's record (the file system word only); the block is then the one line
.BR "archive config" .
A bare flag for a device whose last pull read no archive path is refused
(exit 2) and says to name a file system or to pull again. The back\-out gains
an alternative after its
.B no
lines, as comments:
.B "configure replace <file> force"
(after
.BR "archive config" ,
the newest entry of
.BR "show archive" ),
with the caveats that where a release lacks it
.B "copy <file> running\-config"
only merges, that on IOS 12.4
.B "configure replace"
may skip the removal of
.B "aaa new\-model"
or stop at a line it cannot remove (an
.B "ntp server"
with options)
(do not rely on it for AAA; use the
.B no
lines), and that the file and any copy the replace leaves are to be
deleted afterwards. The kept back\-out records the file (its first comment
line says
.BR "its restore point is <file>" ).
The flag is refused for a device of another vendor, with
.B \-\-plain
and with
.BR \-\-backout .
The report says that the
.B "archive config"
form has not been checked on a device.
.PP
On a Cisco device the region follows the order of the standard block, and
.BR "command authorization is held back" :
the
.B "aaa authorization commands"
and
.B config\-commands
lines are in the paste only when the pulled configuration already uses the
scope's server (its address in a server block or host line and a login or
exec list that names the group). Once the commands list names the server every
further command, the back\-out's included, is asked of it, and it answers FAIL
for a user it does not know and never falls through: a device whose TACACS+
path has not been proven could end with no session able to run a command. The report then says that command authorization
is not in this paste, to check
.B "show privilege"
in a new session with your own account and plan again: the first plan puts
everything else, the second the one group of lines left. A
.B default
list of
.B "aaa authentication enable"
on the device (the standard has none:
.B enable
is the local enable secret) is removed with its own
.B no
line after the added lines; the back\-out types it again, and a list of
another name is left alone. A Cisco device that has no local user at privilege
15 and no
.B "aaa new\-model"
is refused for
.B aaa
(add the break\-glass user first). IOS silently refuses a second server entry
with the same address and key, so a device that holds the standard's server as
a named
.B "tacacs server <OTHER>"
block or as the legacy
.B tacacs\-server host
line on a modern image cannot take
.B "tacacs server TACACS"
beside it. The region then replaces it in an order that keeps the pasting
session: the command\-authorization lists that name the group are taken off
first, then the old entry and block (or
.BR "no tacacs\-server host" ),
then the standard block and the rest of the standard's order, and the lists
again last. A group of another name that held the old entry is pointed at the
standard block by its own name. The back\-out is the inverse (the old block and
entry back, its key a note for you to type, the lists last) and starts by
taking command authorization off. A
.B "radius server <OTHER>"
block at the standard's address and ports is replaced the same way. Only a device
that authorizes commands and shows no server in use (no login or exec list
names a group) is refused for
.B aaa
with the steps by hand below the region, because nothing proves that the
pasting session is a TACACS+ user. The old entry is removed by the region only
when the plan types the standard block's address or key. The syntax is the
setting of
.B "device ios\-syntax"
or the pull's version line, never the ssh flag; a device that needs a pull is
not planned. A Junos statement that a group applies is never changed: its
section is refused.
.PP
A WTI console server has nothing to paste: its settings are the items of
numbered menu pages, a value is live as soon as its Enter is accepted, the
configuration is written to flash when a top\-level menu is left with ESC, and
the unit has no commit\-confirm and no rollback. Its plan is
.BR "a checklist of menu steps for you to type" ,
between
.B "DO THESE STEPS ON: <unit>"
and
.BR "END OF STEPS" ,
one comment line for each setting that differs: the keys typed from the
prompt
.RB ( "/N 28 4" ),
the item's label, what to enter after the arrow (an address item asks the
protocol first:
.BR "1 (IPv4), then 192.0.2.10" )
and, after
.BR "was:" ,
the value the pull showed. The order keeps the way in open: Multiple Logins
first
.RB ( "/N 31" ,
rendered On: a unit with it Off refuses the second session that the checks and
the pull log in from), then the SNMP page, then the page of the scope's
protocol (its Enable last), then
.B "ESC ESC"
(the save) and a
.B CHECK
step: a login as a member of the scope from a second session and
.BR /H .
Above the steps the report says what to have ready: the version check
.RB ( /H
must show
.BR "Version 8.10" ;
.B /J
shows the Site ID the pull read),
a serial console or a second session that stays open, and
.B /SN
to see that no other administrator is on the unit.
A secret step names the command that shows the
value
.RB ( "<the scope's TACACS+ secret: tacctl scope secret show lab>" ,
.BR "tacctl scope snmp lab show \-\-reveal" ):
a unit's plan types no secret unless
.B \-\-secrets
is given;
.B \-\-no\-secrets
is the default made explicit. A secret the unit has is compared by presence
only; a community, which the unit prints in clear, is compared with the
rendered one and its value is never in a record, a diff or
.BR \-\-json .
.PP
The IP tables list is the unit's firewall and can lock out the way in, so it
is presented and never applied: between
.B "IP TABLES (replaces every entry; see the notes)"
and
.B "END OF IP TABLES"
the output has one
.B <iptables>
block, the whole rendered list followed by the unit's own tail as the pull
showed it (the final DROP, the OUTPUT ACCEPT and the three policies; none when
the pull showed none, and the plan warns that the list restricts nothing until
the walkthrough's last step, Step 11), for the page's prompt at
.B "/N, 5"
to replace every entry. The lines of that block are the only lines of a unit's
output that are not comments. A list that differs in an entry, an extra entry
or the order is one finding. The section is refused, with the reason in the
report and the rest of the plan still made, when the list would hold more than
48 entries (the tail counted), holds an entry the paste does not take (not
printable ASCII, not
.B "iptables \-A"
or
.BR "\-P" ,
longer than 256 characters), does not permit the tacctl server's address to
reach the unit's ssh, or does not permit the address given with
.BR \-\-from ;
without
.B \-\-from
a list that ends with the final DROP, and the pull's other sessions, are
warnings. A rule for tcp 22, a port range that holds 22, every tcp port or
every protocol permits ssh. A plain entry of the permit list renders as a rule
for every protocol and port, and a list that pastes one carries a WARNING line
at the top of the report
.RB ( "N plain permit(s) of the rendered list allow every protocol and port" ).
A scope with no management permit list (or none that permits ssh) has no IP
tables section, and one whose server address is not known is refused for it.
What the standard does not render and the unit has is its own and is left
alone (the SNMP system name and trap settings, the tail, the other protocol's
page while it is switched off, the RADIUS items the walkthrough leaves alone);
a second host, a read/write community, the other protocol's page switched on
and a local account the scope does not record are findings, listed with the
step that removes them
.RB ( "/N 25 8" ", then " "<SPACE><CR>" ).
.B breakglass
is compared and reported, never planned (walkthrough Step 10). A step on the
AAA page is warned about: a wrong secret word, host, port or service name does
not stop a login but returns every login to the unit's Default User Access
level.
.PP
For a unit
.B \-\-backout
prints the steps again with the values the pull showed as their targets, last
step first, a secret or community as a line that says to re\-enter the one you
keep, and the old IP tables list as a
.B <iptables>
block between
.B "BACK\-OUT, IP TABLES"
and
.B "END OF IP TABLES"
(first, because it is the change that can lock out; a list that was empty is
cleared slot by slot with
.BR <SPACE><CR> ),
under the same WARNING line when the list it puts back holds plain entries.
.B \-\-converge
offers only the removal of a read/write community
.RB ( "/N 25 8" )
and the Enable of the protocol the scope does not use set to Off, for a unit
with nothing else to type.
.B \-\-plain
and a
.B \-\-section
of
.B roles
or
.B system
are refused for a unit (exit 2).
.PP
A secret the device lacks is typed in its line; one the device has, or the
pull's login could not see, is neither printed nor changed unless
.BR \-\-secrets ,
which types every secret statement of the planned sections again. When a
secret is typed, the output says so (first line of the report, and on standard
error).
.B \-\-no\-secrets
leaves each as a comment line
.RB ( "VALUE NEEDED, then remove this mark" )
so that a paste cannot set the literal placeholder;
.B "\-\-plain \-\-no\-secrets"
is refused.
.B \-\-json
never holds a value; it is an array with one object per device
.RB ( name ,
.BR scope ,
.BR vendor ,
.BR based_on ,
.BR status ,
.BR reason ,
.BR irregularities ,
.BR region ,
.BR after ,
.BR backout ,
.BR extras ,
.BR warnings ,
with
.B \-\-restore\-point
.BR restore_point ,
and with
.B \-\-converge
.BR cleanup ,
.BR cleanup_after ,
.BR cleanup_deferred ,
.BR cleanup_refused ,
.BR cleanup_left ).
.B \-\-plain
is for
.BR "load set" ;
with
.BR \-\-converge ,
a device that has a cleanup makes two files: the plan's and
.IR <name>.cleanup.set .
The files of
.B \-\-out
and
.B \-\-out\-dir
are 0600 and written as the user who ran sudo;
.B \-\-out\-dir
makes its directory with mode 0700 (an existing directory open to others is
refused, and so is one that only a supplementary
group of that user may write); an existing file is refused, all of them before
any is written, unless
.BR \-\-force .
A device that cannot be planned gets no
.B \-\-plain
file (an empty one would read as nothing to load); the name of a device's
cleanup file, and
.I COVER.txt
when a cover is written, count as files of the run. A
.B \-\-plain
run that writes a device's file also removes that device's
.I <name>.cleanup.set
(the
.I <out>.cleanup.set
of
.BR \-\-out )
of an earlier plan when it does not write one itself, and a text
.B \-\-out\-dir
run removes
.I <name>.backout.txt
when the plan has no back\-out to write (each says so on standard error).
.B "\-\-plain \-\-converge"
says on standard error why a device has no cleanup file (deferred to after the
first region, refused, nothing to remove). Several devices print the cover
page first, one line per device
.RB ( consistent ,
.BR "needs change" ,
.BR "NOT consistent, nothing to paste" ,
.BR "cannot be planned" ),
then the outputs separated by a rule;
.B \-\-plain
of several devices needs
.BR \-\-out\-dir .
.PP
A device cannot be planned, with the reason on standard error and exit 1, when
it was never pulled, was pulled more than
.B stale\-days
ago, whose last pull failed, a Cisco device whose syntax is unknown, or one
whose plan has nothing to paste because a section was refused (a WTI unit
whose IP tables list is refused and that has no step is one of these).
Exit status 0 when the outputs were made, 1 when a device cannot be planned, 2
for wrong arguments (and, with
.BR \-\-exit\-code ,
when a device needs a change and none could not be planned), 130 when
interrupted. An engineer plans the devices of their own scopes, and a section
their login's class cannot commit
.RB ( EN\-CLASS :
the server, accounting,
.BR login ,
.BR snmp )
is not in the region, with one report line saying so. Every device is logged
.RB ( "auth.info device config plan user= device= status= sections= converge= secrets= out=" ,
never a value). The Cisco report states which output has been tested on a
device.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device config plan
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer gets the devices of their own scopes only.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
(pull, diff, plan, forget) Every device of yours that a scope covers.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fI<scope>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices of that scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\fR \fIcisco|juniper|wti\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only that vendor's devices.
.TP
\fB\-\-stale\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices never read, last read in failure, or differing.
.TP
\fB\-\-differs\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose last pull differs from what tacctl renders now (from the records; no login).
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<text>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose name contains the text (comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-ip\fR \fI<addr|cidr|text>\fR
(pull, diff, plan, apply, list) Only the devices whose address is that address, lies in that prefix or contains the text (comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fIauto|netconf|ssh\fR
(pull, diff, plan) NETCONF where the device answers (auto, default), NETCONF only, or the ssh command line only; (list) netconf, ssh or none: only the devices last read over that transport (none: never read).
.TP
\fB\-\-concurrency\fR \fI<n>\fR
Read at most n devices at once (never above device.config.max_concurrency).
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<seconds>\fR
Per device, connect included (10\-600; default device.config.timeout).
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-failures\fR \fI<n>\fR
Stop starting devices after n failures.
.TP
\fB\-\-timing\fR
With \-\-pull: print how long each phase of the pull took for each device, as 'device config pull \-\-timing' does, on standard error (with \-\-json one "timings" object there, with the devices and the slowest); standard output stays the plan.
.TP
\fB\-\-server\fR \fI<address|name>\fR
The address the devices are told to authenticate against, for devices behind a translating firewall (not stored).
.TP
\fB\-\-source\fR \fI<address>\fR
The address this server reaches the devices from (not stored).
.TP
\fB\-\-login\fR \fI<local\-user>\fR
(pull, diff \-\-pull, plan \-\-pull) Engineers, one registered Junos device with a pinned key: log in as that local account of the device instead of you (any local account, a break\-glass one included; only root is handled apart: a device never read as you, through its csh wrapper); its password is asked once on the terminal and never kept or cached.
.TP
\fB\-\-pull\fR
Read the devices first (takes the pull options); the plan is made from that pull.
.TP
\fB\-\-section\fR \fI<list>\fR
Only these sections: aaa, roles, mgmt\-acl, snmp, system (the report still names the others).
.TP
\fB\-\-converge\fR
Add the cleanup of a device with nothing else to paste: the region that removes the old servers and the like (a device with a first region gets that and a line to plan again after the paste; a WTI unit: the read/write community and the other protocol's switch).
.TP
\fB\-\-secrets\fR
Type every secret of the planned sections, also the ones the device has (a WTI step names the command that shows the value unless given).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-secrets\fR
Leave each secret as a marked line to fill in (no plain form; a WTI step already does).
.TP
\fB\-\-plain\fR
The statements of one device alone, for 'load set' (\-\-converge: two files, with \-\-out or \-\-out\-dir); a WTI unit has none.
.TP
\fB\-\-backout\fR
Only the back\-out region (what puts the pulled lines back; the one kept from the plan once a later pull replaced them; not with \-\-pull).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
One object per device: the findings, the region, the cleanup and the back\-out; values elided.
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fI<file>\fR
Write one device's output to a file (0600).
.TP
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fR \fI<dir>\fR
One file per device (<name>.txt, .set with \-\-plain, .json with \-\-json; each plan's .backout.txt) and COVER.txt, 0600 in a 0700 directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Replace files \-\-out or \-\-out\-dir would write.
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fI<address>\fR
The address you are logged in from: the cleanup keeps it; the management filter check (in either filter mode) and a WTI unit's IP tables check use it.
.TP
\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR
Exit 2 when a device needs a change (and none could not be planned).
.TP
\fB\-\-restore\-point\fR[=\fI<fs>\fR]
(IOS and IOS\-XE only) A restore point block before the plan: a copy of the running configuration on <fs> (flash:, bootflash:, disk0:), or, bare, 'archive config' where the pull read an archive path; the back\-out names 'configure replace' with it; a file, not a timer (the bare form has not been checked on a device).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device config plan
.TP
.BI "device config apply " "name[,name...] | \-\-all | \-\-scope " scope " | \-\-vendor juniper | \-\-stale | \-\-differs | \-\-name " text " | \-\-ip " addr|cidr|text " [ \-\-check ] [ \-\-confirmed ] [ \-\-max " n " ] [ \-\-pause " seconds " ] [ \-\-hold ] [ \-\-no\-canary ] [ \-\-section " list " ] [ \-\-pull ] [ \-\-transport " auto | cli | netconf " ] [ \-\-concurrency " n " ] [ \-\-timeout " seconds " ] [ \-\-max\-failures " n " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-source " address " ] [ \-\-exit\-code ] [ \-\-json ] [ \-\-yes ]"
Write the plan to Junos devices: what
.B "device config plan"
would have a person paste, typed by tacctl, under a confirmed commit and
proven. It logs in as you, as
.B pull
does (the password is asked for once and never stored), and has two phases.
The
.BR check :
each selected device is read, planned from that read, loaded in an exclusive
configure session
.RB ( "configure exclusive" ),
checked with its own
.B "commit check"
and discarded; the devices that need a change are then shown grouped by
.BR shape
and by what the review shows of each (the same change under one heading,
every secret elided, a statement whose per\-device value differs printed for
each device after its name, with what the plan keeps and what it leaves out;
two devices of one shape whose warnings, sections left out, kept statements
or not\-found deletes differ are two headings). Then each device to apply is
asked for in turn, in the order of the apply (registry order, at most
.BR \-\-max ):
its line (shape and counts), then
.BR "Type the device name to apply to <name>:" .
The name typed back approves the device (spaces and tabs around it are
ignored); anything else (another name, another case, a number, a carriage
return or any other character, Enter) declines it with no second try, and the
end of input declines it and every device after it. There is no answer for several devices
and no default. Every question is answered before the first commit; the
canary,
.BR \-\-pause ,
.B \-\-concurrency
and
.B \-\-hold
run over the approved devices. A declined device is a skipped row
.RB ( "skipped: not approved (the answer typed is not its name)" ),
nothing is sent to it, the approved ones are still applied and the run exits
1; with
.B \-\-json
each answer is an object with
.BR "\(dqphase\(dq:\(dqreview\(dq" ,
.B "\(dqstatus\(dq"
.B approved
or
.B declined
and
.BR "\(dqmode\(dq" ,
a declined device has only that object (no
.B "\(dqphase\(dq:\(dqapply\(dq"
one), and the summary says
.BR "\(dqstop\(dq:\(dqnot\-approved\(dq" .
A Ctrl\-C at a question, or after the last answer and before the first
commit, applies nothing (exit 130): the answers already given stay logged and
one line,
.BR "device config apply review user= result=interrupted applied=0 mode=" ,
with
.B \-\-json
an object
.BR "\(dqphase\(dq:\(dqreview\(dq,\(dqstatus\(dq:\(dqinterrupted\(dq" ,
says the review was cut short. A device whose plan
refuses a section (what makes
.B "device config plan"
exit 1) is not applied: its row is
.BR "failed: the plan refuses <section> (<why>)" .
It needs a terminal; with none the only way through is
.BR "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes" .
The
.BR apply :
each device is committed under
.B "commit confirmed N"
.RB ( "config devices apply\-confirm\-timeout" ),
proven by a fresh login as you through the device's own login path, by
.B "show configuration | compare rollback 1"
being the change the check showed and by a read of the planned sections
lacking nothing, and then confirmed in the session that committed, with a
minute of the window left. On a device with several members (a virtual
chassis, two routing engines, a cluster) the members are the blocks
.B "show version"
lists
.RB ( fpc0: ,
.BR re0: ,
.BR node0: ),
and the answers of
.B "commit check"
and of the commits must name exactly those, each with its own success; a member
that is missing, unexpected or answers otherwise fails the step, and the error
names the member and its text. The local (master) member of a virtual chassis
answers without a label: its success comes first, the other members follow with
their header
.RB ( fpc1: ),
and on a commit its
.B "commit complete"
is the last line, after theirs. It is read as the member the labelled blocks
leave out (exactly one member of
.B "show version"
may lack a block), and held to rules like the others': its lines are the check
(a commit leaves it out or prints it) and, on a confirmed commit only, the note,
then
.B "commit complete"
last; a member's block is the check (optional on a commit),
.B "commit complete"
and, on a confirmed commit only, the note, which every member of a confirmed
commit must print; each line once and in that order. A line that is none of
these, a label twice and two members without a block fail the step. A chassis of one member answers like a single
device:
.B "show version"
prints its header
.RB ( fpc0: )
and the answers carry no member label, which is read, as is a block labelled
with that one member; a block of any other member, or a label twice, fails the
step. Two routing engines that commit without
.B synchronize
answer as a single device does, which is read as before. Over NETCONF each
member is a
.B routing\-engine
element named by its label; a reply of a virtual chassis or a cluster of two or
more members that names no member is refused, because nothing proves the other
members committed (a confirmed commit rolls back by itself), and the command
line transport (the default) can be used; a chassis of one member is read like
a single device, with no member element or one named by that member, and
another name is refused. The shape of these replies over NETCONF is inferred:
no capture of it exists, nor of the commits' replies of a chassis in which every
member has a label on the command line (the shape with an unlabelled master was
captured from a real three-member chassis). A proof that fails cancels the commit
.RB ( "rollback 1" ,
.BR commit )
and the device is
.BR "rolled back" ;
a device whose state could not be read after the confirmed commit is
.BR "PENDING until <time>" :
over the command line it undoes the change at the end of its window, not when
tacctl's session dies, and any
.B commit
or
.B "commit check"
typed on it confirms the change, so nothing may be typed on it before then.
The next apply or check of it reads its commit history first and records what
happened; while the window stands nothing is sent. The history is read whatever
words each commit was typed with
.RB ( synchronize ,
.BR and\-quit ,
.BR force ,
.BR full ,
.BR prepare ,
.BR "at <time>" ,
.BR "confirmed, rollback in N mins" ),
and a newest entry that is none of these is refused with its text. Where the history alone
does not say (a commit after tacctl's by
.B "root via other"
is the device's own rollback or a commit of the system, or the commit after
it is someone else's), the device's own rollbacks do: with tacctl's commit at
history entry N,
.B "show configuration | compare rollback N"
empty is
.B applied
and
.B "compare rollback N+1"
empty is
.BR rolled\-back ,
both read in one operational session; anything else is
.B unknown
and never applied, whatever the rendering is now. The settled result is
printed above the device's line
.RB ( "the earlier apply <id> by <user> was rolled back by the device at <time>" ;
JSON
.BR settled )
and logged
.RB ( "auth.notice device config apply settle user= device= id= result=" ).
The record says
.B pending
from the moment the confirmed commit is typed, so a tacctl killed during the
verification leaves a record the next run settles; a record that cannot be
written then is said at once, in red, on standard error, and the transaction
goes on.
.PP
A device is reached over NETCONF or the command line, as
.B \-\-transport
(default
.BR device.apply.transport )
says.
.B auto
uses NETCONF for a device whose last read found the NETCONF service answering
and whose login may commit, and otherwise the command line, with the reason
after the device's line
.RB ( "via cli: NETCONF port closed at the last read" ;
a device never read says nothing; JSON
.BR via ,
.BR via_reason );
.B cli
is the command line always; and
.B netconf
never falls back: a device that does not answer or cannot confirm is
.BR failed .
Over NETCONF the change is loaded under the candidate's lock (a Junos device
refuses a confirmed commit in a private session), checked, committed with a
confirmed commit and confirmed in the session that committed; a session that
closes or dies without confirming leaves the commit pending until its window
ends, as over the command line, and a failed verification is cancelled at once
(\fBrollback 1\fR and a commit) while tacctl runs. A
candidate that holds another editor's staged change is refused.
A change to the NETCONF service itself
.RB ( "scope netconf" ,
.BR "device netconf" )
is always made over the command line, because its verification depends on the
service; with
.B "\-\-transport netconf"
it is refused. Its verification is a NETCONF probe on a fresh login before the
confirming commit: enabling the service must be answered by a hello as you,
disabling it must find the hello no longer answered
.RB ( "port closed"
or
.BR "no hello" );
a probe that cannot tell fails the proof and the change is rolled back. The
line says
.B "[NETCONF verified: hello ok]"
or
.BR "[NETCONF verified: not answering]" ,
and the record's NETCONF probe becomes what was proved.
.PP
.B \-\-check
stops after the device's own check: nothing is committed, the shape and the
result are recorded
.RB ( \-\-exit\-code :
exit 2 when a device needs a change).
.B \-\-confirmed
applies only shapes an earlier apply confirmed and skips the others
.RB ( "unknown shape <hash>: review and apply it by hand first" ),
and is the one form
.B \-\-yes
answers the review for (every device's question, with no terminal);
.B \-\-yes
without it is refused (exit 2).
.B \-\-max
(default 5) caps the devices of every apply run. The first device of every shape is
applied alone and the rest of its shape follow only when it applied
.RB ( \-\-no\-canary :
together);
.B \-\-pause
(default
.BR "config devices apply\-pause" )
waits between devices and
.B \-\-concurrency
applies several at a time (default one; the checks run in the pool of
.BR pull ).
.B \-\-max\-failures
defaults to 1. At the start of each device's apply the break\-glass hold, the
registry entry (name, address, vendor, hostname, port, host keys) and the
scope that covers it are read again: a device held, changed or no longer
yours since the review fails with the reason and nothing is sent to it.
.B \-\-hold
keeps the change only when, after the fresh login, you log in with one
account of each class and type
.BR OK ;
anything else, or no answer while a minute of the window is left, rolls it
back. A line typed before the question of a device, after the device before it
was answered or timed out, is not its answer; one typed ahead of the first
question is. It needs
a terminal and is refused with
.B \-\-json
(exit 2).
.B \-\-section
limits the sections
.RB ( aaa ,
.BR roles ,
.BR mgmt\-acl ,
.BR snmp ,
.BR system ).
.B \-\-pull
reads the selected Junos devices first, as
.B "device config pull"
does (its lines, records and audit lines; over
.BR device.config.transport ,
since
.B \-\-transport
is how the apply writes), with the one password of the run; a device whose
pull fails is a failed row and is not checked, and a pull that stops
(interrupted, a refused password,
.BR \-\-max\-failures )
ends the run with nothing checked. Then
.B \-\-stale
and
.B \-\-differs
select from the records the pull has just written
.RB ( "skipped <n> (<names>): pulled, and the fresh pull matches what tacctl renders" ;
JSON
.BR left_out.match_after_pull ;
a device that cannot be compared is
.BR "could not be compared" ,
JSON
.BR left_out.not_compared_after_pull ,
and is not checked either),
and the devices left go on to the check:
.B "\-\-pull \-\-differs"
applies to the devices that differ now, and with no other selector pulls every
Junos device you can see (the apply is still capped by
.B \-\-max
and reviewed). Without them every device pulled is checked. With
.B \-\-json
each pull object carries
.BR "\(dqphase\(dq:\(dqpull\(dq" ;
a named Cisco device or WTI unit's refused row comes before them, and a device
whose pull failed has only its pull object (the summary counts it failed).
Select the devices of an apply by exact names, a full address or a CIDR:
.B \-\-name
and
.B \-\-ip
match text.
.B \-\-json
prints one object per device and phase, then the summary, and sends the review
to standard error.
.PP
Never written, whatever is given:
.BR breakglass ,
.B netconf
(unless the scope's or the device's NETCONF setting enables or disables the
service: then
.BR "set system services netconf ssh"
with its limits, or
.BR "delete system services netconf" ),
.BR "system root\-authentication" ,
.BR "system services"
(but for the exact statements of the NETCONF setting and the delete of a service
the system block requires to be absent),
.BR interfaces ,
groups, a local user's credentials, the root user, the account that runs it and
its login class, and the deletion of a whole hierarchy tacctl renders; every
statement is held against the planner's ownership tables before the first is
typed, and a statement outside them stops the device with nothing sent. A
statement that a group supplies is never changed. Refused before any login: a
caller below the superuser, a device held for break\-glass, one no scope covers
or whose scope is not yours; a Cisco device or a WTI unit named is a failed
row and a selector leaves them out. A device that reports another name than
its registered name or hostname fails
.RB ( "calls itself" ),
another editor's staged or open configuration fails it
.RB ( "other editors or staged changes" ).
.PP
The first Ctrl\-C starts no more devices and lets the running one finish, the
second cancels: before the commit the candidate is discarded, after it the
commit is cancelled where the window allows and otherwise left PENDING. Exit
status: 0 every device applied, was checked or had no change; 1 a device
failed, was rolled back or is pending, a device was declined at the review, or devices
were selected and none was applied or found already applied
.RB ( "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes"
that skipped them all:
.BR "Nothing was applied: every device was skipped." ,
JSON
.BR "\(dqstop\(dq:\(dqnothing\-applied\(dq" );
2 wrong arguments; 130 interrupted. The last check or apply of a device is kept in its
record (when, by whom, the change identifier, result, shape, sections,
counts and the confirming commit: no raw output and no secret) and shown in the
.B APPLIED
column of
.BR "device config list" ,
the Configuration row of
.B "device show"
and their JSON as
.BR applied .
Every device is logged
.RB ( "auth.info device config apply user= device= id= shape= result= duration=" ,
.B "auth.notice device config apply review user= device= shape= answer=approved|declined mode="
for each device's answer,
.B "auth.notice device config apply user= approved= shapes= canary= mode="
for the approval, the canary being the first device of each shape or
.B none
