.SS SSH to a device \fB(tacctl ssh)\fR
.TP
.BI "ssh " name|address " [ \-p " port " ] [ \-X | \-Y ] [ \-g ] [ { \-L | \-R | \-D } " spec " ]... [ \-\- " "ssh args" " ]"
Open an ssh session to a registered device or an enrolled host, named by its
name or its registered address. tacctl resolves the name as root and admits
only an active tacctl user (in the store, not disabled, with a password) whose
scopes include the entry's, at every tier, superusers included; an entry in no
configured scope is refused to everyone, and a local account that is not a
user of tacctl is refused
.RI ( "'user' is not a tacctl user" ;
.IR "'user' has no access to scope 'scope' (device name)" ).
Each refusal is logged to syslog
.RB ( "ssh DENY user=" "\fIuser\fR " device= "\fIname\fR " scope= "\fIscope\fR " reason= "\fIreason\fR" ,
auth.warning). An admitted session is logged as
.BI "ssh user=" user " device=" name " addr=" address
(auth.info; a
.BI from= client
field names where the caller logged in from, when it can be told: the first field of
.B SSH_CONNECTION
or the host of the terminal's login record) and, when ssh ends, as
.BI "ssh end user=" user " device=" name " status=" n " duration=" seconds\fR;
.BR ssh (1)
runs as the invoking user
.RB ( "sudo \-u " "\fIuser\fR " \-H ),
with the terminal, returning its exit status. The login is always the invoking
user's own name and the session authenticates by password only: no agent, no
key, no other login; an enrolled host's enrolment account and identity are
never used. It never runs ssh as root: run by root itself (no
.BR SUDO_USER ,
or
.BR SUDO_USER=root )
it refuses with
.IR "tacctl ssh runs ssh as the user who invoked it; run it from your own account, not as root" .
It needs a terminal on stdin
.RI ( "a terminal is required" ).
An address that is not registered is refused with the
.B "device add"
command to register it.
.IP
ssh connects to the device's hostname, else its address (an enrolled host: its
target's host), with its port unless
.B \-p
is given, and always
.B "\-o ConnectTimeout=10 \-o PubkeyAuthentication=no \-o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard\-interactive,password"
.RB ( wti :
.BR "PreferredAuthentications=password" ).
A device with
.B legacy\-ssh
gets
\fB\-o KexAlgorithms=\:+diffie\-hellman\-group14\-sha1,\:diffie\-hellman\-group1\-sha1
\-o Ciphers=\:+aes128\-cbc,\:aes192\-cbc,\:aes256\-cbc,\:3des\-cbc
\-o HostKeyAlgorithms=\:+ssh\-rsa \-o PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms=\:+ssh\-rsa\fR
(old IOS; OpenSSH 8.5 or later; the CBC ciphers are appended after the strong
ones). A device with pinned host keys adds
\fB\-o UserKnownHostsFile=\:/var/lib/tacctl/\:ssh/known_hosts
\-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes
\-o HostKeyAlias=\fR\fIname\fR\fB \-o UpdateHostKeys=no\fR,
so ssh accepts the pinned key and nothing else. An unpinned device is checked
against the user's own known_hosts, after its
.B hostkey\-unpinned
notice is printed (unless acknowledged). Arguments after
.B \-\-
follow the target, so ssh takes them as the remote command; they cannot
replace an option.
.IP
.BR \-X " and " \-Y
forward X11 (untrusted, trusted) to this server's display
.RB ( DISPLAY ,
which sudo keeps for tacctl; without one the command says to log in with
.BR "ssh \-X" );
.BR \-L ,
.B \-R
and
.B \-D
(each repeatable) are ssh's local, remote and dynamic port forwardings, with a
.I spec
of addresses, ports, host names or socket paths only. The session's log line
then carries
.BR forward= \fIkinds\fR
.RB ( x11 ", " local ", " remote ", " dynamic ).
In the login console they are for the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
only (default
.BR superuser );
any other tier is refused
.RI ( "Forwarding (\-X, \-Y, \-L, \-R, \-D) is not available to the tier tier in the console" ;
logged as
.BR "ssh DENY ... reason=forward" ).
There,
.B \-L
and
.B \-D
listen on loopback only:
.B \-g
and a bind address other than loopback
.RB ( 0.0.0.0:8443:localhost:443 ", " *:1080 ,
or an empty one) are refused
.RB ( "ssh DENY ... reason=gateway" )
unless
.B "console forwarding gateway\-ports"
is enabled.
.B \-g
lets
.B \-L
and
.B \-D
ports listen on every address, as ssh's
.BR \-g .
.IP
From a session of the login console (the console marks each line with
.BR TACCTL_CONSOLE= \fIsession\fR),
ssh reads no configuration file and opens nothing but the session: it starts
with
.B "\-F /dev/null \-o PermitLocalCommand=no \-o ControlMaster=no \-o ClearAllForwardings=yes \-o ForwardAgent=no"
.RB ( ClearAllForwardings
left out when the session forwards) and
.B "\-o EscapeChar=none"
(unless
.BR "console ssh\-escape enable" ),
the target follows
.B \-\-
so no word after it is an option, and words after the name's
.B \-\-
that start with
.B \-
are refused. An entry with no pinned host key is refused
.RI ( "'name' has no pinned host key, so the console does not connect to it; an administrator pins it: tacctl device hostkey name accept" ;
logged with
.BR reason=unpinned ).
Its log lines end with
.BI console= session\fR.
.IP
When ssh ends with status 255 on a pinned device, tacctl reads the device's
keys again; if the pinned key is not among them it prints the pinned and the
offered fingerprints, the console command that shows the key on the device,
and
.BI "tacctl device hostkey " name " accept"
or
.BI "set SHA256:" fingerprint
to re-pin after verifying. The read-only tier may run it (the tiers sudoers
rules grant
.B "tacctl ssh *"
and
.BR "tacctl device ssh *" ).
.IP
With the session's password cache on
.RB ( "console password\-cache" )
and empty, and a device with pinned host keys, ssh and
.B "device ssh"
ask for the user's password themselves the first time, before they connect:
the prompt
.B "Password for"
.IR user
.B "(device login):"
is on the terminal without echo (Ctrl\-C ends the line with status 130 and
starts nothing; an empty answer stores nothing), the answer goes into the
cache, and the session then takes the path below, so the password is typed
once, before the device banner, and the next ssh to any device asks nothing;
the log line ends
.BR password=asked .
It is stored before the device has seen it, so a wrong one stays only until an
ssh ends with status 255, which forgets it. A word a device asks for later is
never stored. Without a terminal to prompt on, ssh asks for the password
itself, as without the cache.
.IP
When the session's password cache holds the user's password
.RB ( "console password\-cache" )
and the device has pinned host keys, ssh is not asked for it on the terminal:
it is started as the user directly, without
.BR sudo ,
with the helper
.B SSH_ASKPASS
(the tacctl binary; it answers a password prompt and nothing else),
.BR SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force ,
.BR "\-o NumberOfPasswordPrompts=1" ,
.BR "\-F /dev/null"
and the pinned host key checked strictly; the log line then ends
.BR password=cached .
Words after
.B \-\-
that start with a dash, an unpinned device, and an account that is not in the
local account database keep the prompt. An ssh that ends with status 255
makes the cache forget the password.
