.SS Break-glass hold \fB(tacctl breakglass)\fR
A device's local accounts
.RB ( "tacctl scope breakglass" ;
.B root
among them, for a Junos device)
reach its vty only while no server answers it: a server's refusal is
final, so a local login there is refused while the server is up. On a Cisco
console the
.B CONSOLE
list is local, so a local account logs in there with the server up, lands at
privilege 1, and
.B enable
with the local enable secret takes it to 15. A
.I hold
makes the server stop answering the registered devices (or enrolled Linux
hosts, below) named on purpose, so
that their local accounts can be used on a vty (and those of a Junos or WTI
device) while the server is up. A hold is for an exceptional case; it has no
timer, no automatic release and no whole\-scope form. Several devices are
held in one command, best effort: each is checked on its own, the ones that
pass are held together (one write of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
one render, one restart of each backend that changed) and the others are
named with why under
.BR "Not held:" ;
the exit code is 1 when any was not held.
.PP
An enrolled Linux host
.RB ( "host list" )
is held the same way, by name, with the same record and carve\-out. Its
address is the one
.B "host enroll"
or
.B "host sync"
recorded, else its ssh target's when that is an address (a name is never
resolved for this). The server then answers no TACACS+ or RADIUS login and no
sudo check for it, while the host's local administrator (an account that does
not authenticate through tacctl) and its console work. The report, the prompt
and the way in are the host's own, and the audit lines read
.B host=
in place of
.BR device= .
This server's own host
.RB ( "host enroll \-\-local" ),
a loopback address, a listener's address, a host with no recorded address and
no address as its ssh target, and a name that is both an enrolled host and a
registered device (refused as ambiguous, both named) are not held; a device is
not held, nor moved by
.BR "device address" ,
at an enrolled host's address, and a host is not held at a registered
device's address (a hold there would silence the device too). A host is held
by its name: an address that is only its ssh target's is refused, naming the
host. While the hold stands
.BR "host sync" ,
.BR "host show \-\-check" ,
.B "host enroll"
of the registered host
.RB ( "host move" ),
.B "host target"
with a change,
.BR "host provisioner rotate" ,
.B "host unenroll"
and
.B "host forget"
are refused with the hold named, before anything is attempted over ssh;
.B "host sync \-\-all"
and
.B "host check"
skip the host;
.B "host list"
marks it,
.B "host show"
has a
.B Break\-glass
section, and
.B "tacctl ssh"
warns and connects.
.PP
The record is
.B breakglass_hold.<device>.address|since|by|reason
in
.IR tacctl.yaml
(a device name's dots are written
.B ~
in the key), so every snapshot holds it. Both renderers take the held address
out of every prefix of every scope, and out of every tagged address (RADIUS):
a /24 that holds it answers as the eight prefixes /25 to /32 that
do not, a prefix equal to the address is gone, and every other address is
answered by the scope that answered it before.
.I store.yaml
is never changed.
.B tacquito.yaml
and
.B clients.conf
carry a comment that names the address, the device or host (worded
\fB(device \fIname\fB)\fR or \fB(host \fIname\fB)\fR) and the prefixes it
was carved from, never the reason. Each backend whose file changed restarts once,
at the hold and at the release; a release that changes nothing restarts nothing.
Only the address the device is registered at is carved: a device that sends its
AAA from another address (a source interface, a NAT) is still answered. A record holds a device only while the device is
registered at the record's address (a host: while a host of the name is
enrolled and its recorded address is the record's); any other record (the
device was removed by hand, or registered under the name at another address) is an
orphan, which
.BR "breakglass list" ,
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
name, whose address stays carved, and which
.B "breakglass release"
removes.
While a hold stands,
.B "device remove"
of the device is refused,
.B "device address"
moves the record to the new address (the registry and the record in one write,
one render, one restart of each backend that changed; the new address passes
the checks a new hold passes, and one question names both addresses unless
.B \-\-yes
is given),
.B "device rename"
and
.B "host rename"
move the record,
.BR "device config pull" ,
.B "diff \-\-pull"
and
.B "plan \-\-pull"
refuse the device (a batch skips it),
.B "device config plan"
of the last pull names the hold before its items and the cover page marks the
device,
.B "tacctl ssh"
warns and connects, and
.BR "tacctl rollback"
is refused.
.B "backup restore"
is a render like any other: a hold comes back with a snapshot that has it and
goes with one that lacks it, and the restore names the devices held now and
after it when that set changes.
.B status
(a red line per hold),
.BR "device show" ,
.BR "device list" ,
.BR "device check" ,
.BR "device scan" ,
.BR "scope routing" ,
.BR "scope show" ,
.BR "scope list" ,
.B "scope lookup"
and
.B "config validate"
say so for as long as it stands. Both verbs write an
.B auth.info
line per device (the reason is in the hold's:
.BI "breakglass hold device=" name " addr=" ip " reason=\(dq" text "\(dq by=" user
and
.BI "breakglass release device=" name " addr=" ip " held=" duration " by=" user )
and take the snapshot every change takes. A hold that
.B "device address"
moves writes
.BI "breakglass hold\-move device=" name " from=" ip " to=" ip " by=" user .
.TP
.BI "breakglass hold " "name[=reason][,name[=reason]...]" " [\-\-reason " text ]
Hold break\-glass on registered devices and enrolled Linux hosts, given as one comma
list in one argument (quote it when a reason holds a space). Each device has a reason, 1 to 256
characters: its own after
.B =
(which ends at the next comma, so a reason that holds a comma is given with
.BR \-\-reason )
or the
.B \-\-reason
default; a name with neither is a usage error that names it, and a name is
given once. Each name is checked on its own, as below: the ones that pass
