.SS Emergency lockdown \fB(tacctl emergency)\fR
A \fIlockdown\fR refuses the logins of chosen groups on every device and Linux
host a scope answers. Where a
.I hold
makes the server stop answering one device so that its local accounts can be
used, a lockdown keeps answering and says no: the users of the locked groups
are taken out of the scope when the backends render
.RB ( tacquito.yaml
and the RADIUS users file), so every login and every further command
authorization is refused (TACACS+ FAIL, RADIUS Reject), never silence, and a
device does not fall back to its local accounts on that account.
.I store.yaml
is not changed; lifting the lockdown renders the files as they were. A user of
a superuser group (its tier is superuser and it is not a group at priv\-lvl 15
with no tier setting) is never locked. There is no timer.
.PP
A device's own local accounts are not tacctl users and are not locked. They
stay out of the way only because of the device's method list. Not closed: a
Cisco console line, the Junos root account on the console, a scope that lists
local accounts first
.RB ( "scope aaa\-order local\-first" ),
local ssh keys, sessions that are open (Junos devices, WTI units and Linux
hosts keep a session until it logs in again or runs sudo) and a device that
cannot reach the server.
.B status
names these and
.B enable
warns in red on a local\-first scope; nothing is changed on any device.
.PP
While a lockdown stands, every command that names the scope, a device or
enrolled host in it, or that is about no one scope
.RB ( status ", " "config validate" ", " "scope list" ", " "device list" ", " "host list" )
writes one red line on standard error, once per command:
.B "EMERGENCY LOCKDOWN on scope '<scope>' since <time> by <user> (<duration>): logins of groups <groups> are refused; superusers keep access. Reason: <text>"
Standard output and the exit status are unchanged. An engineer sees the
lockdowns of their own scopes.
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
list them too; the latter reports a record that names a scope or group that is
gone, a group that is a superuser's now, or no group. A scope's rename moves the
record, its removal drops it, and
.B "group remove"
takes the group out of every lockdown; making a locked group a superuser group
.RB ( "group edit" " ... " tier " superuser, or a priv\-lvl edit that does it)"
is refused, and so is
.B "tacctl rollback"
to a release before 0.2.19, until the lockdown is lifted. The log has
.BI "emergency lockdown enable scope=" name " groups=" list " exempt=" users " reason=\(dq" text "\(dq by=" user " locked=" n " result=ok"
and
.BI "emergency lockdown disable scope=" name " groups=" list " held=" duration " by=" user " result=ok" .
The record is
.B emergency.lockdown.<scope>.groups|since|by|reason
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
so every snapshot holds it.
.TP
.BI "emergency lockdown " "scope " "enable " [ "group[,group]..." "] [\-\-tier " tier "] [\-\-reason " text "] [\-\-dry\-run]"
.TQ
.BI "emergency lockdown \-\-all\-scopes enable " [ "group[,group]..." "] [\-\-tier " tier "] [\-\-reason " text "] [\-\-dry\-run]"
Lock the groups named (with no list every group that is not a superuser's; with
\fB\-\-tier\fR the groups of that tier, readonly, operator or engineer) on the
scope, or on every scope, added to what is locked already. Shows the users who
lose access and those who keep it, then asks for the scope's name typed back (the
word \fBall\fR for \fB\-\-all\-scopes\fR, which includes this server's own logins, the scope
\fBtacctl\fR that tacctl manages for them: no command names it, its lines say
"this server's own login" and \fB\-\-all\-scopes disable\fR lifts it);
without a terminal the verb refuses. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR prints the list and changes
nothing. A superuser group, or \fB\-\-tier superuser\fR, is refused. The change is one
write of \fItacctl.yaml\fR, one render and one restart of each backend that changed;
a backend whose restart failed is reported as \fBNOT restarted\fR, the record and
the files are kept, and the verb exits 1. Superuser only.
.TP
.BI "emergency lockdown " "scope " "disable " [ "group[,group]..." ]
.TQ
.BI "emergency lockdown \-\-all\-scopes disable " [ "group[,group]..." ]
Lift the lockdown for the groups named, or all of it; the rendered files come back byte for byte. Superuser only.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command emergency lockdown
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-scopes\fR
(lockdown) Every scope instead of one.
.TP
\fB\-\-tier\fR \fI<tier>\fR
(lockdown) Lock the groups of that tier (readonly, operator or engineer).
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fI<text>\fR
(lockdown) Why, 1 to 256 characters; kept with the record and in the log.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
(lockdown) Print who loses access and change nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(lockdown status) Print the lockdowns as a JSON array.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command emergency lockdown
.TP
.BI "emergency lockdown status " [ scope "] [\-\-json]"
.TQ
.BI "emergency lockdown " scope
The lockdowns that stand: scope, groups, since, how long, by whom, why, the users locked out, the superusers who keep access and the ways a device's local accounts stay usable. \fB\-\-json\fR prints an array with \fBlocked_users\fR and \fBexempt\fR.
