saves it, forgets a cached password
.RB ( "password forgotten (console password\-cache off)" )
and stops caching; with no argument it also says whether a password is cached
now. When the tier is not allowed,
.B on
is refused in one sentence that names the fix and saves nothing. The words take no
name: the root side takes the user from the one
.BR sudo (8)
was run by, so nobody sets another user's choice. Every user of the operator
tier and up may run these three forms (the sudoers row names them exactly); the
other forms are the superuser's. The choice is stored in
.B settings.password_cache_users
and logged as
.BR "console set key=password\-cache item=user:" \fIname\fR.
.B "user rename"
moves it and
.B "user remove"
drops it, as for the system shell of a user. A plain
.B "tacctl shell \-\-password\-cache"
is an explicit request and needs only the permission of the tier.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console password-cache
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR. Every user of the operator tier and up may run it with no argument (the state) and with on or off (their own choice, which needs their tier to be allowed); tiers, idle, max and user need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console password-cache
.TP
.BI "console password\-cache user " "[ " name " [ clear ] ]"
Show who turned the cache on (each with the tier), a user's setting, or clear it
so that the cache is off for that user; a session the user has open keeps its
cache until it ends or they turn it off. The superuser's.
.TP
.BI "console password\-cache tiers " "[ " csv "|none ]"
Show or set the tiers that are allowed to keep the user's
network password in memory for the session, so that a device pull, a diff and
the
.B ssh
lines after it ask once
.RB ( operator ,
.BR engineer ,
.BR superuser ;
default
.BR none :
off for every tier). A listed tier is only allowed: each user turns the cache
on for themselves. The password lives only in the memory of the session's own
process, in a locked page of a process marked not dumpable, and in no file, log,
argument or environment; the agent is shut between lines. It is used for the
lines that run
.BR "device config pull" ,
.BR "device config diff \-\-pull" ,
.BR "device config plan \-\-pull" ,
.B ssh
or
.BR "device ssh" ,
and for no other (a
.B diff
or a
.B plan
without
.B \-\-pull
logs in to nothing). The shell hands such a line a token in the environment of
its
.B sudo
process and kills the token when the line ends; while the line runs the agent
gives the password once to
the user or to root with that token, and after that the token is good for
.B forget
only. A process of the same user that reads the line's environment and reaches
the socket before that one get can still obtain it. A pull that had
to prompt stores the password after the first device accepted it; a device that
refuses a cached password makes it forgotten at once. It is forgotten when the
session ends, after the idle time, after the maximum lifetime, when
.B passwd
changes the password, when the clock goes backwards, and with the shell's word
.BR "console forget" .
Root can read any process: the cache
protects the password against other users and against its landing in files and
logs.
.TP
.BI "console password\-cache idle " "[ " min " ]"
Show or set how long a cached password lives without a use, in minutes (1 to
120, default 15). Each use restarts it.
.TP
.BI "console password\-cache max " "[ " hours " ]"
Show or set the lifetime of a cached password from the moment it was cached, in
hours (1 to 24, default 8). Use does not extend it.
.TP
.B console forget
Forgets the cached password of this
.B "tacctl shell"
or console session now. It is a word of the shell and the console, handled in
the session's own process without sudo, and works while the cache is off (it
says so). Run as a command, outside a session, it only says where the cache is.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console forget
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console forget
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell " "[ " tier " { enable | disable } ]"
Show or set who may start a system shell from the console. With no argument it
lists each tier
.RB ( readonly ", " operator ", " engineer ", " superuser )
as
.B enabled
or
.BR disabled ,
then the users that have a setting of their own, with their tier. The
superuser tier is
.B "enabled (always)"
and cannot be disabled; readonly and operator are disabled by default; the
engineer tier is
.B "disabled (never)"
and
.B enable
is refused for it (a shell on the server would reach its secrets). A tier
name alone prints that tier's state. Setting a tier writes
.B settings.system_shell_tiers
(the superuser tier is always in the list). Those who have the system shell
may also open an editor from the console
.RB ( "scope block ... edit|adopt" ,
.BR "device block ... edit|adopt" ):
an editor can start a shell. Only a superuser sets it, for a tier, a user or
the path; an operator and an engineer may read each form (no argument, a tier
alone,
.BR "user " \fIname\fR
alone and
.BR path ),
and every form that sets something is refused for them (logged as a tier
denial). The change
is logged as
.BR "console set key=system\-shell item=" \fItier\fR
at
.BR auth.notice .
It applies from the next console login.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console system-shell
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR. An operator may read it (no argument, a tier, user <name>, path); setting it for a tier or a user, and setting the path, need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console system-shell
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell user " "name " "[ { enable | disable | clear } ]"
Show or set the system shell for one user, over the tier's setting in both
directions: enabled, it gives a user of a tier that has none the system shell;
disabled, it takes it from a user of a tier that has it. Without a value it
prints the user's effective state and why
.RB ( "user setting" ", " "tier operator" ", " always ", " never ).
.B clear
removes the user's setting, and the tier decides again. The user must exist.
An enable is for the tier the user has when it is set
.RB ( readonly
or
.BR operator ):
after a move to another tier it gives nothing and is listed as
.BR "stale: set for" " \fItier\fR" ", now " \fItier\fR
until it is set again (it counts again if the user returns to that tier); a
disable holds in any tier.
A superuser always has the system shell: disabling it is refused, and
enabling it stores nothing. An engineer never has it: enabling it is refused
(with the reason of the tier), and disabling stores nothing. The settings are
kept in
.B settings.system_shell_users
and are logged as
.BR "console set key=system\-shell item=user:" \fIname\fR.
.B "user rename"
moves the setting to the new name and
.B "user remove"
drops it (both say so and are logged as
.BR "console set" ),
so a user created later under the name does not inherit it, and neither does
.B "user add"
when a hand\-edited entry of the name is left. An entry for a name that is no
user is not honoured; it is listed with
.BR "no such user" ,
and
.B clear
removes it.
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell path " "[ " path " ]"
The system shell, default
.IR /bin/bash .
It must be an absolute path to an executable file listed in
.IR /etc/shells .
.TP
.B console install
Puts the server's pieces in place, as
.B host sync
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
does: the line
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
in
.IR /etc/shells ,
and sshd's drop-in
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/tacctl\-console.conf
for the members of
.BR tac\-console :
.B "ForceCommand /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console"
(every login, remote command and subsystem, sftp included, reaches the
console, which reads the client's command from
.BR SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND ),
.B "DisableForwarding yes"
(left out, with
.BR "AllowAgentForwarding yes" ,
when
.B console agent\-forwarding
is enabled; for the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
a block before it sets
.BR "DisableForwarding no" ,
.B "AllowTcpForwarding yes"
and
.BR "X11Forwarding yes" ),
.BR "AllowTcpForwarding no" ,
.BR "AllowStreamLocalForwarding no" ,
.BR "X11Forwarding no" ,
.BR "PermitTunnel no" ,
.BR "PermitTTY yes" ,
.B "PubkeyAuthentication no"
(a key login would bypass TACACS+),
.B ClientAliveInterval 300
and
.BR "ClientAliveCountMax 2" .
Each change of the drop-in is checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ;
when sshd refuses it the previous file comes back and the command exits 1,
otherwise sshd is reloaded
.RB ( "systemctl reload ssh.service" ,
or
.BR sshd.service ).
Each piece is reported
.BR Installed: ,
.B Updated:
or
.BR Unchanged: ,
then the check below runs. The engineer tier's own drop-in
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00\-tacctl\-engineer.conf
(every forwarding and tunnel closed for the members of
.BR tac\-engineer )
is written the same way, and by every
.B host sync
of the server even when the console is not installed; an earlier
.I tacctl\-engineer.conf
is removed. Refused when
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
is missing
.RB ( "tacctl upgrade" " makes it)."
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console install
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console install
.TP
.B console remove
Takes the console's drop-in (checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
sshd reloaded) and the
.I /etc/shells
line away; the engineer tier's drop-in stays (an earlier
.I tacctl\-engineer.conf
is renamed). Refused, nothing changed, while an account still has the console
as its login shell; they are named, with the way to give them
.I /bin/bash
back.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console remove
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console remove
.TP
.B console check
Whether sshd applies the console's settings: the drop-in present and this
release's, the
.B Include
of
.I sshd_config.d/*.conf
in
.IR /etc/ssh/sshd_config ,
and
.B "sshd \-T"
for the first account whose shell is the console. It also checks the
engineers (the drop-in of the engineer tier and that its name sorts before
the console's, a leftover
.IR tacctl\-engineer.conf ,
each member's shell) and names every member of
.B tac\-superuser
that is a tacctl account and not a superuser-tier tacctl user, a removed
user's account included (its UID is in tacctl's range; root is not named). A
member that is no tacctl account (a local administrator added by hand) is only
listed, and does not make the check fail. Exit 1, with the red
warning, when anything is off.
.PP
The console asks tacctl for its settings with the hidden
.BR _console\-policy ,
which the tiers sudoers rules grant to every tier.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console check
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console check
.SS Console sessions \fB(tacctl\-console)\fR
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
is a symbolic link to the tacctl binary; started under that name (as a login
shell,
.BR \-tacctl\-console )
tacctl is the console: the interactive shell
.RB ( "tacctl shell" )
with the prompt
.IB host "> "
(the server's short host name, or the name
.B "server name"
sets) and the banner
.IR "tacctl\~console on host \(em type 'help'. Devices: device list. This session is logged." ,
whose lines run as
.PP
.RS
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.BI TACCTL_CONSOLE= session
.B /usr/local/bin/tacctl
.I words
.RE
.PP
with the rule of
.B tacctl shell
for
.BR \-n .
The login environment is discarded except
.BR TERM ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_* ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ,
.B SSH_CLIENT
and
.BR SSH_TTY ;
.B PATH
is
.I /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
and
.B SHELL
the console. At the start it asks the root side for its settings
.RB ( "sudo \-n tacctl _console\-policy" );
without an answer the defaults apply (30 minutes idle, no system shell), and
only the read-only commands are listed. The answer also carries the caller's
tier, which Tab,
.BR ? ,
a typed space and
.B help
use to list only the commands that tier can run (see
.BR "tacctl shell" ).
The session ends after the idle timeout at the prompt
.RB ( "console idle\-timeout" ;
not while a line runs).
.PP
.BI "\-c " line
(the remote command sshd passes:
.BR "ssh " "\fIserver\fR " "'user list'" )
runs one tacctl line, with the connection as its standard input. A string
that is not one line beginning with a tacctl command or
.B help
\(em
.BR scp ,
.BR sftp ,
.BR rsync ,
any program, the shell's own words, an empty string \(em is refused with
.I "the tacctl\~console does not run programs; file transfer is not available"
and exit status 126, and nothing runs. Any other argument is refused the same
way
.RI ( "the tacctl\~console takes no options" ).
With standard input not a terminal and no
.BR \-c ,
the lines of the input run as a batch, as in
.BR "tacctl shell" .
A word that starts with an underscore at the prompt or in a batch
.RB ( __help ,
.BR __complete ,
.BR _console\-policy ,
the entry points of the scripts, the key and the console itself) is not a
command there: it is answered as any word that is no command, and nothing runs.
.PP
A user who must change the password
.RB ( "user require\-password\-change" ;
never a superuser) gets, at the start of the console, a notice (\fBA password
change is required of your account.\fR: no device accepts the login, and
nothing here runs until then but the change itself) and the password change
dialog, the line
.BR passwd ,
straight away. Until the password is changed nothing else runs: every other
line, the console's own words
.RB ( help ,
.BR history ,
.B system\-shell
and
.BR "console forget" )
included, is refused with
.B A password change is required: nothing runs here until it is made but 'passwd' (and 'exit').
and status 1, and Tab and ? list the change only (and
.B exit
and
.BR quit ,
which end the session). A line typed while the session is locked is not
entered in the history file, and Up, Down and Ctrl\-R recall no earlier line
until the lock lifts. The root side tells the console in its policy line
.RB ( password_change=yes ,
only for a caller who is held to it, and
.B password_change=unknown
when the store could not be read). When the policy line cannot be read at all
(sudo refuses, the answer is not a policy) or says
.BR unknown ,
it cannot be told whether a change is required, and a caller who is no
superuser is held the same way, with the notice
.B It could not be read whether a password change is required of your account
instead of the dialog (the next line asks again); a superuser, known by the
system group tac\-superuser, is never held by a read error. When the password is
changed the root side is asked once more and the session goes on as any other. Each line a locked session would
refuse asks the root side again first, so a session left locked by a password
changed in another one goes on as soon as the requirement is gone. The dialog is held to the idle time
like a prompt: one nobody answers is ended, and the session ends as an idle one.
Ctrl-C in the
dialog declines it and ends the session (status 130); any other failure (a
wrong current password, a new one that is too weak or differs from its
confirmation) leaves a prompt that is still locked, where
.B passwd
tries again. A line given with
.B \-c
and a batch are held the same way, with no dialog: a
.B passwd
line runs, any other is refused with status 1. The session is logged:
.BI "console password-change required|unknown|done|failed session=" id " user=" user " [status=" n ]\fR,
and each refused line
.BI "console DENY session=" id " user=" user " reason=password\-change first=" verb|\- " [len=" n ]
(auth.warning; the word is named under the rule of the other refusals). The
command line holds the caller to the change as well (see
.BR "user require\-password\-change" ).
.PP
.B system\-shell
(the console's own word; interactive sessions only) starts the user's system
shell
.RB ( "console system\-shell path" ,
default
.IR /bin/bash )
as the user, without arguments, with the console's environment and
.BR SHELL= \fIpath\fR;
the console's idle timer does not run meanwhile, and sshd's drop-in still
governs the connection. It is open to the tiers
.B "console system\-shell"
gives it (superusers always; the others by their tier or by a setting of their
own); otherwise it is refused with
.IR "system-shell is not available for the tier tier on this console. An administrator enables it with: tacctl console system-shell user name enable (or tier enable for the tier)"
(status 1). In a batch or through
.B \-c
it is refused with status 126.
.PP
Syslog (tag
.BR tacctl\-console ):
.BI "console start session=" id " user=" user " from=" address " tty=" tty " mode=" interactive|command|batch\fR,
.BI "console end session=" id " user=" user " reason=" exit|eof|idle|hangup|signal|command|failed|locked " lines=" n " status=" n\fR,
.BI "console DENY session=" id " user=" user " reason=command first=" verb|\- " [len=" n ]
(auth.warning; the word is the first word of the refused string when it is a
verb of tacctl or one of the console's own words, else
.B first=\-
and
.B len=
is the length of the string, so a password typed at the prompt is never in the
log),
.BI "console system\-shell start" \fR,
.B end
.RB ( status= ", " duration= )
and
.BR DENY .
Each line is also in sudo's log, and
.B tacctl ssh
logs
.BI console= id\fR.
.SH RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
render the configuration for logging in to a device against the RADIUS
backend when given
.BR "--protocol radius" ,
or without the flag for a scope that resolves to RADIUS (its auth-method,
else its only protocol;
.BR "scope auth-method" ).
The server address, the authentication and accounting ports (the
.B auth
and
.B acct
listeners shown by
.BR "server listen --backend radius show" )
and the scope's shared secret are filled in; the management ACL, the
exec timeout, the scope's
.B aaa-order
(the server first, or local first) and the local fallback behave as in
the TACACS+ output. Without the flag, and for a scope that does not
resolve to RADIUS, the output is the TACACS+ configuration.
.PP
Cisco output is a
.B "radius server"
block,
.B "aaa group server radius"
(named by
.BR "scope radius-group" ),
.BR "aaa authentication login" ,
.BR "aaa authorization exec"
(the privilege level comes from the reply's
.BR "Cisco-AVPair shell:priv-lvl=N" ,
which the scope must send: see
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" )
and
.BR "aaa accounting exec" ,
with the same
.B "privilege exec level"
mappings. Juniper output is
.BR "system radius-server" ,
.BR authentication-order ,
.B "system accounting destination radius"
and the template users that the reply's
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
selects, with the per-class allow-commands and deny-commands rules,
which stay in force because the class is local to the device.
.PP
RADIUS has no per-command authorization and no command accounting, and
tacctl's RADIUS accepts PAP only. Each RADIUS output lists under the
configuration what the Access-Accept carries for the device
.RB ( Service-Type
and the vendor's attribute, enabled for the scope or for tagged
addresses only) and the TACACS+ features that are not available
(rules set with
.B "group commands"
are not enforced by the server).
.PP
tacctl refuses, with an error and no output, when the configuration
would not work: the RADIUS backend is not enabled
.RB ( "backend enable radius" ),
the scope's
.B "scope protocols"
filter leaves out
.BR radius ,
the scope sends the vendor's attribute to none of its devices (neither
enabled with
.B "scope vendor-attrs"
nor an address tagged with
.BR "scope devices" ;
the error prints the command that enables it),
or the scope secret holds a character that IOS or Junos read as syntax
(whitespace, quotes, a backtick, a non-ASCII character, or any of
.BR "? ! # $ \\e ; { } [ ] | & < > , * ( )" ).
Letters, digits and
.B ". _ + / = : @ % ^ ~ -"
paste unquoted, and
.B "scope secret generate"
makes such a secret. The secret is shared with TACACS+, so changing it
means changing it on every device of the scope. A secret longer than 63
characters renders with a warning. A listener bound to one IPv4 address
supplies the server address; an IPv6 or loopback bind is warned about.
Operator overrides
.IR cisco-radius.template ,
.I juniper-radius.template
and
.I wti-radius.template
in the templates directory work as for the TACACS+ templates; see
.BR FILES .
.SH TIERS
A user of tacctl who has a local account on the tacctl server (a member of
.BR tac\-users )
reaches tacctl through
.BR sudo (8)
and is held to a tier: by tacctl itself, which checks the caller before every
command, and by the rules of
.BR "config sudoers tiers" ,
which are written from the same table. A group's tier is the one set on it
.RB ( "group edit " \fIgroup\fR " tier " \fItier\fR ,
kept as
.BI tier. group
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ),
else the band of its priv-lvl: 15 and up
.BR superuser ,
7 to 14
.BR operator ,
below 7
.BR readonly .
The fourth tier,
.BR engineer ,
sits between operator and superuser and is only ever set on a group. A user
that is unknown or disabled, or whose group has no priv-lvl, has no tier and
may run nothing. A group at priv-lvl 15 or more that has no tier setting and is
not the built-in
.B superuser
group holds its members at the operator tier until a
superuser who is not a member sets it again; so does a
.I tacctl.yaml
that cannot be read, for every managed caller. Root, and a local administrator
who is no tacctl user, are not held to a tier.
.PP
A tier may run what the tiers below it may. Under each tier below are the verbs
for which it is the lowest; everything not listed (users, groups, scopes and
the change of any secret, backends, backups and their restore, upgrades and
every global setting) is the superuser's. The shell and the console list only
the verbs the caller's tier can run. Each entry of COMMANDS names its tier on a
.B Requires
line.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: tiers
.TP
.B readonly
(no arguments), passwd, status, version, help, \-h, \-\-help,
user list, user show, group list, group show, scope list,
backend list, backend status, ssh, device list, device show,
device notice, device ssh, device client\-config, _completion\-names,
_console\-policy, \-\-version, \-v, hash, completion, man
.TP
.B operator
log tail, log search, log failures, log accounting, config validate,
backup list, device check, device scan, device discover,
device export, device config, console show, console check,
console system\-shell, console password\-cache, console forget
.TP
.B engineer
device add, device remove, device rename, device address,
device hostname, device vendor, device port, device description,
device location, device legacy\-ssh, device ios\-syntax,
device hostkey, device import, scope devices, device config,
device enroll, device collect, device onboard, device sync,
device support\-bundle, config cisco, config juniper, config wti,
host list, host show, scope staging, scope secret, scope snmp,
scope mgmt\-acl, device snmp, scope block, device block, scope show,
breakglass list
.TP
.B superuser
Every verb not listed above, and every verb of the tiers below.
.PP
The gate sees two words, so a verb that the code splits further is listed at its
two\-word level and then limited by the code:
.TP
.B breakglass list
An engineer lists the holds of the devices of their own scopes only; hold and release need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B config cisco
An engineer may print the walkthrough and read source\-interface for a scope of their own; setting or unsetting source\-interface needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B console password\-cache
Every user of the operator tier and up may run it with no argument (the state) and with on or off (their own choice, which needs their tier to be allowed); tiers, idle, max and user need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B console system\-shell
An operator may read it (no argument, a tier, user <name>, path); setting it for a tier or a user, and setting the path, need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device block
An engineer may read the configuration blocks of a device of their own scope (list, show [\-\-effective] [\-\-reveal], check); set, remove, edit and adopt need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device config
An operator may run list; show, pull, diff, plan and apply need the engineer tier (a device's walkthrough carries its scope's secret, a plan can type it, a pull logs in to the device as you, and a check or an apply writes to it as you), and an engineer gets the devices of their own scopes only; forget and shapes (shapes files what an apply confirmed) need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device import
An engineer imports from standard input only (device import \-).
.TP
.B device list
The CONFIG column (and config, release, model and serial of \-\-json) is the operator tier's; a read\-only user's list has none.
.TP
.B device notice
Every tier may run list; ack and unack need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device show
The Configuration, Release, Model and Serial rows (and config, release, model and serial of \-\-json) are the operator tier's; a read\-only user's output has none.
.TP
.B device snmp
An engineer may read the settings of a device of their own scope (show [\-\-reveal [<user>]], version, port, timeout, v3\-user list|default, clients list); every setter and clear needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B host show
An engineer reads what tacctl recorded of a host; \-\-check logs in to it and needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope block
An engineer may read the configuration blocks of a scope of their own (list, show [\-\-reveal], check); set, remove, edit and adopt need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope mgmt\-acl
An engineer may read the management filter mode of a scope of their own (mode, with no value); the permit list, the ACL and filter names and setting the mode need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope secret
An engineer may run show for a scope of their own; set and generate need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope snmp
An engineer may read the settings of a scope of their own (show [\-\-reveal [<user>]], version, port, timeout, contact, v3\-user list|default, clients list); every setter, clear and test needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope staging
An engineer may run list for a scope of their own; the other verbs need the superuser tier.
.\" END GENERATED: tiers
.PP
An engineer's verbs reach only the devices, hosts and secrets of the scopes
the engineer is a member of; another scope's device is not found, and a change
that would touch one is refused. Each read of a secret by an engineer is
logged to syslog (never the value).
.SH AUDIT LOG
Every line tacctl writes to the log goes to syslog through
.BR logger (1),
facility
.BR auth ,
tag
.B tacctl
(the login console's own lines: tag
.BR tacctl\-console ).
tacctl keeps no log of its own; where the lines go from there is the site's
concern.
.PP
A command that changes state, or logs in somewhere every time it runs, writes
two central lines, whatever else it logs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cmd start v=1 user=alice uid=1001 origin=sudo tier=superuser tty=pts/3 console=\- cmd="user add" args="dave ops \-\-hash <redacted>" sid=k3x9a0bq
cmd end v=1 user=alice cmd="user add" result=ok status=0 duration=0.312s snapshot=20261021_093015_412 sid=k3x9a0bq
.RE
.fi
.PP
so that
.B "grep 'cmd end'"
lists every transaction. Every command has a class in a table in the code:
.TP
.B action
It changes state or logs in somewhere every time it runs. The start line is
written before the command does anything, after the tier gate and the
preflight; the end line where the exit status is decided.
.TP
.B mixed
It is a read or a write by its later arguments
.RB ( "scope secret" " shows or sets, " "device port" " <name> shows and"
.B "device port"
<name> 2222 sets). The start line is written just before the first thing the
command changes, so a mixed command that only read writes nothing.
.TP
.B read
No central line: lists, shows, checks, scans, the completion helpers,
.B hash
and
.BR shell ,
which runs each line as its own tacctl process.
.PP
A command that is not in the table is treated as an action, and the test suite
fails until its row is added. The lines a verb writes of its own
.RB ( "ssh start" ", " "ssh end" ", the console session, " "breakglass hold" ", "
.BR "host enroll" ", " "device config apply" ", ...)"
are kept; the pair is written for those verbs too. The commands of each class
are:
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: audit-classes
.TP
.B "action"
_phase, backend disable, backend enable, backup unkeep,
breakglass hold, breakglass release, console install, console remove,
device add, device enroll, device notice, device remove,
device rename, device ssh, device support\-bundle, emergency lockdown,
group add, group edit, group remove, host enroll, host forget,
host move, host rename, host sync, host unenroll, passwd, scope add,
scope remove, scope rename, server addresses, ssh, user add,
user disable, user enable, user move, user passwd, user remove,
user rename, user require\-password\-change
.TP
.B "action, auth.notice"
backup restore, config restore,
group edit (the field is tier or priv\-lvl), install, log clear,
store rollback, uninstall, upgrade
.TP
.B "mixed"
backup keep, backup recover, config allow, config bcrypt\-cost,
config branch, config cisco, config deny, config devices,
config juniper, config linux, config loglevel, config mgmt\-acl,
config password\-age, config password\-min\-length, config render,
config secret\-min\-length, config snmp, config validate, config wti,
console agent\-forwarding, console color, console forwarding,
console idle\-timeout, console list\-max, console password\-cache,
console space\-completion, console ssh\-escape, device address,
device block, device collect, device config, device description,
device hostkey, device hostname, device import, device ios\-syntax,
device legacy\-ssh, device location, device netconf, device onboard,
device port, device snmp, device stale\-days, device sync,
device timeout\-scale, device vendor, group commands, group junos,
group preset, group privilege, group reset, host default\-method,
host provisioner, host target, scope aaa\-order, scope auth\-method,
scope block, scope breakglass, scope default, scope devices,
scope exec\-timeout, scope mgmt\-acl, scope netconf, scope prefixes,
scope protocols, scope radius\-group, scope secret, scope snmp,
scope staging, scope tacacs\-group, scope timeout\-scale,
scope vendor\-attrs, server listen, server metrics, server name,
user email, user scope
.TP
.B "mixed, auth.notice"
config audit\-strict, config sudoers, console system\-shell, rollback,
store import
.\" END GENERATED: audit-classes
.PP
The fields, in the order written:
.TP
.B v
The format version of the central lines
.RB ( 1 ).
A collector pins itself to it. The older per\-verb lines carry no
.BR v .
.TP
.BR user ", " uid ", " origin
Who ran the command:
.B SUDO_USER
and
.BR SUDO_UID ,
checked against the passwd database before any command runs, with
.BR origin=sudo .
A process with no
.B SUDO_USER
(a root shell, a script run as root) is
.BR "user=root uid=0 origin=root" :
the one case where the log holds no login identity, as sudo's own log holds
none.
.TP
.B tier
The tier the gate held the caller to
.RB ( unrestricted
for root and for a local administrator who is no tacctl user).
.TP
.BR tty ", " console
The terminal the command runs on, and the login\-console session it was typed
in;
.B \-
for none.
.TP
.BR cmd ", " args
The command's words as the tier gate sees them
.RB ( "user add" ", " "scope secret" ", " "device config" ),
and the arguments after them, each word quoted and cut at 64 bytes, the whole
at 512.
.TP
.BR result ", " status
How the command ended and its exit status:
.B ok
(0);
.B failed
(any status that is not one of the next three);
.B denied
(a verb refused the caller);
.B interrupted
(130: Ctrl\-C, SIGTERM or a hangup);
.B aborted
(status 2, written when tacctl's own code panicked).
.TP
.B duration
Seconds with millisecond precision, from the start line to the end line.
.TP
.B snapshot
The id of the pre\-change snapshot or snapshots the command took, comma\-separated
when it took more than one
.RB ( \- 
when none was taken or the newest was current).
.TP
.B reason
On a
.B "cmd DENY"
line:
.B superuser
or
.BR scope .
.TP
.B sid
The session id, last on every line.
.TP
.B class
.B class=unexpected
on a start line means a command classified as a read reached a write: a fault
in the class table, never expected.
.TP
.B mode
On the review, approval and apply lines of
.B "device config apply"
and on the approval line of
.BR "device onboard" :
.B attended
when the device's name was typed back,
.B scripted
when
.B "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes"
answered the review.
.PP
A value is written as it is when it holds only letters, digits and
.BR ". _ : / @ + , \-"
(a comma, so that a list of scopes or snapshot ids stays one word);
otherwise it is double\-quoted with Go's escapes, so a line is always one line
and a collector can split on spaces outside quotes. An empty value is
.BR \- .
.PP
Priorities:
.B auth.info
for an action;
.B auth.notice
for the weighty ones (marked in the list above);
.B auth.warning
for every refusal and for an end line whose result is not
.BR ok .
.SS Detail lines
The central pair says who ran which command and how it ended. A verb that
changes something also writes a line of its own beside it, saying what it
changed: the object and, for a setting, the value before and after
.RB ( from= " and " to= ,
.B \-
for none, a list comma\-separated). It is written once the change is made, so it
sits between
.B "cmd start"
and
.BR "cmd end" ;
except for the verbs named after the list, a change that failed has no detail
line and the end line says
.BR result=failed .
It has the priority of the command's class, ends in
.BI by= user
and the
.BR sid ,
and holds a name or a count, never a secret, a hash or a password. The events
and their keys, in the order written:
.TP
.B "user add"
name, group, scopes.
.TP
.B "user remove, user disable, user enable"
name.
.TP
.B "user passwd"
name, source (prompt, hash or generated).
.TP
.B "user rename"
from, to, uid (carried or \-).
.IP "\fBuser password\-change\fR"
name, state (required or cleared), via (require, add, passwd or self): a requirement to change the password set or lifted, for whom (the actor is by=).
.TP
.B "user move"
name, from, to (the groups).
.TP
.B "user scope"
name, op (add, remove or replace), scopes.
.TP
.B "group add"
name, priv\-lvl, tier.
.TP
.B "group remove"
name.
.TP
.B "group edit"
name, key (priv\-lvl, tier, wti\-level or junos\-class), from, to. One line for each setting changed, so a priv\-lvl change that also records or drops the tier writes a tier line too; priv\-lvl and tier at auth.notice.
.TP
.B "group commands"
name, op (default, add, remove or seed), rule, action, count.
.TP
.B "group privilege"
name, op (add, remove or seed), count.
.TP
.B "group preset"
name, preset (roles), changes; one line for each group changed.
.TP
.B "config sudoers, config sudoers tiers"
op (install or remove), file, and group for the first.
.IP "\fBconsole set\fR"
key, item, from, to. key is the setting (tiers, user, system\-shell, forwarding, agent\-forwarding, ssh\-escape, password\-cache, idle\-timeout, space\-completion, color, list\-max) and item the tier, user or part of it; auth.notice for tiers, user and system\-shell.
.TP
.B "backup restore"
id, pre (the snapshot taken first), keep\-uids, legacy; auth.notice.
.TP
.B "store import"
source, replace, force; auth.notice.
.TP
.B "store rollback"
pre; auth.notice.
.TP
.B "install"
branch; auth.notice.
.TP
.B "upgrade"
from, to (commits), branch; auth.notice.
.TP
.B "uninstall"
backups and logs (kept or removed); auth.notice.
.TP
.B "scope add"
name, prefixes, default.
.TP
.B "scope remove"
name, users.
.TP
.B "scope rename"
from, to, users, hosts.
.TP
.B "scope default"
from, to.
.TP
.B "scope prefixes"
op (add, remove, move or remove\-all), scope, cidr, and to for a move.
.TP
.B "scope protocols, scope vendor\-attrs, scope auth\-method, scope aaa\-order, scope exec\-timeout, scope tacacs\-group, scope radius\-group, scope netconf"
scope, from, to.
.TP
.B "scope devices"
scope, cidr, from, to (the vendor tag).
.TP
.B "scope mgmt\-acl"
scope, op (add, remove, clear, cisco\-name or juniper\-name), value.
.TP
.B "scope secret set"
scope, source (set or generate).
.TP
.B "snmp set"
kind (community, v3\-user, clear, version, port, timeout, clients or contact), and scope or device; from and to for config snmp port and timeout.
.TP
.B "secret\-read"
kind, name. A secret was shown (kind scope, snmp, snmp\-device, snmp\-default, block, block\-adopt, store, config, snapshot or backup); written for every caller, a superuser's too.
.TP
.B "secret\-export"
scope, file. config linux script wrote a file that holds the scope's secret.
.TP
.B "config allow, config deny, config mgmt\-acl"
op (add, remove, clear, cisco\-name or juniper\-name), value.
.TP
.B "config password\-age, config bcrypt\-cost, config password\-min\-length, config secret\-min\-length, config loglevel, config branch, server metrics, server name"
from, to.
.TP
.B "server listen"
backend, listener, from, to.
.TP
.B "server addresses"
op (add or replace), address.
.TP
.B "config devices"
key, from, to.
.TP
.B "config audit\-strict"
from, to (on or off); auth.notice.
.TP
.B "config cisco source\-interface"
scope, from, to.
.TP
.B "config render"
backends, force.
.TP
.B "config linux uid"
user, uid.
.TP
.B "backend enable, backend disable"
id.
.TP
.B "host sync"
name, target, created, updated, renamed, removed, remove\-home, result, and reason when it failed; one line for each host.
.TP
.B "host move"
scope, moved, failed (a move of every drifted host).
.TP
.B "device add"
name, address, vendor, count.
.TP
.B "device remove"
names (the first 20, then +n), count.
.TP
.B "device rename"
from, to, count, and reason=device\-name when
.B "device enroll"
took over the name the device is configured with.
.TP
.B "device enroll hostkey, device collect hostkey, device onboard hostkey"
user (the person, in place of by=), addr, device, alg, fp, result (accepted,
skipped or refused), source (confirmed or flag); every answer to a host key's question, before the key is
pinned or used; auth.notice.
.TP
.B "device enroll"
user (in place of by=), addr, device, vendor, source (snmp, given or asked),
result; one line for each address at the end of the run.
.TP
.B "device import"
source, added, updated, removed, count.
.IP "\fBdevice set\fR"
name, field, from, to. field is address, hostname, vendor, port, description, location, legacy\-ssh, ios\-syntax, netconf, stale\-days (no name) or notice:<kind>.
.PP
The verbs that logged before 0.2.17 keep their lines
.RB ( "host enroll" ", " "host unenroll" ", " "host rename" ", " "host forget" ", " "host target" ", "
.BR "breakglass hold" ", " release ", " remove\-record
and
.BR hold\-move ", " rollback ", " "device sync" ", " block\-set ", " block\-edit ", " block\-adopt
and
.BR block\-remove ", " "scope breakglass" ", " "scope staging" ", " "group reset" ", "
.BR "device hostkey" ", " "device config forget" ", " "linux engineer\-sudo" ", "
.B "console provision"
and
.BR deprovision )
and gain
.B result=ok
at the end of a change that was made. A change that failed is written too, at
.BR auth.warning ,
with
.B "result=failed reason=word"
in its place; the word is the class of the error, never its text:
.B script
(the client script failed on the host),
.B store
(the snapshot or the write of the store, the registry or tacctl.yaml failed),
.B race
(another command changed the name or the record meanwhile),
.B lock
(another command holds the host),
.B write
(a file could not be written),
.BR ssh " and " sshd
(the connection or sshd's check failed) and
.BR interrupted .
.B "host unenroll"
also says
.BR force=yes|no .
The failed
.B "breakglass hold"
line does not repeat the text typed after
.BR \-\-reason :
.B reason=
there is the word.
.B "device sync"
gains
.B names
(the first 20 devices written, then +n) and
.BR result .
.B passwd
says
.BR source=self ,
and the lines that had no actor
.RB ( "uid\-map renumbered" ", " "uid\-range set" ,
the two
.B hostkey\-mismatch
lines, and
.BR "console policy ... unusable" )
end in
.BI by= user \fR;\fP
.B "scope staging done"
stays without an actor, as the sweep that writes it has none.
.PP
.B "device config apply"
writes a review line for each device asked,
.BR "device config apply review user= device= shape= answer=approved|declined mode=" ,
then, when any device was approved, the approval line
.BR "device config apply user= approved=<n> shapes= canary= mode=" ,
and each applied device's line
.BR "device config apply user= device= id= shape= sections= result= commit= until= mode=" ,
at
.BR auth.notice .
.B mode
is
.B attended
for the names typed back and
.B scripted
for
.BR "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes" ;
nothing typed at the review is written. A review cut short by Ctrl\-C writes
no approval line but
.BR "device config apply review user= result=interrupted applied=0 mode=" .
.B "device onboard"
says
.B mode=attended
on its approval line.
.PP
.B "device enroll"
writes a
.B "device enroll hostkey"
line for every answer to a host key's question, before the key is pinned or
used:
.B result=accepted
(the name typed back, or
.BR \-\-host\-key ),
.B skipped
(Enter, or the end of input) or
.B refused
(an answer that is not the device's name, a name the rules refuse, a
.B \-\-host\-key
the device does not offer);
.B source=confirmed
for the name typed at the terminal,
.B flag
for
.BR \-\-host\-key .
.B device=
is the name shown or given,
.B \-
before one is known; what was typed is never written, and a fingerprint is a
public value. At the end it writes one
.B "device enroll"
line per address, whose
.B source=
is where the vendor came from and whose
.B result=
is how far the address went
.RB ( refused ", " skipped ", " failed ", " not\-tried ", " registered ,
.BR registered\-other ", " pull\-failed ", " planned ", " applied
or
.BR declined ).
Between them come the
.B "device add"
line of each registration, the pull's and the plan's lines, a
.B "device rename"
line with
.B reason=device\-name
and the apply's lines with
.B mode=attended
when the review is run.
.B "device collect"
and
.B "device onboard"
ask the same question before their login and write the same line as
.B "device collect hostkey"
and
.BR "device onboard hostkey" ;
for
.BR collect ,
.B device=
is the SNMP name when it is the answer
.RB ( \-\-register ),
on a refusal too, and
.B \-
when the answer is the address or the address was skipped.
.SS Refusals
The tier gate's refusals keep their words and carry the session id
.RB ( "tier DENY user=carol tier=readonly cmd=user remove sid=k3x9a0bq" ).
A caller who must change the password is refused every command but
.BR passwd ,
.B version
and
.B help
right after the gate, with
.BR "password\-change DENY user=bob tier=operator cmd=\(dquser list\(dq" ,
at
.BR auth.warning ,
which names the two words the gate sees and never an argument, and ends with
.B state=unknown
when the store could not be read and the caller is not known to be a superuser
(see
.BR "user require\-password\-change" ).
A verb the gate lets through can still refuse: an engineer naming a scope's
secret to change, or another scope's device. That is the third central line, at
.BR auth.warning :
.PP
.nf
.RS
cmd DENY v=1 user=bob uid=1002 tier=engineer cmd="scope secret" args="lab set <redacted>" reason=superuser sid=k3x9a0bq
.RE
.fi
.PP
with
.B reason=superuser
(the verb is the superuser's) or
.B reason=scope
(the thing named is not in the caller's scopes). The message and the exit
status are the verb's own. If the command had written its start line,
.B "cmd end"
follows with
.BR result=denied .
A collector matches
.B DENY
for both kinds. The gate's own refusal ends the command before it starts, so it
has no end line.
.SS Secrets on the command line
.B args=
writes
.B <redacted>
for the value of a flag that carries a secret or a password hash
.RB ( \-\-hash
of
.B "user add"
and
.BR "user passwd" ,
.B \-\-secret
of
.BR "scope add" )
and for the value in
.BR "scope secret <scope> set <value>" ;
the flag
.B \-\-stdin
of
.B "scope secret"
is not redacted, and the
.B \-
of
.B "\-\-hash \-"
is.
A word typed where the command asks for a secret at a prompt or on standard
input is redacted too (a switch given a value, such as
.BR \-\-stdin=x ,
and a word that starts with
.B \-
and is no flag of the command included). The actions
.BR "user add" ,
.BR "user passwd" ,
.B "scope add"
and
.B passwd
write the start line before they refuse such a line; the SNMP verbs and
.B "scope secret"
are mixed and refuse it without a line, but an engineer's refusal inside the
verb writes
.B "cmd DENY"
with the same
.BR args= .
Every word after the arguments of
.B "user add"
and
.BR "user passwd" ,
of
.B "scope add"
and of
.BR "scope snmp" ", " "device snmp"
and
.B "config snmp"
.RB ( community " and " "v3\-user add" ),
every word of
.B passwd
and
.BR hash ,
and every word of
.B "scope secret"
but the scope, the sub-command and
.BR \-\-stdin ,
and a lone word after it that is no sub-command. A secret typed in the place
of a name
.RB ( "user passwd <password>" )
is logged as the name. Nothing else is redacted: free text such as
.B "breakglass hold \-\-reason"
is why a device was taken out of AAA, and it is logged as typed, as are the
arguments after
.B \-\-
of
.BR ssh .
Whatever is typed on a command line is also in sudo's own log, which records the
whole line, and
.BR ps (1)
shows it while the command runs; the forms that keep a value off the command
line are
.B "scope secret <scope> set"
(a prompt, or
.BR \-\-stdin ),
.B "user add"
and
.B "user passwd"
with
.B "\-\-hash \-"
(the hash on standard input), and the SNMP verbs. Communities, SNMPv3 keys and
passwords are read from a prompt or
standard input and are never written to a log; the login console's refusal logs
the first word of a string only when it is a verb.
.SS Correlating
Every line of one command, the detail lines of the verb and the tier gate's
included, ends in the same
.BI sid= id
(8 characters of a\-z and 0\-9, made for each run). The
.B snapshot=
of the end line names the snapshot taken before the change (snapshots, comma\-separated,
when the command took more than one)
.RB ( "backup diff" " shows it against the live state). " "tacctl ssh"
and the login console keep their own
.B session=
beside it. The id is for searching, not a key: two commands may share one, and
the timestamps tell them apart.
.PP
A start line with no end line means the command did not finish: SIGKILL, a
power loss, a crash of the process. The snapshot named by the command's lines
holds the state before it, and
.B "config validate"
says what the files are now. One
.B sid=
holds a whole
.BR "device enroll" :
the acceptance of each key, the registration, the pull, the rename, the plan
and, when the review was run, the apply of each device; the end line names the
run's one snapshot. An
.B upgrade
that replaces tacctl with the new version and runs again ends its first pair at
the hand-over; the second run writes its own pair, with its own
.BR sid .
.SS When logger fails
A signal that ends a command (Ctrl\-C, SIGTERM, a hangup) does not drop its
lines. When
.B logger
cannot be started (it is in
.IR util\-linux ;
.B install
and
.B upgrade
warn when it is missing) or exits with an error (the syslog socket refused),
the command prints once on standard error
.B "[WARN] The audit line was not written (logger: reason); the command continues."
and goes on with its own status; the lines it could not write are lost.
.SS audit.strict
With
.B "audit.strict: true"
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(default
.BR false ;
set with
.BR "config audit-strict on|off" ;
see
.BR "CONFIGURATION KEYS" )
a command that changes state is refused when its
.B "cmd start"
line cannot be written, before it changes anything:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[ERROR] The audit line cannot be written (logger: reason) and audit.strict is set; nothing was changed.
.RE
.fi
.PP
and the status is 1. This holds for an action command and for a mixed command
at the first thing it would change; a command that only reads runs as before.
The warning above is not printed beside the refusal. An end line that cannot be
written is that warning and nothing more: the command has already run. The
refusal comes before the first write, which for a few commands is after a
prompt or a read (\fBdevice collect\fR: after its first login;
\fBdevice config apply\fR: after the choice of devices and the password
prompt); \fBdevice config apply \-\-check\fR writes the central pair too.
The acceptance of a host key (\fBdevice enroll\fR, \fBdevice collect\fR,
\fBdevice onboard\fR) writes its \fBhostkey\fR line before the key is
used; a line that cannot be written refuses that device (\fBonboard\fR: the
run) before its key is pinned or anything is sent to it.
.PP
A command that shows a secret writes its
.B secret-read
line before it prints anything; when that line cannot be written the command
is refused, whatever the caller's tier, before any secret is printed:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[ERROR] The audit line cannot be written (logger: reason) and audit.strict is set; nothing was shown.
.RE
.fi
.PP
and the status is 1. These are the commands that show a secret
(\fBscope secret show\fR, \fBconfig cisco\fR, \fBjuniper\fR and \fBwti\fR,
\fBdevice config show\fR, \fBdevice config plan\fR unless
\fB\-\-no\-secrets\fR, the \fB\-\-reveal\fR of \fBscope snmp\fR,
\fBdevice snmp\fR and \fBconfig snmp show\fR, \fBscope block\fR and
\fBdevice block show \-\-reveal\fR, their \fBedit\fR and \fBadopt\fR,
\fBstore show\fR, \fBconfig dump\fR, \fBbackup diff\fR, and the diff of
\fBbackup restore\fR and \fBconfig restore\fR).
.B "config linux script"
writes its
.B secret-export
line before the script, which holds the scope's secret, and is refused the
same way before the file is written. The \fBdevice config\fR verbs that build
a device's expected configuration only to compare it or with the secrets
elided (\fBpull\fR, \fBdiff\fR, \fBapply\fR, \fBlist\fR,
\fBplan \-\-no\-secrets\fR; \fBdevice collect\fR and \fBonboard\fR too)
write a
.B secret-read
line for the secrets it carries but are not refused for it: a line that
cannot be written is the warning. Those that change something are refused by
their
.B "cmd start"
line as above;
.B "scope secret generate"
writes its start line before it prints the value it generated.
.PP
.B "config audit\-strict"
is never refused by
.BR audit.strict :
when
.B logger
is down and every change is refused,
.B "tacctl config audit\-strict off"
turns the setting off (with the warning that its own lines were not written).
.B "rollback 0.2.16 \-\-apply"
removes the key, which 0.2.16 refuses, and its dry run warns when it is on:
0.2.16 runs a command whose audit line cannot be written.
.SS Forwarding
The facility is
.BR auth ;
the program names are
.B tacctl
and
.BR tacctl\-console .
A collector matches the program name. With rsyslog, in
.IR /etc/rsyslog.d/tacctl.conf :
.PP
.nf
.RS
if $programname == 'tacctl' or $programname == 'tacctl\-console' then @@loghost.example.net:514
.RE
.fi
.PP
With journald,
.B "journalctl \-t tacctl \-t tacctl\-console"
reads them. Keep the log where an operator who runs tacctl cannot rewrite it.
.SH CONVENTIONS
The following patterns apply uniformly across subcommand families.
.TP
.B No arguments
Dispatcher commands
.RB ( user ,
.BR group ,
.BR config ,
.BR log ,
.BR backup ,
.BR hash ,
and every nested dispatcher such as
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope aaa-order" ,
.BR "scope exec-timeout" ,
.BR "scope tacacs-group" ,
.BR "scope radius-group" ,
.BR "scope auth-method" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ,
.BR "scope devices" ,
.BR "scope mgmt-acl" ,
.BR "scope breakglass" ,
.BR "scope block" ,
.BR "user scope" ,
or
.BR "group privilege" )
print their own usage and return without side effects.
Scalar getter/setters
.RB ( "config loglevel" ,
.BR "server listen" ,
.BR "server metrics" ,
.BR "config password-age" ,
.BR "config bcrypt-cost" ,
.BR "config password-min-length" ,
.BR "config secret-min-length" ,
.BR "config audit-strict" ,
.BR "config branch" ,
.BR "scope default" )
print the current value when called with no arguments.
.TP
.B Help
.B \-h
or
.B \-\-help
as the last word after the words of a command
.RB ( "tacctl user \-\-help" ,
.BR "tacctl user add \-h" ,
.BR "tacctl device config pull \-\-help" )
prints the help of that command: the text
.B ?
prints in the shell and the console, from the same code. A command with
sub-commands lists each with its arguments and description; any other prints
its usage lines, its options with their values and descriptions, and what comes
next (\(lqNext: <username> <group>\(rq). It exits 0, runs as the invoking user
(no sudo, no password) and lists no live names. On a terminal the help is in
colour; piped, with
.B NO_COLOR
set or with
.B TACCTL_COLOR=never
it is plain. No verb has a flag of its own named
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-help .
The word is a value after
.B \-\-
or as the value of a flag, and
.B tacctl \-\-help
alone is the top-level usage.
.TP
.B Multi-item input
Every
.B add
/
.B remove
subcommand that takes a CIDR or a Cisco exec command accepts either a
single value or a comma-separated list
.RI ( "cidr1,cidr2,..." ).
Every input is validated first; a bad entry aborts the operation
without writing anything.
.TP
.B CIDR semantics
Every CIDR-list subcommand
.RB ( "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ,
.BR "config mgmt-acl" )
canonicalizes input before storage:
.B 10.1.5.5/24
becomes
.BR 10.1.5.0/24 ;
.B 2001:DB8::/32
becomes
.BR 2001:db8::/32 .
Exact duplicates (after canonicalization) are rejected as no-ops on
.BR add .
Overlapping CIDRs of different prefix lengths coexist
.RI ( "10.0.0.0/8 and 10.99.0.0/16 can both be present" ).
Stored order is by broadcast-address ascending (IPv4 before IPv6):
disjoint ranges sort by their end address, and an overlapping subnet
falls immediately above its containing supernet.
.TP
.B Scope prefix invariants
Every CIDR (after canonicalization) belongs to
.B exactly one
scope. Adding a prefix already owned by a different scope is rejected
with a message naming the owning scope; remove it
from the owning scope first with
.B "scope prefixes " \fIowner\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR
before re-adding it elsewhere. Overlapping prefixes
.I across
scopes are allowed and routed to the narrowest scope that contains the client:
a scope with
.B 10.5.0.0/16
coexists with a scope holding the supernet
.BR 10.0.0.0/8 .
.PP
tacctl emits one
.B secrets:
entry per (scope, prefix) pair, sorted globally by prefix specificity
(v4 before v6; smaller broadcast first) after every mutation; a scope with N
prefixes becomes N entries that share
.BR name: " and " secret.key: .
.BR "scope list" ", " "scope show" " and " "config show"
present one entry per scope.
.PP
Do not hand-edit the
.B secrets:
block under multi-prefix scopes: the same-name entries must carry
identical
.B secret.key
values.
.B config validate
reports any divergence.
.TP
.B "clear and remove \-\-all"
.B clear
and
.B "remove \-\-all"
subcommands always prompt with
.B [y/N]
and print a warning about the resulting posture (for example, "no
clients can connect" or "fails open").
.TP
.B "Filters and opt-ins"
A setting that narrows something down is a filter: its verbs are
.B set
and
.BR clear ,
and empty (cleared) means everything
.RB ( "scope protocols" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ).
A setting that turns something on is an opt-in: its verbs are
.B enable
and
.BR disable ,
and nothing is on until it is enabled
.RB ( "backend enable|disable" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ).
An opt-in has no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none .
.TP
.B "Membership lists"
A list that names exactly what is in is a membership list, and empty
means nothing, not everything
.RB ( "user scope" ,
the scopes a user can authenticate from: none means nowhere;
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
the clients a scope serves: emptying it removes the scope).
Its verbs are
.B add
and
.BR remove :
.B "remove \-\-all"
empties it, and
.B "user scope"
replaces its whole list with
.BR replace .
.TP
.B Service restart
Changes to the store, and to the
.I tacctl.yaml
settings a backend renders (command rules, listeners,
.BR backends.* ),
re-render every enabled backend and restart the backends whose files
changed. Settings only device configurations read (mgmt-acl permits and
names, per-group priv-exec mappings, per-scope AAA order, exec timeout and
group labels) and tacctl's own tunables (bcrypt cost, password age, \&...)
restart nothing.
.TP
.B Prompts
Every confirmation prompt cancels, with a message, when standard input is
closed.
.B \-\-yes
(or
.B \-\-force
where documented) answers yes.
.TP
.B Flags
Long-form flags
.RI ( --hash ,
.IR --scopes ,
.IR --scope ,
.IR --prefixes ,
.IR --secret ,
.IR --protocols ,
.IR --vendor-attrs ,
.IR --match ,
.IR --action ,
.IR --branch ,
.IR --protocol ,
.IR --method ,
.IR --backend ,
.IR --listener ,
.IR --server ,
.IR --name ,
.IR --port ,
.IR --identity ,
.IR --output )
take a single argument;
.IR --default ,
.IR --force ,
.IR --legacy ,
.IR --check ,
.IR --replace ,
.IR --json ,
.IR --local ,
.IR --all ,
.IR --build-on-host ,
.IR --allow-uid-mismatch ,
.IR --apply ,
.IR --hosts ,
.I --remove-home
and
.I --yes
take none. Required positional arguments come before flags.
.TP
.B Audit lines
Every state\-changing command writes lines to syslog, ending in a session id
.BI sid= id
that ties the lines of a run together; see
.BR "AUDIT LOG" .
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B tacacs
TACACS+, served by tacquito built from source; installed by
.B "tacctl install"
and enabled by default.
.TP
.B radius
RADIUS, served by FreeRADIUS from the distribution's package; installed and
enabled by
.BR "tacctl backend enable radius" .
.PP
Every change renders the configuration of every enabled backend from the
store, all or none, and restarts only the backends whose files changed.
With more than one backend enabled,
.BR status ,
.BR "config show" ,
.B "config validate"
and
.B log
print a labelled section per backend
.RB ( "== Backend: radius (radius, freeradius) ==" ).
.PP
.B Listeners
are
.BI listeners. backend . name
in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "server listen" ).
The TACACS+ listener
.B default
(tcp :49; the port the device walkthroughs and the Linux host scripts name for TACACS+, and the pulls and plans compare, is its
port) runs in
.BR tacquito.service ;
every further one is an instance
.BI tacquito@ name .service
of
.BR tacquito@.service ,
.B PartOf
and
.B WantedBy
.BR tacquito.service ,
so
.B "systemctl restart tacquito"
covers them all. Each unit has a rendered drop-in
.IR <unit>.d/tacctl.conf .
RADIUS listeners
.RB ( auth ,
udp :1812;
.BR acct ,
udp :1813; their ports are the RADIUS ones the walkthroughs and scripts name) are
.B listen
sections of tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration. TACACS+ over TLS is reserved
in the schema and not implemented.
.PP
.B RADIUS.
.B "backend enable radius"
installs
.B freeradius
and
.B freeradius\-utils
(Debian/Ubuntu; RHEL family from AppStream) and starts the distribution's
unit
.RB ( freeradius
or
.BR radiusd )
with a drop-in that runs tacctl's own instance
.RB ( "\-n tacctl\-radius \-D" " <its dictionary>" ):
the package's configuration is not read, edited or unlinked, so there is no
EAP, inner tunnel, proxying or default client. A unit that is already
running or enabled without tacctl's drop-in is somebody's RADIUS server and
is refused. One client per prefix of every scope RADIUS serves, with the
scope's secret; a user authenticates only from a client in one of their
scopes; disabled users and the
.B root
sink never do. PAP only, against the stored bcrypt hashes (no CHAP,
MS-CHAP or EAP). No per-command authorization and no command accounting.
An Access-Accept carries
.B Service-Type
always, and a vendor's privilege attribute only where the scope opts in
.RB ( "scope vendor-attrs" ", " "scope devices" ).
FreeRADIUS 3.2.5 and 3.0.27 log a "BlastRADIUS check" block for a client the
first time it sends Message-Authenticator and require it from that client
from then on.
.SH STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
(users, groups, scopes, filters) and
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
(settings) are canonical. Generated from them:
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
and the tacquito unit drop-ins; for RADIUS
.IR tacctl\-radius.conf ,
.I tacctl\-radius.users
and
.I tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
in the FreeRADIUS configuration directory.
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
holds their checksums.
.PP
A change is checked against hand edits, snapshotted, written, rendered into
a staging area for every enabled backend and proved there (read back, the
daemon's own config check), then installed, and the backends whose files
changed are restarted; a failure at any step puts the store,
.I tacctl.yaml
and every generated file back.
.PP
.B Concurrent commands.
A command that changes the store or
.I tacctl.yaml
holds one lock,
.IR /etc/tacctl/.store\-apply.lock ,
from before it copies the files it may put back to after its last restart, so that a command that
fails does not put back what another one wrote in between. A second command
prints
.I "Waiting for another tacctl command that is changing the configuration..."
once and runs when the first is done;
.B Ctrl\-C
ends the wait (\c
.IR "Nothing was changed." ,
exit 1;
.B install
and
.BR upgrade ,
which have built or pulled by then, say that the store,
.I tacctl.yaml
and the services were not changed and to run the command again). Every write of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
the store commands,
.BR "config render" ,
.BR "config loglevel" ,
.BR "server listen|metrics" ,
the RADIUS listener changes,
.BR "backend enable|disable" ,
.BR "store import" ,
.BR "store rollback" ,
.BR "backup restore" ,
.B upgrade
(after its self-update) and
.B install
(from its seed to its end) take it; a command that only reads does not. A file
that was edited by hand while a command ran is saved as
.I <file>.overwritten\-<timestamp>
in
.I /etc/tacctl
(and named in a warning) before the command puts its own copy back.
.PP
.B Drift.
A generated file that no longer matches its checksum was edited by hand:
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
show a
.B DRIFT
line and every change is refused (exit 3). Keep a hand edit of
.I tacquito.yaml
with
.B "store import \-\-replace"
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" ;
discard it with
.B "config render \-\-force"
(a copy goes to
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups/legacy/ ).
RADIUS files can only be re-rendered.
.PP
.B Legacy read-only mode.
Without a store (an upgrade whose gate stopped, or after
.BR "store rollback" )
tacctl reads
.I tacquito.yaml
directly: read commands work, commands that change users, groups, scopes
or filters are refused with
.IR "store not initialised" .
Only TACACS+ exists in that mode.
.SH UPGRADING
.B "tacctl upgrade"
pulls the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
and, when the installed binary was not built from the commit now checked out,
builds it again and re-executes it once, so that
.B "tacctl version"
always matches the clone. Then, in this order, it:
.IP 1. 4
moves
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups/
and
.I templates/
from
.I /etc/tacquito
to
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
leaving links at the old paths (idempotent; a regular file written there by
other software wins over the moved copy, which is kept under
.IR backups/legacy/ );
.IP 2. 4
installs
.B tacquito.service
and
.BR tacquito@.service ,
imports a hand-managed drop-in
.I tacctl\-overrides.conf
into
.I tacctl.yaml
once, renders
.I tacctl.conf
in its place and keeps the old file under
.IR backups/legacy/ ;
.IP 3. 4
on an install that has no store yet, runs the store gate:
.B "store import \-\-check"
(import, render, equivalence of what tacquito loads from both files, a load
test of the rendered file with the new binary on a loopback port). Only when
it passes is the store written, the old file kept as
.IR backups/legacy/tacquito.yaml.pre\-store. <timestamp>,
.I tacquito.yaml
rendered and tacquito restarted.
.PP
When the gate stops (unrepresentable content, a render that is not
equivalent, the tacquito binary missing) the upgrade still completes, leaves
.I tacquito.yaml
and the daemon as they were, and tacctl is in legacy read-only mode. Fix what
.B "store import \-\-check"
reports and upgrade again, or accept the difference:
.B "store import"
.RB [ \-\-force ],
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" .
.B "store rollback"
undoes the move into the store.
.PP
The shipped operator rules and privileges do not hold
.IR "clear ..." ,
.IR "monitor capture" ,
or the unbounded and interactive forms of
.B ping
and
.BR traceroute ,
and Junos
.B OP-CLASS
has no
.B clear
bit. A list stored in
.I tacctl.yaml
keeps what it holds, except an operator or readonly list equal to what 0.2.3
shipped, which the upgrade drops. The upgrade and
.B "config validate"
name the stored settings that still carry a removed command;
.B "group privilege reset operator"
and
.B "group commands reset <group>"
(or
.BR "group reset <group> --preset --only commands,junos" ,
for the values of a role preset of 0.2.3, which keeps its
.B show
denies)
are the way back. A device configured earlier keeps its old lines until they
are removed (until then its operators keep the old reach when the server does
not answer, and always over RADIUS). The first upgrade onto this release prints
them (on Cisco
.BR "no privilege exec all level 7 ping" ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR "monitor capture" ,
and
.B "no privilege exec level 7 clear"
.IR "counters, line, ip arp, arp-cache, mac address-table dynamic" ;
on Junos
.BR "delete system login class OP-CLASS permissions clear" ),
and
.B "device config plan"
prints the lines a pulled device needs, in the form its image stores them.
.PP
The Cisco rendering has no
.B "aaa authentication enable"
line, so a pulled Cisco device that has the list reads
.B differs
in
.B "device config list"
until
.B "device config plan <device>"
is pasted; no new pull is needed. On a device that has the list the 0.2.5
walkthrough rendered, the plan is one line in its
.B aaa
region,
.BR "no aaa authentication enable default group " <G> " enable"
(the back\-out types it again); a list of another name stays, and a device
with no list has nothing to paste for it. Until the line is removed,
.B enable
on that device asks the server, and any user the server knows reaches
privilege 15 with their own password. A device with no enable secret has no
.B enable
once the list is gone: set one first (Step 0).
.PP
Going back to 0.2.16 is
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.16"
(a dry run), then
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.16 \-\-apply"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.16" ;
it removes
.BR audit.strict ,
which a 0.2.16 binary refuses. Until 0.2.16 is installed, do not add the
.B audit
setting to
.I tacctl.yaml
again; run the rollback again if it was. The snapshot it takes holds the 0.2.17
form and is restored with 0.2.17.
.PP
Going back to 0.2.9 is
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.9"
(a dry run), then
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.9 \-\-apply"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.9" ;
it removes the transport setting of
.B "device config apply"
and the NETCONF service setting of the scopes and devices, and lists what
0.2.10 left that 0.2.9 ignores (the
.B via
field of the apply records). Until 0.2.9 is installed, do not run
.BR "scope netconf" ,
.BR "device netconf" ,
.B "config devices apply\-transport"
or
.BR "device config apply \-\-transport" ;
run the rollback again if one ran. The snapshot it takes holds the 0.2.10 form
and is restored with 0.2.10. Going back to 0.2.8 is this rollback, the install
of 0.2.9 and then 0.2.9's own
.BR "tacctl rollback 0.2.8" .
.PP
Going back to 0.2.3 is
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.3"
(a dry run), then
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.3 \-\-apply \-\-yes"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.3" .
The rollback converts the keys and fields a 0.2.3 binary refuses (listed under
.B rollback
in COMMANDS).
Until 0.2.3 is installed, a command that writes the console's
settings, a device's SNMP settings or configuration syntax, a scope's Cisco
source interface or the
.B device.config
keys writes the 0.2.5 form again; run the rollback again to convert it. The
snapshot the rollback takes holds the 0.2.5 form and is restored with 0.2.5
(0.2.3's
.B "backup restore"
refuses a
.I tacctl.yaml
with a key it does not know). Going back to 0.2.2 is this rollback, the
install of 0.2.3 and then 0.2.3's own
.BR "tacctl rollback 0.2.2" .
.SH CONFIGURATION KEYS
.SS tacctl.yaml
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
holds the operator's overrides of tacctl's tunables (see
.BR FILES ).
A key set back to its default is removed from the file; a key that is not in
this list, or whose value does not fit, is refused with the reason before
anything is written. Read one key with
.BR "config get" ,
the merged result with
.BR "config show"
or
.BR "config dump" ,
and check a hand-edited file with
.BR "config validate" .
A name in angle brackets stands for a group, a scope, a backend or a listener;
the keys of a family exist once for each.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: config-keys
.TP
.B audit.strict
true or false. Default: false.
Whether a command that changes state, or shows a secret, is refused when its audit line cannot be written: with true, an action or mixed command whose cmd start line logger did not write exits 1 before it changes anything, and a command whose secret-read line logger did not write exits 1 before it shows the secret (the AUDIT LOG section has the messages); any other read runs, and an end line that cannot be written is the warning. config audit-strict is never refused by it. Set with \fBtacctl config audit\-strict\fR.
.TP
.B backends.enabled
non\-empty list of backends (tacacs, radius). Default: [tacacs].
The backends that serve the model, in render and restart order. Set with \fBtacctl backend enable\fR or \fBtacctl backend disable\fR.
.TP
.B backends.tacacs.level
integer, 0 to 100. Default: 20.
Log level of tacquito. Set with \fBtacctl config loglevel\fR.
.TP
.B backends.tacacs.metrics_address
host:port. Default: 127.0.0.1:8080.
Where tacquito's Prometheus exporter listens. Set with \fBtacctl server metrics address\fR.
.TP
.B bcrypt.cost
integer, 10 to 14. Default: 12.
Cost of the bcrypt hashes tacctl makes from now on. Set with \fBtacctl config bcrypt\-cost\fR.
.TP
.B device.apply.confirm_timeout
integer, 3 to 60. Default: 10.
Minutes of the commit confirmed of every device config apply: the device undoes the change by itself after that long unless the confirming commit is typed. Set with \fBtacctl config devices apply\-confirm\-timeout\fR.
.TP
.B device.apply.pause
integer, 0 to 3600. Default: 60.
Seconds device config apply waits between the devices of a batch after the canary; 0: none. Set with \fBtacctl config devices apply\-pause\fR.
.TP
.B device.apply.transport
one of auto, cli, netconf. Default: cli.
How device config apply reaches a Junos device: NETCONF where the last read found it answering and the login may commit, else the command line (auto), the command line only (cli), or NETCONF only (netconf). Set with \fBtacctl config devices apply\-transport\fR.
.TP
.B device.config.confirm_timeout
integer, 1 to 60. Default: 5.
Minutes of the commit confirmed that ends the region of a Junos device config plan: the switch undoes the paste by itself after that long unless the one word commit is typed. Set with \fBtacctl config devices confirm\-timeout\fR.
.TP
.B device.config.max_concurrency
integer, 1 to 64. Default: 8.
The most devices device config pull reads at once; --concurrency can lower it for a run, never raise it. Set with \fBtacctl config devices max\-concurrency\fR.
.TP
.B device.config.timeout
integer, 10 to 600. Default: 90.
Seconds one device may take in a pull, connect included. Set with \fBtacctl config devices timeout\fR.
.TP
.B device.config.transport
one of auto, netconf, ssh. Default: auto.
How device config pull reads a device: NETCONF where it answers and the ssh command line otherwise (auto), NETCONF only, or ssh only. Set with \fBtacctl config devices transport\fR.
.TP
.B device.timeout_scale
integer, 1 to 10. Default: 1.
The timing scale of every device: a whole number from 1 to 10 that multiplies each network timing used to reach a device (the connect and handshake of the sessions, the host-key scans, the SNMP timeout up to its maximum, the per-device time of a pull or an apply within its bounds); a scope's and a device's own win. Set with \fBtacctl config devices timeout\-scale\fR.
.TP
.B host.default_method
one of tacplus, radius. Default: tacplus.
The method a Linux host is enrolled with when its scope decides none. Set with \fBtacctl host default\-method\fR.
.TP
.B linux.engineer_sudo
non\-empty list of absolute command paths. Default: none (unset).
The commands tac-engineer may run through sudo on enrolled hosts other than this server; unset: every command. Set with \fBtacctl config linux engineer\-sudo\fR.
.TP
.B linux.uid_max
integer, 1000 to 4294967293. Default: 89999.
Last UID and GID of that range. Set with \fBtacctl config linux uid\-range\fR.
.TP
.B linux.uid_min
integer, 1000 to 4294967293. Default: 80000.
First UID and GID of the Linux accounts tacctl makes, one range for every host. Set with \fBtacctl config linux uid\-range\fR.
.TP
.B mgmt_acl.names.cisco
name of 1 to 63 characters (a letter, then letters, digits, _ or \-). Default: VTY\-ACL.
Name of the ACL config cisco emits. Set with \fBtacctl config mgmt\-acl cisco\-name\fR.
.TP
.B mgmt_acl.names.juniper
name of 1 to 63 characters (a letter, then letters, digits, _ or \-). Default: MGMT\-ACL.
Name of the filter config juniper emits. Set with \fBtacctl config mgmt\-acl juniper\-name\fR.
.TP
.B mgmt_acl.permits
list of CIDRs, each with optional services after a colon (tcp/22, tcp/161\-162, udp/*, icmp, comma separated). Default: [].
The entries the management ACL and filter permit: a CIDR, or a CIDR with its services (10.0.0.0/8:tcp/22,udp/161,icmp). Set with \fBtacctl config mgmt\-acl add\fR or \fBtacctl config mgmt\-acl remove\fR.
.TP
.B password.max_age_days
integer, 1 or more. Default: 90.
Days after which a password counts as old: tacctl status warns. Set with \fBtacctl config password\-age\fR.
.TP
.B password.min_length
integer, 8 to 64. Default: 12.
Shortest password typed at a prompt. Set with \fBtacctl config password\-min\-length\fR.
.TP
.B scope.default
scope name (a letter, then up to 31 letters, digits, _ or \-) or null. Default: lab.
The scope new users and devices land in without a choice; null: none. Set with \fBtacctl scope default\fR.
.TP
.B secret.min_length
integer, 16 to 128. Default: 16.
Shortest shared secret an operator may type. Set with \fBtacctl config secret\-min\-length\fR.
.TP
.B server.addresses
list of one IP address (not a host name, unspecified, loopback, link\-local, broadcast or IPv4\-mapped IPv6). Default: none (unset).
The address devices and hosts reach this server at, a list that holds one address for now; the enrolment of this server and install record it (detected from the interface with the default route when none is recorded and none is given), and server addresses changes it. Set with \fBtacctl server addresses\fR.
.TP
.B server.name
name of 1 to 63 characters in lower case (a letter, then letters, digits or hyphens, not ending in a hyphen); not unset. Default: none (unset).
The name of this server in the console prompt and its notices, in place of the host name; unset: the host name. Set with \fBtacctl server name\fR.
.TP
.B snmp.port
integer, 1 to 65535. Default: 161.
UDP port of the agents. Set with \fBtacctl config snmp port\fR.
.TP
.B snmp.timeout
integer, 1 to 10. Default: 2.
Seconds to wait for an answer (one retry). Set with \fBtacctl config snmp timeout\fR.
.TP
.B snmp.v3.auth
one of sha, sha256. Default: none (unset); sha is used.
Authentication protocol of the v3 user. Set with \fBtacctl config snmp v3\-user\fR.
.TP
.B snmp.v3.priv
one of aes128. Default: none (unset); aes128 is used.
Privacy protocol of the v3 user. Set with \fBtacctl config snmp v3\-user\fR.
.TP
.B snmp.version
one of v2c, v3. Default: none (unset).
SNMP version of the name lookup of device add and device check; unset: no lookup. Set with \fBtacctl config snmp community\fR or \fBtacctl config snmp v3\-user\fR.
.TP
.B privileges.<group>
list of Cisco priv\-exec commands (an entry may start with exec:, exec all:, configure: or configure all:). Default: the shipped value of each built\-in group (tacctl config defaults).
The Cisco priv-exec commands the group's level may run; unset: the shipped list. Set with \fBtacctl group privilege\fR.
.TP
.B commands.<group>
ordered list of {name, action, match} rules ending in the name: "*" catch\-all. Default: the shipped value of each built\-in group (tacctl config defaults).
The group's command rules, in order, ending in the catch-all; unset: the shipped rules. Set with \fBtacctl group commands\fR.
.TP
.B aaa.order.<scope>
one of tacacs\-first, local\-first. Default: tacacs\-first.
Order of the methods in the Cisco aaa lines and the Junos authentication-order the scope's configurations emit. Set with \fBtacctl scope aaa\-order\fR.
.TP
.B exec_timeout.<scope>
integer, 0 to 60. Default: 60.
Idle-session timeout in minutes the scope's device configurations set; 0: never. Set with \fBtacctl scope exec\-timeout\fR.
.TP
.B tacacs_group.<scope>
name of 1 to 63 characters (a letter, then letters, digits, _ or \-). Default: TACACS\-GROUP.
The label of the Cisco aaa group server tacacs+ the scope's configuration names. Set with \fBtacctl scope tacacs\-group\fR.
.TP
.B radius_group.<scope>
name of 1 to 63 characters (a letter, then letters, digits, _ or \-). Default: RADIUS\-GROUP.
The label of the Cisco aaa group server radius the scope's configuration names. Set with \fBtacctl scope radius\-group\fR.
.TP
.B cisco.source_interface.<scope>
interface name in the long form IOS prints (Loopback, GigabitEthernet, TenGigabitEthernet, TwentyFiveGigE, FortyGigabitEthernet, HundredGigE, FastEthernet, Ethernet, Serial, Vlan, Port\-channel, Tunnel, BVI, Management, then the number, with / . : separators), such as Loopback0 or GigabitEthernet0/0; not an abbreviation such as lo0. Default: none (unset).
The interface the scope's Cisco devices source their TACACS+ and RADIUS requests from (ip tacacs source-interface, ip radius source-interface); unset: the standard renders and compares no such line, and the walkthrough prints it commented. Set with \fBtacctl config cisco source\-interface\fR.
.TP
.B scope_auth_method.<scope>
one of tacacs, radius. Default: none (unset).
The protocol the scope's device configurations, new hosts and Linux scripts use when a command names none; unset: not decided (the scope's protocols filter, then the global default). Set with \fBtacctl scope auth\-method\fR.
.TP
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.cisco.<scope>
name of 1 to 63 characters (a letter, then letters, digits, _ or \-). Default: VTY\-ACL.
The scope's own ACL name, in place of mgmt_acl.names.cisco. Set with \fBtacctl scope mgmt\-acl\fR.
.TP
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.juniper.<scope>
name of 1 to 63 characters (a letter, then letters, digits, _ or \-). Default: MGMT\-ACL.
The scope's own filter name, in place of mgmt_acl.names.juniper. Set with \fBtacctl scope mgmt\-acl\fR.
.TP
.B scope_mgmt_acl.permits.<scope>
list of CIDRs, each with optional services after a colon (tcp/22, tcp/161\-162, udp/*, icmp, comma separated). Default: [].
The scope's own permitted entries (a CIDR, or a CIDR with its services), in place of mgmt_acl.permits. Set with \fBtacctl scope mgmt\-acl\fR.
.TP
.B scope_mgmt_acl.mode.<scope>
one of ssh\-only, strict. Default: none (unset).
The mode of the scope's Junos management filter: strict accepts every protocol from the permit list and has no default-accept term; unset: ssh-only, the filter that restricts ssh, NETCONF and SNMP. Only strict is stored. Set with \fBtacctl scope mgmt\-acl\fR.
.TP
.B listeners.<backend>.<name>
mapping with network, address, role, metrics_address, tls (tls is reserved). Default: tacacs.default is tcp :49; radius.auth is udp :1812; radius.acct is udp :1813.
Where a backend's daemon listens; the built-in listeners can be changed and reset, not removed. Set with \fBtacctl server listen\fR.
.TP
.B junos.<group>.deny_commands
list of regular expressions. Default: none (unset).
POSIX extended regular expressions of the commands the Junos class of the group may not run. Set with \fBtacctl group junos\fR.
.TP
.B junos.<group>.deny_configuration
list of regular expressions. Default: none (unset).
POSIX extended regular expressions of the configuration statements the Junos class may not touch. Set with \fBtacctl group junos\fR.
.TP
.B wti_level.<group>
one of viewonly, user, superuser, administrator. Default: none (unset).
The WTI access level of the group, in place of the one its priv-lvl band gives. Set with \fBtacctl group edit\fR or \fBtacctl group add\fR.
.TP
.B tier.<group>
one of readonly, operator, engineer, superuser. Default: none (unset).
The tacctl tier of the group, in place of the one its priv-lvl band gives. Set with \fBtacctl group edit\fR or \fBtacctl group add\fR.
.TP
.B breakglass_scope.<scope>.users
list of name:role entries, role one of admin, operator, readonly, at most 16. Default: none (unset).
The break-glass local users the scope's device configurations create. Only the names and roles are kept; no credential is stored. Set with \fBtacctl scope breakglass\fR.
.TP
.B blocks.<scope>.<vendor>
non\-empty list of block names (snmp, system). Default: none (unset).
The configuration blocks in force for the scope and vendor (cisco or juniper), a list of snmp and system; a file under the blocks directory that this list does not name is ignored. Set with \fBtacctl scope block\fR.
.TP
.B blocks_device.<device>.<vendor>
non\-empty list of block names (snmp, system). Default: none (unset).
The configuration blocks in force for one device and vendor (cisco or juniper), a list of snmp and system; the device's lower case name is written with every '.' as '~' (jsw1~lab~example~net), and a file under the blocks directory that this list does not name is ignored. Set with \fBtacctl device block\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.version
one of v2c, v3. Default: none (unset).
The scope's SNMP version; unset: snmp.version. Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.port
integer, 1 to 65535. Default: none (unset).
The scope's agent port; unset: snmp.port. Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.timeout
integer, 1 to 10. Default: none (unset).
The scope's timeout in seconds; unset: snmp.timeout. Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.v3.auth
one of sha, sha256. Default: none (unset).
The scope's v3 authentication protocol; unset: snmp.v3.auth. Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.v3.priv
one of aes128. Default: none (unset).
The scope's v3 privacy protocol; unset: snmp.v3.priv. Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.contact
text of 1 to 120 characters. Default: none (unset).
The sysContact the scope's walkthroughs set. Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B snmp_scope.<scope>.clients
list of IPv4 CIDRs. Default: [].
The SNMP clients the scope's walkthroughs allow (the server's own address first and a final deny are added when rendered, not stored). Set with \fBtacctl scope snmp\fR.
.TP
.B breakglass_hold.<device>.address
one IPv4 or IPv6 address, in its canonical form, without a prefix length. Default: none (unset).
The address the server does not answer while the hold stands (a device's registered address, a host's recorded one); a name's dots are written ~ in the key. The renderers take it out of every prefix and tagged address. Set with \fBtacctl breakglass hold\fR.
.TP
.B breakglass_hold.<device>.since
RFC 3339 time. Default: none (unset).
When the hold was made (RFC 3339). Set with \fBtacctl breakglass hold\fR.
.TP
.B breakglass_hold.<device>.by
text of 1 to 256 characters (a user name for by). Default: none (unset).
The user who held it. Set with \fBtacctl breakglass hold\fR.
.TP
.B breakglass_hold.<device>.reason
text of 1 to 256 characters (a user name for by). Default: none (unset).
Why, 1 to 256 characters; logged and shown, never rendered into a backend file. Removed with the hold by tacctl breakglass release. Set with \fBtacctl breakglass hold\fR.
.TP
.B emergency.lockdown.<scope>.groups
list of group names, at most 64. Default: none (unset).
The groups whose logins the scope refuses while the lockdown stands: both renderers take the scope out of their users. Superuser groups are never listed. Set with \fBtacctl emergency lockdown\fR.
.TP
.B emergency.lockdown.<scope>.since
RFC 3339 time. Default: none (unset).
When the lockdown began (RFC 3339). Set with \fBtacctl emergency lockdown\fR.
.TP
.B emergency.lockdown.<scope>.by
text of 1 to 256 characters (a user name for by). Default: none (unset).
The user who began it. Set with \fBtacctl emergency lockdown\fR.
.TP
.B emergency.lockdown.<scope>.reason
text of 1 to 256 characters (a user name for by). Default: none (unset).
Why, 1 to 256 characters; logged and shown, never rendered into a backend file. Removed with the lockdown by tacctl emergency lockdown disable. Set with \fBtacctl emergency lockdown\fR.
.TP
.B netconf_scope.<scope>.state
one of enable, disable. Default: none (unset).
The NETCONF service of the scope's Junos devices: enable or disable (unset: leave, nothing is planned or compared). Set with \fBtacctl scope netconf\fR.
.TP
.B netconf_scope.<scope>.connection_limit
integer, 1 to 250. Default: none (unset).
The connection limit of an enabled NETCONF service (1 to 250); unset: none stated. Set with \fBtacctl scope netconf\fR.
.TP
.B netconf_scope.<scope>.rate_limit
integer, 1 to 250. Default: none (unset).
The rate limit of an enabled NETCONF service (1 to 250); unset: none stated. Set with \fBtacctl scope netconf\fR.
.TP
.B timeout_scale_scope.<scope>
integer, 1 to 10. Default: none (unset).
The timing scale of the scope's devices, 1 to 10, in place of device.timeout_scale; a device's own wins. Set with \fBtacctl scope timeout\-scale\fR.
.\" END GENERATED: config-keys
.SS console.yaml
.I /etc/tacctl/console.yaml
holds the login console's settings (see
.BR "tacctl console" ).
Edit it with
.BR "tacctl console" ;
an absent file is the defaults, and a key that is not in this list is refused
(the
.B tiers
and
.B users
mappings that releases before 0.2.20 wrote are read and ignored).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: console-keys
.TP
.B version
integer. Default: 1.
The format of the file; this release reads and writes 1.
.TP
.B settings.idle_timeout
integer, 0 to 1440 minutes (0: none). Default: 30.
Idle time after which a console session ends. Set with \fBtacctl console idle\-timeout\fR.
.TP
.B settings.agent_forwarding
true or false. Default: false.
Whether the console's ssh and sshd's drop-in forward the ssh agent. Set with \fBtacctl console agent\-forwarding\fR.
.TP
.B settings.ssh_escape
true or false. Default: false.
Whether the escape character of the console's ssh stays on. Set with \fBtacctl console ssh\-escape\fR.
.TP
.B settings.system_shell
absolute path. Default: /bin/bash.
The program the console's system-shell word starts. Set with \fBtacctl console system\-shell path\fR.
.TP
.B settings.system_shell_tiers
list of tiers (not engineer). Default: [superuser].
The tiers that may start the system shell. The superuser tier always may, and is written into the list; engineer never. Set with \fBtacctl console system\-shell\fR.
.TP
.B settings.forwarding_tiers
list of tiers (not engineer). Default: [superuser].
The tiers whose console logins may forward X11 and TCP ports. Set with \fBtacctl console forwarding tiers\fR.
.TP
.B settings.gateway_ports
true or false. Default: false.
Whether those tiers may bind forwarded ports to an address other than loopback. Set with \fBtacctl console forwarding gateway\-ports\fR.
.TP
.B settings.list_max
integer, 1 to 1000, or \-1 (never ask). Default: 40.
The number of completions the console's shell lists without asking "Show all N?". Set with \fBtacctl console list\-max\fR.
.TP
.B settings.space_completion
true or false. Default: true.
Whether a typed space at the console's prompt completes a fixed word, as on Junos. Written to the file only when false. Set with \fBtacctl console space\-completion\fR.
.TP
.B settings.color
true or false. Default: absent (auto).
Whether the console's lists are in colour: true (on) or false (off). Written to the file only when set; a file without the key is auto, the terminal decides. Set with \fBtacctl console color\fR.
.TP
.B settings.system_shell_users
mapping of user name to disable or enable:<tier>. Default: absent (no user has one).
The users with a system-shell setting of their own, which wins over their tier's: enable:<tier> gives it to a user of a tier that has none, for as long as the user is in that tier (readonly or operator), and disable takes it from a user of a tier that has it. Written to the file only when a user has one. Set with \fBtacctl console system\-shell user\fR.
.\" END GENERATED: console-keys
.PP
A mapping
.B settings.password_cache
(\fBtiers\fR: a list of operator, engineer, superuser, default none;
\fBidle\fR: minutes, 1 to 120, default 15; \fBmax\fR: hours, 1 to 24, default
8) is written only when one of its three values is not the default. Set with
.BR "tacctl console password\-cache" .
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
takes it out of the file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command did what it was asked, or showed what there was to show. A
confirmation answered no cancels the command with nothing changed, and ends
with 0 for the removals
.RB ( "user remove" ,
.BR "group remove" )
and for
.BR "store rollback" .
.TP
.B 1
The command refused or failed, and said why on standard error: a value that
does not fit, a name that does not exist or exists already, a store or
.I tacctl.yaml
that cannot be read or written, a caller whose tier does not allow the
command, a check that found a problem
.RB ( "config validate" " (with " "\-\-json" ", after it has printed its object)" ", "
.BR "console check" ", " "device check" ),
a device that could not be read, compared or planned
.RB ( "device config pull" ", " "device config diff" ", " "device config plan" ),
a device
.B "device config apply"
failed, rolled back or left pending (and a review that was not answered, and
an apply that applied none of its devices and found none already applied:
.B "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes"
that skipped them all), a step of a lifecycle phase that failed. An unknown
sub-command prints on standard error one error line that names it
.RB ( "[ERROR] Unknown subcommand: 'seed'" ),
a
.B Did you mean
line with the sub-commands of that command it may have meant when something
fits (within two letters of the word, or the commands that have it as a verb
further down:
.B "device config seed"
names
.BR "device config shapes seed" ;
in the shell and the console only the commands the caller's tier can run)
and a pointer
.RB ( "Run 'tacctl <command> \-h' for usage." ),
not the usage, and ends with 1 as well. No word,
.B help
and a word that starts with
.B \-
still print the usage of a command that has them
.RB ( host
has no
.B help
word). When the word is a sub-command of a
command that has fixed sub-commands
.RB ( "device config seed jr1" )
it is answered before
.B sudo
runs, like an unknown command, so it asks for no password; a word that follows
a name or a value
.RB ( "scope secret lab shwo" ),
a flag and its value, a verb of another family and a verb the tier cannot run
are left to
.BR sudo ,
which asks first. An unknown command
.RB ( "tacctl enroll" )
prints on standard error one error line that names it, the commands it
may have meant (the verbs of that name,
.BR "device enroll" " and " "host enroll" ,
or a command within two letters of it) and a pointer to
.B tacctl
for the list, and ends with 1; it is answered before
.B sudo
runs, so it never asks for a password;
.BR tacctl ,
.BR "tacctl help" ,
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
print the usage and end with 1. Nothing was changed unless the
message says what was.
.TP
.B 2
The arguments were wrong for a verb that checks them itself and says so
.RB ( "store show" ,
.BR "store import" ,
.BR "store rollback" ),
or a backend id is unknown, or the backend does not provide the action asked
of it, or
.B "device config diff \-\-exit\-code"
found a device that differs, or
.B "device config plan \-\-exit\-code"
found a device that needs a change, or
.B "device config apply \-\-check \-\-exit\-code"
found a device that needs a change, or the arguments of
.BR "device config pull" ", " diff ", " plan ", " apply ", " shapes ", " list " or " forget
were wrong
.RB ( apply
without a terminal and without
.BR "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes" ,
and
.BR "apply \-\-hold \-\-json" ,
included).
.TP
.B 3
A backend command was refused because a file tacctl rendered holds a hand edit
that the command would discard (see
.BR "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION" ),
or
.B "store import \-\-check"
could not prove the import safe. Nothing was written.
.TP
.B 126
sudo could not be run to re-run tacctl as root, or
.B tacctl\-console
was given an argument it does not accept.
.TP
.B 127
sudo is not installed, and tacctl was not run as root.
.TP
.B 130
The command was interrupted at a prompt (Ctrl-C at a password prompt, for
example), or a
.BR "device config pull" ,
.B "device config diff \-\-pull"
or
.B "device config plan \-\-pull"
was stopped by Ctrl-C (the first stops starting devices and lets the running
ones finish, a second cuts them off), or a
.B "device config apply"
was (a transaction in flight finishes under the first; under the second a
candidate is discarded and a pending commit cancelled where the window allows,
else reported
.BR PENDING ).
A pull changes nothing on a device.
.PP
.B "tacctl ssh"
and
.B "tacctl device ssh"
end with the status of the ssh they run, and
.B "tacctl shell \-c"
with the status of the line it ran.
.PP
A command that changes state writes its status to the audit log as the
.B status=
of its
.B "cmd end"
line, with a
.B result=
word (see
.BR "AUDIT LOG" ):
.B ok
for 0,
.B interrupted
for 130,
.B denied
when a verb refused the caller (the status is the verb's own, 1),
.B aborted
(status 2) when tacctl itself panicked, and
.B failed
for any other status.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
tacctl reads the variables below. Everything else it needs is a fixed path
(see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B "SUDO_USER, SUDO_UID, SUDO_GID"
Set by
.BR sudo (8)
for the person behind it. tacctl takes the caller's name from
.BR SUDO_USER :
the tier gate, the target of
.BR passwd ,
the scopes an engineer reaches, the user
.B ssh
and
.B host
run ssh as. The pair
.B SUDO_USER
and
.B SUDO_UID
is checked against the passwd database before anything runs, and a
mismatch denies every command. Files a command writes for the caller
.RB ( "config linux script \-\-output" ,
for example) are handed to
.B SUDO_UID
and
.BR SUDO_GID .
.TP
.B HOME
Where the history of
.B "tacctl shell"
and of the login console is kept
.RI ( ~/.local/state/tacctl/history ).
.TP
.B "USER, LOGNAME"
The login name the console records in its session log
.RB ( USER
first, then
.BR LOGNAME ).
.TP
.B "SSH_CLIENT, SSH_TTY, SSH_CONNECTION"
Set by sshd. The console logs the client address and the terminal of a session;
.B tacctl shell
reads its lines from the pipe when a command arrives over ssh with
.B SSH_CONNECTION
set and no terminal
.RB ( SSH_TTY
unset).
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
The agent socket
.B host
and
.B ssh
hand to the ssh they run as the caller, so an agent's key reaches the host.
sudo drops it unless the tiers drop-in keeps it
.RB ( "config sudoers tiers" ).
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X11 display of sshd's forwarding, handed to
.B "tacctl ssh \-X"
and
.B \-Y
after its shape is checked, and kept by the tiers drop-in for the console.
.TP
.B "VISUAL, EDITOR"
The editor of
.B "scope block ... edit|adopt"
and
.BR "device block ... edit|adopt" :
.BR VISUAL ,
else
.BR EDITOR ,
else
.BR editor ,
else
.BR vi ,
run as the invoking user. sudo drops both, so the sudoers drop-ins keep them for
those four command lines only; tacctl removes them from its own environment at
start, so no program it runs inherits them.
.TP
.B TACCTL_ASKPASS
.RI "Set by a " "tacctl shell" " or console session whose password cache is on, as " socket : token
in the environment of the
.B sudo
of each line that uses it, with a token made for that line and dead when it
ends (never on sudo's command line); the tiers drop-ins keep it for those
lines only
.RB ( "device onboard"
asks it for your own password after its review, and
.B "device collect"
for your own login; a line with
.B \-\-login
logs in as a device's local account and never has it). The root side of
.BR "device config pull" ,
.B "block ... adopt"
and
.B ssh
reads it once and removes it from its own environment, and the helper ssh runs as
.B SSH_ASKPASS
(the tacctl binary, started with
.B TACCTL_ASKPASS_HELPER=1
and the prompt as its only argument) answers a password prompt with it and
nothing else. See
.BR "console password\-cache" .
.TP
.B "TACCTL_COLOR, NO_COLOR, TERM"
Whether output is styled (bold titles, colour in the
.BR [INFO] ,
.B [WARN]
and
.B [ERROR]
lines and in help and listings). The lists of the shell, the console,
.B ?
and
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
use one palette: command names, sub-commands, fixed words and
options teal, live names (users, groups, devices, hosts) bold, descriptions
plain. Styling appears only on a terminal, decided
for standard output and standard error separately: a stream is styled when it is
a terminal and
.B TERM
is neither empty nor
.BR dumb .
.B TACCTL_COLOR=always
styles both streams even into a pipe and
.B TACCTL_COLOR=never
neither; it wins over the rest, and any other value is ignored. Otherwise a
non-empty
.B NO_COLOR
means never. The tags carry their word, so nothing depends on colour. The
sudoers drop-ins keep
.B NO_COLOR
and
.B TACCTL_COLOR
so that the person's choice reaches the root process; without a drop-in the
variables do not cross
.BR sudo (8),
and the terminal alone decides. The login console decides by its terminal only:
its sessions carry neither variable.
.TP
.B TACCTL_CONSOLE
Set by the login console, as
.BI TACCTL_CONSOLE= session
on the command line of each sudo it runs, to mark a command as run from a
console session. The drop-in keeps it. tacctl reads it only to tighten what it
does (the hardened ssh, the
.B console=
field of the log lines).
.TP
.B TACQUITO_SRC
The clone of the tacquito source that
.B install
and
.B upgrade
build the server from (default
.IR /opt/tacquito\-src ).
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The directory where tacctl stages private copies while it works (rendered
configuration trees, a candidate sudoers file, a script for a host, a check of
an import), each made with mode 0600 or 0700 and removed on every path. Default:
the system temporary directory
.RI ( /tmp ).
.BR sudo (8)
may replace the variable with its own environment policy, in which case
tacctl sees the value that policy leaves.
.PP
tacctl also reads variables that move its files and set its clock for the test
suite
.RB ( TACCTL_ETC ,
.BR TACCTL_STATE_DIR ,
\&...). They are not an interface.
.SH FILES
.SS tacctl
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/
State directory, mode 0700, root only.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
The canonical users, groups, scopes and connection filters (shared secrets and
password hashes included). Mode 0600, root only. Print it with
.BR "tacctl store show" .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
Operator overrides of tacctl's tunables and settings (see
.BR "CONFIGURATION KEYS" ).
The defaults are built into tacctl and printed by
.BR "tacctl config defaults" ;
an empty file is deleted. A file that does not parse is never written into: a
setter refuses with
.RI "\(lqtacctl.yaml: could not parse " path ": " reason \(rq
and exits 1, and commands that only read use the defaults and warn once on
stderr.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
The device registry (see
.BR "DEVICE REGISTRY" ):
registered device names with address, vendor, port, hostname,
description, legacy-ssh, pinned host keys and acknowledged notices, and the
registry's settings
.RB ( stale_days ,
.BR generic_names ).
Mode 0600, root only; absent means no devices. Edit it with
.BR "tacctl device" ,
which locks it, validates the result and replaces it atomically. Snapshots
include it. The pinned keys of enrolled hosts are kept under
.BR hosts: .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp.yaml
The SNMP community and the pool of v3 users (a name and two passphrases each,
and the default user) that
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
read a device's sysName with. Mode 0600, root only, written atomically by
.BR "tacctl config snmp" ;
never printed. Absent means none. Version 2 of the format holds
.B users:
and
.BR default: ;
a file of version 1 (0.2.22 and before: one v3 user) reads as a pool of one
user that is the default. A command saves version 1 while the file fits it (the
community and at most one v3 user, the default), and version 2 only for what
version 1 cannot hold (a second or a non\-default user, a default that names
another level's user, a device's file without an
.B snmp:
entry). A device's file follows its entry: version 2 while the device has none
(it counts all the same), version 1 once any setting gives the device an entry
when the content fits (a pool of two stays version 2), version 2 again if the
entry is emptied; the command says so when it rewrites a file that way, and a
file it cannot write is a warning that names the command that writes it again
(exit status 1 when the file is then ignored). The same format is the scopes' and the
devices' files below.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp/\fIscope\fR.yaml
The SNMP community, or the pool of v3 users and the default user, of one scope
.RB ( "tacctl scope snmp" ):
the credentials a device of that scope is read with, and printed in its
walkthroughs, instead of the default's. Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root
only; printed only by
.BR "scope snmp ... show \-\-reveal" .
Absent means the scope has none of its own. A scope's rename moves the file and
its removal deletes it.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp/devices/\fIname\fR.yaml
The SNMP community, or the pool of v3 users and the default user, of one registered
device
.RB ( "tacctl device snmp" ):
read in place of its scope's and the default's. The file is named for the
device in lower case. Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root only; printed only
by
.BR "device snmp ... show \-\-reveal" .
Absent means the device has none of its own. A file of version 1 counts only while the
device has an
.B snmp:
map of its own; one without (such as a rollback to 0.2.3 leaves) is ignored,
not read, and said once by
.B "device show"
and
.BR "device snmp ... show" .
A device's rename moves the file, its removal and
.B "device import \-\-replace"
delete it, snapshots hold it, and
.B "backup restore"
makes the directory match the registry it restores. The device's non\-secret SNMP
settings are the
.B snmp:
map of its entry in
.IR devices.yaml .
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
removes that map and leaves the file.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/blocks
The directory of configuration blocks, 0700 root. A scope's blocks are under
its own name and a device's under
.IR _devices ;
a scope cannot be called
.BR _devices .
Snapshots include it and
.B backup restore
brings it back.
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
removes the lists in
.B tacctl.yaml
that put blocks in force and leaves the files.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/blocks/\fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR
One configuration block of a scope: vendor syntax that tacctl owns beside the
structured settings.
.I vendor
is
.B juniper
or
.BR cisco ;
.I block
is
.B snmp
or
.BR system .
Mode 0600 in 0700 directories, root only; a block holds community strings, so
it is printed only by
.BR "scope block ... show \-\-reveal" .
A block is in force only while
.BR tacctl.yaml
names it
.RB ( blocks.\fIscope\fR.\fIvendor\fR ).
A scope's rename moves its blocks and its removal deletes them.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/blocks/_devices/\fIdevice\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR
One configuration block of a registered device, in place of its scope's block
of the same object. The directory is named for the device in lower case; it is
named in
.B tacctl.yaml
by
.BR blocks_device.\fIdevice\fR.\fIvendor\fR ,
the device's dots written as
.BR ~ ,
and written by
.BR "device block" .
A device's rename moves it and its removal deletes it; a new device, or a
rename onto a name whose blocks are already on disk, is refused.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/console.yaml
The login console's settings (see
.BR "CONFIGURATION KEYS" ).
Mode 0600, root only; absent means the defaults. Edit it with
.BR "tacctl console" .
Snapshots include it.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
The login console: a symbolic link to
.IR /usr/local/bin/tacctl ,
made by
.B install
and refreshed by
.BR upgrade .
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/tacctl\-console.conf
sshd's drop-in for the members of
.B tac\-console
(the accounts whose shell is the console): the console forced, every
forwarding closed, key logins off. Written by
.B host sync
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
and
.BR "console install" ,
checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
refreshed by
.BR upgrade ,
removed by
.BR "console remove" ,
.B host unenroll
of that host and
.BR uninstall .
Do not edit.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00\-tacctl\-engineer.conf
sshd's drop-in for the members of
.B tac\-engineer
(the engineer tier): every forwarding and tunnel closed whatever their shell
(agent forwarding follows
.BR "console agent\-forwarding" ).
The
.B 00\-
prefix makes sshd read it before
.IR tacctl\-console.conf ,
so an account in the engineer group and in a group the console lets forward (a
stale
.BR tac\-superuser )
stays locked down. Written at every
.B host sync
or
.B "host enroll \-\-local"
of this server and by
.BR "console install" ,
whether or not the console is installed; checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
refreshed by
.BR upgrade ,
left in place by
.B "console remove"
and removed by
.BR uninstall .
Do not edit.
.TP
.I /etc/shells
Gets the line
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
with the drop-in, and loses it with it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/tier\-pinned
Marker that
.B "tacctl upgrade"
recorded the tier of every group at priv-lvl 15, once (a fresh install writes
it at once). It is outside
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
so a setting lost later is not recorded again by a routine upgrade.
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/operator\-policy\-noted
Marker that
.B "tacctl upgrade"
printed, once, on its first run onto this release, the privilege lines a device
configured earlier keeps (a fresh
install writes it at once). Delete it to be told again.
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/wti\-plain\-acl\-noted
Marker that
.B "tacctl upgrade"
named, once, on its first run onto this release, the scopes whose plain
management ACL entries permit every protocol
and port on WTI units (a fresh install writes it at once). Delete it to be told
again.
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts
Generated from
.I devices.yaml
on every registry write (and after
.BR "backup restore" ):
one
.I "name type key"
line per pinned host key, for ssh's
.B HostKeyAlias
lookup. Mode 0644, root-owned, in a directory 0755 under
.I /var/lib/tacctl
(0711: users pass through it, only root lists it), so every user's ssh can
read it. Do not edit it.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
The checksum of every file tacctl rendered, from which
.B status
and
.B config validate
report a hand edit as drift.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/.store.lock
.TQ
.I /etc/tacctl/.store\-apply.lock
Lock files, mode 0600, created when first needed and left in place. The first
serialises writes of the store; the second is the apply lock, taken by every
command that changes the store or
.I tacctl.yaml
and held through its restarts (see
.BR "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION" ).
Do not delete them while a command runs.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/<file>.overwritten\-<timestamp>
The version of
.IR store.yaml " or " tacctl.yaml
that a command found edited by hand while it ran and replaced when it put its
own copy back. Mode 0600; kept until removed.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/
Automatic snapshots of the canonical files, one directory per snapshot, last
30 retained, root-only;
.I legacy/
holds old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
backups, the pre-store config and files a render or
the state migration displaced.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/backups\-kept/
The snapshots
.B "backup keep"
copied out of
.IR backups/ ,
one directory per snapshot (mode 0700, files 0600, a file
.I kept
with the note, who set it and when). No retention reaches it; it is removed only by
.BR "backup unkeep" ,
by hand, or with the state directory by
.BR uninstall .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
Host-specific overrides for the device config templates
.RI ( cisco ,
.IR cisco-legacy ,
.IR juniper ,
.IR wti ,
and for
.BR "--protocol radius"
.IR cisco-radius ,
.IR juniper-radius " and " wti-radius ).
A file named
.I <name>.template
here is used in place of the one shipped in
.IR config/templates/ .
Install puts a copy of each shipped template here, and
.B "tacctl upgrade"
replaces one with the new release's only while it is as tacctl wrote it:
.I .shipped.sha256
records what tacctl wrote (in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format). A changed template is kept, the new release's version is written
beside it as
.IR <name>.template.new ,
which is never used for rendering, and the upgrade warns, naming both. A
template with no record counts as unchanged when it is byte for byte a version
tacctl shipped, according to the git history of
.IR /opt/tacctl .
.TP
.IR /etc/tacctl/linux\-hosts ", " /etc/tacctl/linux\-uids
The registry of enrolled Linux hosts (with each host's method) and the
UID each user gets on every host. Snapshots include both, and
.B backup restore
puts them back.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/.linux\-uids.lock
The lock of the UID ledger: mode 0600, created when first needed and left in
place. Held, for the milliseconds of a read and write and never across a run on
a host, by the commands that write
.I linux\-uids
or decide from it. Not snapshotted.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/hosts/
One
.IR name .json
per enrolled host: what the last
.B host enroll
or
.B host sync
did (when, by whom, how it ended, the accounts it changed), the facts it read
of the host (operating system, sshd, PAM modules, the UID range of
.IR /etc/login.defs ),
and the last
.BR "host provisioner rotate" .
Printed by
.BR "host show" .
Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root only; renamed with the host and removed by
.BR "host unenroll" .
Snapshots include them.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/linux/
The pam_tacplus source tarball and the modules built in containers.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json
The seen cache of
.BR "device scan" :
what the logs showed of each address, where each backend's next scan starts,
and the last host-key re-scan of each entry. Mode 0600, root only; derived
from the logs, not snapshotted, rebuilt by
.BR "device scan \-\-full" ;
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json
The record of each device's last configuration pull
.RB ( "device config pull" ):
when, by whom, over which transport, what the NETCONF probe found, the result
and the state of each managed section, keyed by the device's lower\-case name. Mode 0600, root only; derived from the devices, not
snapshotted, rebuilt by a pull. A rename carries a record along, a removal and
.B "device config forget"
delete it.
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
leaves it (as it leaves
.IR device\-config/ );
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/
One file per device,
.IR <name>.yaml :
the managed sections of its last successful pull, one literal block per
section, in the form they are compared in: a secret's value is not in it.
Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root only; derived, not snapshotted;
.B uninstall
removes the directory.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/device\-apply/
What
.B "device config apply"
keeps:
.I shapes.json
(0600: each shape's statements with every secret elided and who confirmed it)
and
.I locks/<name>.lock
(one lock file per device, so that one device is applied by one run at a
time). Root only; not snapshotted; no raw device output and no password is in
it.
.TP
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl ", " /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-tiers
Optional sudoers rules. Installed only via
.B "tacctl config sudoers install"
and
.BR "tacctl config sudoers tiers install" ;
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
rewrites a file that is exactly this release's text with 0.2.3's.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
The tacctl binary, built on the host from the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
by
.B "tacctl install"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.TP
.I /opt/tacctl/
Clone of the tacctl repository: the Go sources and vendored dependencies
tacctl is built from, the shipped templates, units, patches and this manual.
.B "tacctl upgrade"
pulls it.
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
is the bootstrap: it installs the Go toolchain if needed, builds the binary
when it is missing or out of date, and runs it.
.TP
.I /etc/bash_completion.d/tacctl
The bash completion script, generated by
.BR "tacctl completion bash" .
.TP
.I /usr/local/go/
The Go toolchain
.B install
fetches (the version the release pins) to build tacquito;
.I /usr/local/go/bin/go
is the compiler tacctl runs.
.TP
.I /etc/login.defs
Read, never written, by
.B "host enroll \-\-local"
for the UID range of this server's own accounts.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub
The public host keys of a host, read over the login in use by
.BR "host enroll" ,
.B "host show \-\-check"
and
.B "host provisioner rotate"
to pin them and compare them with the registry.
.TP
.I /root/
Where
.B uninstall
leaves what it is asked to keep:
.IR tacquito\-backups\-<time>.tar.gz ,
.I tacquito\-logs\-<time>.tar.gz
(the accounting logs) and, for the RADIUS backend,
.IR tacctl\-radius\-logs\-<time>.tar.gz .
.TP
.I /tmp/
The default of
.B TMPDIR
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ):
where tacctl makes the private files and directories it stages while it works
and removes them afterwards.
.TP
.I /var/tmp/
Where
.B "scope block ... edit"
and
.B "device block ... edit"
make the private directory of the editor (mode 0700, handed to the invoking
user, removed afterwards on every path). It is not
.BR TMPDIR .
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/tacctl.1.gz
This manual. Written by
.B install
and
.BR upgrade ;
.B tacctl man
shows the copy the binary carries (the page of the running version), and
.B tacctl man \-\-path
says whether this file is the same.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
The
.B tacctl shell
history of the invoking user: mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines, redacted (the value of
.BR \-\-secret ,
.BR \-\-hash ,
.B \-\-password
and
.BR \-\-email ,
.BR \-\-note ,
what follows
.B set
in
.BR "secret ... set" ,
the address of
.BR "user email" ,
and the rest of a
.B user
line from its first word with an @).
.SS TACACS+ (tacquito)
.TP
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
TACACS+ server configuration, rendered by tacctl from the store. Mode 0640,
owned by
.BR tacquito : tacquito .
The old paths of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups
and
.I templates
in
.I /etc/tacquito
lead into
.IR /etc/tacctl .
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service
Systemd unit file (the default listener).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito@.service
Template unit, one instance per further listener.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service.d/tacctl.conf
Systemd drop-in holding the listen address, log level, metrics address
and accounting log of the unit. Rendered by tacctl from
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "tacctl server listen" ,
.BR "tacctl config loglevel" ,
.BR "tacctl server metrics" );
edits are overwritten. Instances have
.IR tacquito@<name>.service.d/tacctl.conf .
.TP
.I /var/log/tacquito/accounting.log
Per-session accounting records
.RI ( accounting\-<name>.log
per further listener). Rotated daily, retained 90 days
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacquito ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito
The tacquito server binary.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito-hashgen
Standalone bcrypt hash generator shipped with tacquito.
.TP
.I /opt/tacquito-src/
Clone of the tacquito server source code, used to rebuild the binary
during
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.SS RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.I <raddb>
is
.I /etc/freeradius/3.0
(Debian/Ubuntu) or
.I /etc/raddb
(RHEL family);
.I <logs>
is
.I /var/log/freeradius
or
.IR /var/log/radius .
No file of the FreeRADIUS package is edited.
.TP
.IR <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.conf ", " <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.users
tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration (listeners, clients, policy) and its users
file (bcrypt hashes), rendered from the store; mode 0640, root and the
daemon's group.
.TP
.I <raddb>/tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
The dictionary the daemon is started with: the package's, then the WTI
vendor and tacctl's internal attributes.
.TP
.IR /usr/sbin/freeradius ", " /usr/lib/freeradius/ ", " /usr/share/freeradius/dictionary ", " /run/freeradius/freeradius.pid
What the package provides and the configuration names: the daemon, its module
directory
.RI ( libdir ),
the main dictionary that
.I <raddb>/tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
includes, and the pid file
.RI ( pidfile ).
On the RHEL family they are
.IR /usr/sbin/radiusd ,
.IR /usr/lib64/freeradius/ ,
the same dictionary and
.IR /run/radiusd/radiusd.pid .
Read, not edited.
.TP
.IR /etc/systemd/system/freeradius.service.d/tacctl.conf " (" radiusd.service.d " on RHEL)"
Drop-in that starts the distribution's unit as tacctl's instance; present
exactly while the backend is enabled.
.TP
.IR <logs>/tacctl\-auth.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-accounting.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-radius.log
One line per Access-Accept or Access-Reject; accounting detail records; the
daemon's log
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacctl\-radius ).
.SS Linux hosts
.TP
.IR /etc/pam.d/tacctl\-* ", " /etc/tacctl\-pam_radius.conf ", " /var/lib/tacctl\-client/
On an enrolled host: the PAM files tacctl writes (root-only; for tacplus
they hold the shared secret), the pam_radius_auth server file (0600), and
the client's state.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install on a fresh host:
.B tacctl install
.TP
Add a superuser:
.B tacctl user add alice superuser
.TP
Restrict device subnets in the default scope:
.B tacctl scope prefixes lab add 10.1.0.0/24,172.16.5.0/24
.TP
Add a broad prod scope and a narrower lab scope (the /24 wins for 10.10.99.x):
.nf
.B "  tacctl scope add prod_dc_east --prefixes 10.10.0.0/16 --secret generate"
.B "  tacctl scope add lab_east     --prefixes 10.10.99.0/24 --secret generate"
.fi
.TP
Trace which scope a client IP falls into:
.B tacctl scope lookup 10.10.99.7
.TP
Grant a user full production access across two datacenters:
.B tacctl user scope alice add prod_dc_east,prod_dc_west
.TP
Generate a Cisco-ready configuration for the prod scope:
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east
.TP
Generate legacy IOS 12.x syntax for an older device:
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --legacy
.TP
Print the serial-menu walkthrough for a WTI console server in the lab scope:
.B tacctl config wti --scope lab_east
.TP
Serve the same users over RADIUS, with Cisco and Juniper privilege attributes for prod:
.nf
.B "  tacctl backend enable radius"
.B "  tacctl scope vendor-attrs prod_dc_east enable cisco,juniper"
.B "  tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --protocol radius"
.fi
.TP
Tag one address of a scope as a WTI unit (it gets WTI-Super and nothing else):
.B tacctl scope devices lab_east set 10.10.99.5 wti
.TP
Enroll a Linux host with pam_radius_auth, then switch it to pam_tacplus:
.nf
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method radius"
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method tacplus"
.fi
.TP
Bind the listeners to a specific management IP:
.nf
.B "  tacctl server listen tcp 10.1.0.1:49"
.B "  tacctl server listen --backend radius --listener auth udp 10.1.0.1:1812"
.fi
.TP
Check, without writing anything, whether a tacquito.yaml can move into the store:
.B tacctl store import --check
.TP
Expose the Prometheus exporter to an external scraper (default is loopback-only):
.B tacctl server metrics address 10.1.0.1:8080
.TP
Enable passwordless invocation for members of the adm group:
.B tacctl config sudoers install adm
.TP
Print client-side bcrypt generation instructions to share with a user:
.B tacctl hash commands
.SH NOTES
.B IPv4 only by default.
The TACACS+ listener runs with
.B -network tcp
and the RADIUS ones with
.BR udp ,
which bind IPv4 only. Switching to
.B tcp6
or
.B udp6
enables a dual-stack socket on most platforms; IPv4 clients then
connect with IPv4-mapped addresses
.RB ( ::ffff:10.1.0.1 )
that do not match IPv4 prefix ACLs configured via
.BR "tacctl scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
or
.BR "config deny" .
Add equivalent IPv6 prefix rules before enabling
.B tcp6.
.PP
.B Juniper template users are required.
Every
.B local-user-name
referenced by a group class (for example
.BR RO-CLASS ,
.BR OP-CLASS ,
.BR RW-CLASS )
must have a matching local user on each Juniper device. Without it,
authentication succeeds but Junos rejects the login silently.
Run
.B tacctl config juniper
to regenerate the device-side configuration after adding or editing
groups.
.PP
.B Built on the host.
tacctl is a single Go binary, built on the server from the sources in the
clone
.I /opt/tacctl
(its dependencies are vendored there: no network or module proxy is used for
the build) with the Go toolchain in
.IR /usr/local/go ,
which
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
installs when it is missing, after checking the published SHA-256 of the
download. The first build fills about 130 MB of Go build cache; later builds
reuse the build cache of root
.RI ( /root/.cache/go-build ).
.B "tacctl version \-\-long"
prints the commit and build date of the installed binary, which equals the
commit of the clone after every
.BR "tacctl install" " or " "tacctl upgrade" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR visudo (8),
.BR radiusd (8),
.BR pam_radius_auth (8)
.SH PROJECT
https://github.com/rett/tacctl
